window.statoblastDocsSearch = [{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["overview","lifecycle","market","components"],"path":"explanation/system-overview.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"A five-minute introduction to Zoltar, Statoblast, Trading, and OpenOracle.","text":"Start here Zoltar and Statoblast are Ethereum protocols for prediction markets, designed to make outcome manipulation costly. This page assumes you know what Ethereum and an ERC-20 token are, and nothing else. It states the problem, defines the vocabulary you will meet on every other page, introduces the four components, and follows one market from start to finish.","title":"System overview","topic":"Introduction","weight":1},{"fragment":"problem","heading":"The problem","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/system-overview.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"A five-minute introduction to Zoltar, Statoblast, Trading, and OpenOracle.","text":"A prediction market lets people trade on a question such as “Will the fictional Aurora spacecraft launch before 31 December 2030?” Its shares are worth something only after someone decides what actually happened. That decision is the weak point: whoever controls it controls the money. Zoltar and Statoblast make the decision expensive to corrupt in two ways. First, anyone who reports an outcome must stake REP, the protocol's reputation token, behind it, and the contracts settle in favour of the outcome with the most REP staked. Reporters whose outcome loses forfeit their stake, so backing an answer the rest of the market will not support is costly. Whether that pressure keeps reports honest depends on the participation assumptions in the security model. Second, when reporters cannot agree, the system does not pick a side. Its ledger, called a universe, splits into child universes, one per possible answer, and the market decides which child stays valuable. A dishonest majority of REP cannot stop honest participants from moving their REP into the child that records the honest answer; what that does and does not guarantee is covered with Zoltar.","title":"System overview","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"vocabulary","heading":"Four words you will see everywhere","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/system-overview.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"A five-minute introduction to Zoltar, Statoblast, Trading, and OpenOracle.","text":"REP The Reputation token . Holding REP lets you report outcomes and underwrite markets. It is also what you risk: a reporter loses REP when the outcome they supported loses. Universe A ledger that can branch . Markets and REP balances belong to a particular universe. Questions are registered globally, so the same question can have markets in multiple universes. When reporters split irreconcilably, the universe forks into children, one per possible answer, each with its own REP. Portfolio A shared capital and settlement system that backs admitted questions. Each owner keeps an individual REP account and commitment; each question keeps separate outcome shares and accounting. A portfolio can hold one fixed question or many. SecurityPool The question contract inside a portfolio. It exposes outcome shares, reporting and redemption; portfolio modules hold REP and ETH.","title":"System overview","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"components","heading":"Four components","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/system-overview.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"A five-minute introduction to Zoltar, Statoblast, Trading, and OpenOracle.","text":"Zoltar The ledger. It records questions, universes, REP, and forks. It never judges which answer is true. Statoblast core The market. It deploys a SecurityPool for a question, universe, and pool configuration, mints and redeems shares, settles ordinary disputes locally, and moves markets into child universes after a fork. Trading An exchange for Statoblast shares. It is one of possibly many; it neither creates nor resolves markets. OpenOracle A price feed. It supplies a contestable REP/ETH exchange rate whenever the protocol must check that REP still covers ETH obligations. It has no say in market outcomes.","title":"System overview","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"lifecycle","heading":"One market, start to finish","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/system-overview.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"A five-minute introduction to Zoltar, Statoblast, Trading, and OpenOracle.","text":"Someone registers the Aurora question in Zoltar and admits it to a portfolio under that portfolio’s admission policy. Owners deposit REP and authorize shared commitments. Traders purchase complete sets through the portfolio: one Yes, one No and one Invalid share at the question’s current backing rate. ETH goes to central settlement custody, with a separate balance for the question. Traders can exchange those shares; reporting begins after the question ends. Register question Admit question to portfolio Deposit portfolio REP Mint complete sets Trade shares Question ends First REP deposit starts the Escalation Game Local resolution or Zoltar fork Choose branches and materialize child claims Repair missing collateral through a Collateral Repair Auction Redeem Market lifecycle. The ordinary path resolves locally and skips the migration and repair steps. The fork path creates one child pool per answer and uses shared repair accounting and a branch auction for each portfolio child. Reporters stake REP behind an answer in the pool's Escalation Game , a dispute round whose deadline moves later as the contested amount grows. When the deadline finally passes, the market resolves if exactly one answer holds more REP than every other, and winning shares redeem the question’s backing from central settlement custody. Usually that is the end of the story. If two answers reach the non-decision threshold , the universe can split into Invalid, Yes and No children. Owners record their branch choices before the portfolio’s cutoff. Shared branch accounting migrates their capital without requiring a separate owner claim for every question. Shareholders can materialize claims in multiple children, but ETH is not duplicated. Each portfolio branch repairs its shortfall through a shared Collateral Repair Auction ; winning bidders become capital owners. Forks and migration explains the process, and what a fork does and does not promise describes its limits. Follow a market through Statoblast · Understand branching universes · Look up a term","title":"System overview","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["contracts","architecture","calls","assets"],"path":"explanation/contract-architecture.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Which Zoltar and Statoblast contracts call each other, deploy each other, and move assets.","text":"Start here Users call SecurityPools , the Statoblast contracts that expose each market's shares and reporting. Each question belongs to a portfolio , which holds cash in its central settlement engine. Pools rely on Zoltar for universes and REP , on a price coordinator for REP/ETH prices, and on the SecurityPoolForker after a fork. Related reading: the generated transaction reference for exact caller and prerequisite rules, How Statoblast works for lifecycle context, and the invariant catalog for cross-contract safety properties.","title":"Contract architecture","topic":"Architecture","weight":1},{"fragment":"product-layers","heading":"Product layers","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/contract-architecture.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Which Zoltar and Statoblast contracts call each other, deploy each other, and move assets.","text":"Zoltar — questions, universes, REP, forks Statoblast core — pools, collateral, shares, resolution, migration Trading — one implementation of swaps and liquidity around shares OpenOracle — contestable REP/ETH prices augurScan and bots — indexing, evidence, and permissionless operations Product-layer map. These products share protocol data but retain separate responsibilities and deployment boundaries.","title":"Contract architecture","topic":"Architecture","weight":0},{"fragment":"conceptual-model","heading":"What each contract owns","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/contract-architecture.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Which Zoltar and Statoblast contracts call each other, deploy each other, and move assets.","text":"ZoltarQuestionData stores questions; Zoltar manages universes, forks and universe-specific REP. SecurityPoolPortfolio registers admitted questions and coordinates shared operations. A fixed single-question portfolio uses this same architecture. The capital ledger records each owner's REP principal, commitment, health target and earned income. Its commitment index supports bounded health and retirement operations. The settlement engine and book track question demand, ETH, fee streams, minimum backing floors and recovery assets. A SecurityPool is a question adapter. Its ShareToken records outcome balances across that question's universe lineage; its EscalationGame resolves reports. Reporter REP has separate custody. The shared OpenOracle feed accepts independently funded price candidates. Question price coordinators adapt that shared evidence. The portfolio fork coordinator records branch choices and prepares shared branch accounting. The uniform auction sells freshly unchosen provider REP for ETH when a branch needs repair.","title":"Contract architecture","topic":"Architecture","weight":0},{"fragment":"overview","heading":"Purchase and resolution","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/contract-architecture.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Which Zoltar and Statoblast contracts call each other, deploy each other, and move assets.","text":"A trader purchases complete sets through the portfolio. Admission, fresh price, healthy capacity, fees and the question's backing floor are checked atomically. The question adapter mints distinct outcome shares. After final resolution, winning claims redeem against their question's settlement accounting. Returning complete sets earlier reduces demand and can free commitment for retirement.","title":"Contract architecture","topic":"Architecture","weight":0},{"fragment":"edges","heading":"Fork boundaries","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/contract-architecture.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Which Zoltar and Statoblast contracts call each other, deploy each other, and move assets.","text":"Owners choose once per portfolio and universe. Shared roots and branch balances avoid a migration transaction per owner per question. A question adapter and reporter continuation are materialized only when used. Token holders still migrate their own claims through each intervening universe. Reporter checkpoint preparation is bounded per transaction and reusable by descendants. See the fork lifecycle , uniform auctions , and the contract inventory for module interfaces and limits.","title":"Contract architecture","topic":"Architecture","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["glossary","terms","REP","universe","underwriting commitment","haircut","trading"],"path":"reference/glossary.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Canonical definitions for protocol assets, states, and mechanisms.","text":"Reference Canonical, plain-language definitions for terms used throughout the documentation.","title":"Protocol glossary","topic":"Introduction","weight":1},{"fragment":"universes-and-rep","heading":"Universes and REP","keywords":[],"path":"reference/glossary.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Canonical definitions for protocol assets, states, and mechanisms.","text":"Universe A Zoltar ledger and identity for universe-specific REP and application state. Universe 0 is the initialized root; later universes are descendants created through forks. Child universe A deterministic descendant that represents one valid answer to its parent's fork question. In a binary-market example, a Statoblast fork on its own question creates Invalid, Yes, and No children. If an unrelated fork interrupts that market, its children instead follow the unrelated categorical or scalar fork question's valid answers. Question A global Zoltar object that identifies a proposition, its end time, and its answer options. Outcome One possible answer to a question. Statoblast binary markets use Invalid, Yes, and No. Invalid The legitimate third outcome for ambiguous, malformed, or otherwise unanswerable market wording. REP Universe-specific reputation tokens used as reporting, dispute, and security capital. Theoretical REP supply Zoltar's per-universe REP supply figure, from which it derives the fork and non-decision thresholds. Burns reduce it, and each child universe starts from its parent's supply after the fork threshold burn plus the fork initiator's migration balance. Fork threshold The amount of parent-universe REP that a fork initiator supplies and burns to begin a fork, derived by getForkThresholdAttoRep from that universe's theoretical REP supply. See migration accounting . Fork haircut The part of the fork threshold that Zoltar burns without crediting to the initiator's migration balance: ⌊fork threshold ÷ forkBurnDivisor ⌋, at most 20%. Contract comments call it the burn haircut. REP added to a migration balance after the fork starts is credited in full. Migration balance A parent-universe credit ( migrationRepBalanceAttoRep ) created when REP is burned or committed for a fork. The same credited amount can be minted independently in each valid child, while per-child accounting prevents an account from consuming more than that amount in any one child. Child REP REP minted for one child universe. Its ledger and economic value are separate from parent and sibling REP.","title":"Protocol glossary","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"security-pools","heading":"SecurityPools, shares, and vaults","keywords":[],"path":"reference/glossary.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Canonical definitions for protocol assets, states, and mechanisms.","text":"Portfolio A shared REP capital ledger and ETH settlement system backing questions admitted by its configured policy. Owners retain individual commitments and health settings; questions have separate outcome shares and settlement balances. A portfolio with one fixed question uses the same architecture as a portfolio admitting many questions. SecurityPool The portfolio-bound contract for one question in one universe. It exposes shares, local reporting and redemption; its portfolio modules hold capital and settlement cash. Lineage An origin SecurityPool and every child pool forked from it. Pools in one lineage share an origin ID and one outcome-share token contract. Origin pool A question pool first registered in a portfolio, before any child pool is prepared for its lineage. Settlement collateral The question’s backing in the portfolio settlement book. Complete sets and winning shares redeem their proportional value; holding fees reduce backing only above the configured floor. Open interest The exposure created by outstanding question claims. Portfolio settlement demand aggregates the obligations that healthy underwriting commitments must support. Complete set One matching unit each of Invalid, Yes, and No shares, minted against ETH and redeemable together while the pool is operational. Outcome share An ERC-1155 token representing one outcome claim in one universe-specific SecurityPool. Vault An owner’s capital account: its own REP principal, commitment and fee claims in the shared capital ledger. An auction winner may control a separate vault contract that owns such an account. Underwriting commitment An owner’s authorized amount of ETH obligations. Healthy commitments contribute to shared portfolio capacity and earn fees; losses from a distressed position stay with that owner. Security multiplier The portfolio’s fixed protocol backing multiplier, expressed in basis points. Owners can select an additional personal health target; the two settings have different purposes. Vault health Whether an owner’s total free and pledged principal covers its commitment at the protocol backing requirement and conservative REP price. See liquidations . Health target An owner-selected target of at least 100% relative to the protocol backing requirement. It constrains commitment changes and surplus withdrawals; falling below that personal buffer alone does not trigger liquidation. Minting capacity The healthy portfolio commitment available to support additional settlement demand. Purchases also require a current funded price and an eligible question. Utilization The fraction of shared capacity already supporting settlement demand. The portfolio’s configured annual holding-fee rate does not vary with utilization. Fee reserve Fees retained for underwriters rather than for outcome-share redemption. Accumulators credit eligible owners without visiting every question when an owner claims. Holding fee The configured linear charge on a question’s fee principal while its stream is eligible to run. It reduces share backing over time but stops before crossing the minimum backing floor. See fees .","title":"Protocol glossary","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"escalation","heading":"Escalation Game","keywords":[],"path":"reference/glossary.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Canonical definitions for protocol assets, states, and mechanisms.","text":"Escalation Game The local Statoblast dispute mechanism in which REP-backed balances accumulate behind Invalid, Yes, and No. Start bond The minimum Escalation Game deposit: the larger of 1 REP and the universe's theoretical REP supply when the pool was deployed ÷ 10 7 . Origin-pool deployment requires it to be below the non-decision threshold; a game started with a bond at or above that threshold is clamped to one atto-REP below that threshold. A smaller deposit is accepted only when it fills an outcome exactly to that threshold. A child pool continuing a forked game keeps its parent's start bond. Binding capital The median of the three outcome balances that determines the Escalation Game's scheduled deadline. Required support The REP an outcome must hold to stay in contention, called attrition cost in the contracts ( totalCostAttoRep ). It is zero for the three-day activation delay, then rises from the start bond to the non-decision threshold over 49 days. The game's deadline is the moment it passes binding capital. Non-decision threshold The balance that two outcomes must each reach for the Escalation Game to enter non-decision: half of the universe's fork threshold, rounded up, read when the game opens and fixed for that game and any game carried from it. Non-decision The Escalation Game state in which two outcomes have each reached the non-decision threshold ( NonDecisionState.Local ). The game picks no winner, and anyone can fork the universe on the pool's own question. Reward-eligible cap One and a half times binding capital. Only the portion of winning Escalation Game deposits up to this cap earns a bonus; deposits beyond it return principal only. See the EscalationGame reference . Escalation haircut Two fifths of binding capital, burned at Escalation Game settlement instead of being paid to winners as a bonus, unless the pool's own fork already paid it through the fork haircut. The contracts call it the winner haircut ( burnEscalationWinnerHaircut ).","title":"Protocol glossary","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"forks-and-repair","heading":"Forks and repair","keywords":[],"path":"reference/glossary.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Canonical definitions for protocol assets, states, and mechanisms.","text":"SecurityPoolForker The shared contract that prepares question children and their inherited reporting state after portfolio branch migration. Migration window The period in which parent REP can migrate to a child universe. Portfolio owners must record their branch choice before an earlier choice cutoff; shared branch processing then uses that recorded allocation. Carried game A child question’s continuation of an unresolved parent Escalation Game. Frozen history authenticates inherited claims; new wallet REP deposits use the continuation’s current rules. Fork continuation The preparation needed before a child question resumes an inherited unresolved reporting game. Purchases wait until the question and shared branch are ready. Collateral Repair Auction A uniform-price auction of a portfolio branch’s saleable REP for ETH to repair branch claims. Winning bids automatically own separately accounted capital positions. It does not select truth.","title":"Protocol glossary","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"liquidation","heading":"Liquidation","keywords":[],"path":"reference/glossary.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Canonical definitions for protocol assets, states, and mechanisms.","text":"Target vault The unhealthy vault whose underwriting commitment a liquidation reduces. Receiver vault The vault that accepts the transferred commitment and the REP award. It must stay healthy on its whole resulting commitment. Operator An account submitting permissionless maintenance transactions, such as a funded takeover of distressed commitment. A takeover receiver must authorize its own capital and satisfy the transaction bounds.","title":"Protocol glossary","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"prices","heading":"Prices","keywords":[],"path":"reference/glossary.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Canonical definitions for protocol assets, states, and mechanisms.","text":"OpenOracle An economically contestable price-reporting contract used by Statoblast for fresh REP-per-ETH prices (written REP/ETH elsewhere in these pages), not market outcomes. Price coordinator A question-facing adapter to the portfolio’s shared REP/ETH feed. It exposes current prices and requests funded OpenOracle reports; it does not queue vault operations.","title":"Protocol glossary","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"trading","heading":"Trading","keywords":[],"path":"reference/glossary.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Canonical definitions for protocol assets, states, and mechanisms.","text":"Trading pool The constant-product pair ( TwoWayConstantProductPair , called the pair in the Trading reference) that trades one SecurityPool's Yes and No shares against each other. The Trading factory creates at most one per SecurityPool. Trading router The TwoWayConstantProductRouter contract that turns ETH into a position and a position back into ETH by combining SecurityPool complete-set calls with trading-pool swaps. Long shares The Yes or No shares a trader holds on the side they expect to win. Entering through the trading router also leaves the trader one Invalid share for each complete set it minted, fewer than the long shares received. Insured exit A trading-router exit that swaps some long shares for the opposite outcome, then redeems them with the trader's Invalid shares as complete sets for ETH.","title":"Protocol glossary","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["ABI","transactions","callers","events"],"path":"reference/contracts.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Index of the per-contract interaction reference.","text":"Open a contract to see its read surface, any security assumptions or trust boundary, and each state-changing entrypoint with its caller, prerequisites, effects, and primary signals. The entrypoint count is the number of state-changing function signatures a contract answers, counting each overload, each selector its fallback() routes to a delegate, and receive() .","title":"Contract interactions","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"table-columns","heading":"Table columns","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Index of the per-contract interaction reference.","text":"Every contract page uses the same five columns. Transaction : the function name with its parameter names in ABI order. Overloads appear as separate signatures. Caller : the addresses the contract accepts as msg.sender . Main prerequisites : the conditions the call requires, quoting a revert string where a caller needs it to tell failures apart. State or asset effect : what a successful call changes. Primary signals : the events a successful call emits, each with the condition under which it is emitted, including events emitted by contracts the call reaches. A row says so when a call can succeed without emitting an event. Contract Purpose State-changing entrypoints ZoltarQuestionData Creates immutable, content-addressed scalar or categorical questions and exposes their display metadata. 1 Zoltar Registers universe forks, charges the fork haircut , and mints branch-specific child REP . 6 ReputationToken Implements universe-specific ERC-20 REP, ERC-2612 permits, and ERC-3009 transfers while enforcing the supply ceiling maintained by Zoltar. 10 SecurityPoolFactory Deploys and registers portfolio question ledgers and their shared price feeds. 7 SecurityPool Question ledger for one shared portfolio, question and universe. 22 SecurityPoolForker Routes shared-portfolio question forks and lazy child question preparation. Child-game trust boundary 5 EscalationGame Escrows outcome REP, raises the running resolution cost, detects non-decision , and settles local or carried deposits. 21 OpenOraclePriceCoordinator Per-question adapter to the portfolio universe’s shared REP/ETH feed. 5 ShareToken Stores universe-aware ERC-1155 outcome shares and materializes a holder's persistent source entitlement in selected fork branches. 12","title":"Contract interactions","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["questions","categorical","scalar","encoding"],"path":"reference/contracts/zoltarquestiondata.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the createQuestion entrypoint.","text":"Creates immutable, content-addressed scalar or categorical questions and exposes their display metadata. ZoltarQuestionData source contract","title":"ZoltarQuestionData","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/zoltarquestiondata.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the createQuestion entrypoint.","text":"Use getQuestionId before submission; questionCreatedTimestamp and questions for direct lookup; getQuestionCount and getQuestions for indexed or paged discovery; and getQuestionEndDate , getOutcomeLabels , splitUint256IntoTwoWithInvalid , hasNonZeroScalarReservedBits , isMalformedAnswerOption , and getAnswerOptionName when validating or displaying answers. In the QuestionData tuple, startTime and endTime are uint48 , while numTicks is uint120 ; clients must use these exact widths because they determine the getQuestionId and createQuestion selectors.","title":"ZoltarQuestionData","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/zoltarquestiondata.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the createQuestion entrypoint.","text":"Each call lists its parameter names in ABI order. The contract index defines the columns. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals createQuestion(questionData, outcomeOptions) Anyone Question ID not already created; end time is on or after start time. Scalar questions use no labels, require display maximum greater than minimum, and positive ticks. Categorical questions require nonempty labels whose keccak256(abi.encode(label)) values are strictly descending. Stores the question at its deterministic content hash, records the creation timestamp, appends it to discovery order, and stores categorical labels when supplied. QuestionCreated","title":"ZoltarQuestionData","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["fork","universe","migration","child REP"],"path":"reference/contracts/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the forkUniverse, burnRep, deployChild, and migration-balance entrypoints.","text":"Registers universe forks, charges the fork haircut , and mints branch-specific child REP . Zoltar source contract","title":"Zoltar","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the forkUniverse, burnRep, deployChild, and migration-balance entrypoints.","text":"Use universes , forkThresholdDivisor , forkBurnDivisor , zoltarQuestionData , genesisReputationToken , childReputationTokenCount , getForkTime , getForkChoiceDeadline , getForkRecapitalizationDeadline , getEarliestForkTime , forkQuestionMatches , getRepToken , getForkThresholdAttoRep , getNonDecisionThresholdAttoRep , getUniverseTheoreticalSupplyAttoRep , getChildUniverseId , getDeployedChildUniverses , getMigrationRepBalanceAttoRep , and getChildMigrationRepAmountsAttoRep to reconstruct universe and migration state. The choice and recap deadlines are the native fork time plus eight and nine weeks. A child may next fork at its parent fork time plus ten weeks; origin universes have no parent cooldown. Late preparation never moves these timestamps. The child migration getter accepts an array of child universe IDs and returns cumulative amounts in the same order, with zero for unused IDs. The fork threshold is the live universe theoretical supply divided by forkThresholdDivisor , rounded up, so every nonzero-supply universe has a positive fork cost. Construction requires a deployed genesis REP token with the REPv2 getTotalTheoreticalSupply() selector, theoretical supply from one attoREP through 11 million REP, forkThresholdDivisor > 1 , and forkBurnDivisor >= 5 , which caps the uncredited fork haircut at 20% of the threshold. The constructor emits UniverseInitialized once, for genesis universe 0 ; deployChild does not emit it, so child universes are discovered through DeployChild and ChildReputationTokenInitialized . Genesis REP uses ordinary ERC-20 approvals because the configured mainnet REPv2 token does not implement ERC-2612 or ERC-3009.","title":"Zoltar","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"security-assumptions","heading":"Security assumptions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the forkUniverse, burnRep, deployChild, and migration-balance entrypoints.","text":"These calls depend on A15 intended question selection and A25 safe immutable parameters .","title":"Zoltar","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the forkUniverse, burnRep, deployChild, and migration-balance entrypoints.","text":"Each call lists its parameter names in ABI order. The contract index defines the columns. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals forkUniverse(universeId, questionId) Any address able to fund the current fork threshold Initialized and unforked universe; existing ended question; sufficient caller REP; a child must have reached its parent fork timestamp plus ten weeks. Genesis REP requires allowance; child REP is burned directly without allowance. Records the fork, removes threshold REP from the parent universe, and credits the caller with the threshold minus the uncredited fork haircut. The REP burn events (genesis REP Transfer to the burn address, or child REP Transfer and Burn ), then UniverseForked burnRep(universeId, amountAttoRep) Any REP holder; the caller can burn only its own balance Initialized universe; positive amount; sufficient caller REP and theoretical supply. Genesis REP requires allowance. Permanently removes REP without creating migration credit; escalation settlement uses this to burn the escalation haircut when its own fork has not already paid it. The REP burn events (genesis REP Transfer to the burn address, or child REP Transfer and Burn ), then RepBurned deployChild(universeId, outcomeIndex) Anyone Parent forked; outcome is well formed; child is not already deployed. Deploys the deterministic child REP token and initializes the child universe. TheoreticalSupplySet , ReputationTokenInitialized , DeployChild , and ChildReputationTokenInitialized addRepToMigrationBalance(universeId, amountAttoRep) Parent REP holder Universe forked; sufficient caller REP. Genesis REP requires allowance; child REP is burned directly without allowance. Burns or sinks additional parent REP and increases the caller's reusable migration balance. The REP burn events (genesis REP Transfer to the burn address, or child REP Transfer and Burn ), then MigrationRepAdded prepareAndSplitMigrationRep(universeId, amountAttoRep, outcomeIndexes, preparationAttoRep) Parent REP holder Forked universe; positive split amount; nonempty valid outcomes; sufficient parent REP for the exact preparationAttoRep supplied and sufficient migration credit for every split. Outcome order is unrestricted. Genesis REP requires allowance for preparation; child REP needs no allowance. Adds the supplied preparation amount to migration credit, then splits the requested REP into the supplied outcomes using the existing migration checks. Both steps revert together on failure. Callers compute any preparation shortfall from cumulative child migration amounts, rather than current child token holdings. The addRepToMigrationBalance signals when preparationAttoRep is positive; TheoreticalSupplySet , ReputationTokenInitialized , DeployChild , and ChildReputationTokenInitialized when children are deployed; child REP Transfer , Mint , and MigrationRepSplit for each destination splitMigrationRep(universeId, amountAttoRep, outcomeIndexes) Migration-balance holder Universe forked; positive split amount; nonempty list of well-formed outcomes; the cumulative amount per child must not exceed the caller's migration balance. Mints amountAttoRep of child REP into every selected branch, deploying missing children lazily. TheoreticalSupplySet , ReputationTokenInitialized , DeployChild , and ChildReputationTokenInitialized when children are deployed; child REP Transfer and Mint , then MigrationRepSplit , per selected branch","title":"Zoltar","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["REP","ERC-20","permit","supply"],"path":"reference/contracts/reputationtoken.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the ERC-20, ERC-2612 permit, and ERC-3009 authorization entrypoints.","text":"Implements universe-specific ERC-20 REP, ERC-2612 permits, and ERC-3009 transfers while enforcing the supply ceiling maintained by Zoltar. ReputationToken source contract","title":"ReputationToken","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/reputationtoken.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the ERC-20, ERC-2612 permit, and ERC-3009 authorization entrypoints.","text":"Use getTotalTheoreticalSupply , zoltar , universeId , and repNumber to identify the token and its supply ceiling; the standard ERC-20 reads name , symbol , decimals , totalSupply , balanceOf , and allowance for balances; and nonces , DOMAIN_SEPARATOR , and authorizationState to build and check ERC-2612 and ERC-3009 signatures.","title":"ReputationToken","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/reputationtoken.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the ERC-20, ERC-2612 permit, and ERC-3009 authorization entrypoints.","text":"Each call lists its parameter names in ABI order. The contract index defines the columns. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals initialize(universeId, totalTheoreticalSupplyAttoRep, repNumber) Zoltar only Called once by Zoltar as part of child-universe creation; universe, REP number, and theoretical supply are nonzero; supply does not exceed 11 million REP. Atomically assigns the child universe, global display sequence, name, symbol, and theoretical-supply ceiling used to bound migration mints. TheoreticalSupplySet and ReputationTokenInitialized mint(account, valueAttoRep) Zoltar only account is nonzero; resulting ERC-20 supply does not exceed theoretical supply. Mints branch REP to an account. Mint and ERC-20 Transfer burn(account, valueAttoRep) Zoltar only account is nonzero and has sufficient REP; theoretical supply covers the burn. Burns account REP and reduces both actual and theoretical supply by the same amount. Burn and ERC-20 Transfer transfer(to, value) REP holder Destination is nonzero; caller has sufficient balance. Moves REP from the caller without changing actual or theoretical supply. Transfer approve(spender, value) Any REP account setting its own allowance Spender is nonzero. Replaces the named spender allowance without moving REP. Approval transferFrom(from, to, value) A spender with sufficient allowance from from Source and destination are nonzero; source has sufficient balance; caller has sufficient allowance, including when caller equals source. Moves REP from from ; a finite allowance decreases by value , while an infinite allowance remains unchanged. Neither allowance path emits Approval . Transfer only permit(owner, spender, value, deadline, v, r, s) Anyone presenting the owner's valid ERC-2612 signature Deadline has not passed; signature matches the current chain, token domain, owner, spender, value, and nonce. Sets the signed allowance and advances the owner's permit nonce. Approval transferWithAuthorization(from, to, value, validAfter, validBefore, nonce, v, r, s) Anyone presenting the source's valid ERC-3009 signature The strict validity window is open; the nonce is unused; signature fields and source balance are valid. Consumes the authorization nonce and transfers the exact signed amount to the signed recipient. AuthorizationUsed and Transfer receiveWithAuthorization(from, to, value, validAfter, validBefore, nonce, v, r, s) The signed recipient presenting the source's valid ERC-3009 signature Caller equals the signed recipient; the strict validity window is open; the nonce is unused; signature fields and source balance are valid. Consumes the authorization nonce and transfers the exact signed amount to the caller-bound recipient. AuthorizationUsed and Transfer cancelAuthorization(authorizer, nonce, v, r, s) Anyone presenting the authorizer's valid ERC-3009 cancellation signature The nonce is unused and the cancellation signature matches the authorizer. Marks the authorization nonce as consumed without transferring tokens. AuthorizationCanceled","title":"ReputationToken","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["factory","deployment","registration","CREATE2"],"path":"reference/contracts/securitypoolfactory.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the origin and child pool deployment entrypoints.","text":"Deploys and registers portfolio question ledgers and their shared price feeds. No standalone deployment entry point exists. SecurityPoolFactory source contract","title":"SecurityPoolFactory","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/securitypoolfactory.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the origin and child pool deployment entrypoints.","text":"Each question derives its start bond as max(1 REP, universe theoretical REP supply / 10,000,000). The factory has no standalone vault or debt minima. Deployment requires the configured operations and accounting delegates. securityPoolDeploymentCount and strict securityPoolDeploymentsRange enumerate canonical question ledgers. portfolioCount and strict portfoliosRange enumerate every caller that configured a portfolio namespace, in registration order; a listed address is not authenticated as a portfolio. getPoolId , getSecurityPool , getSecurityPoolOriginId and getSecurityPoolHasInheritedForkOutcome identify their lineage. getPortfolioOriginId includes the caller portfolio namespace. getSecurityPoolPortfolio authenticates its portfolio; sharedPriceFeeds is indexed by portfolio and universe. getChildPoolDependencies returns a prepared coordinator and a zero per-question auction address. Branch collateral repair auctions are created by the shared fork coordinator. zoltar identifies the protocol root.","title":"SecurityPoolFactory","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/securitypoolfactory.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the origin and child pool deployment entrypoints.","text":"Each call lists its parameter names in ABI order. The contract index defines the columns. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals initializePortfolioPriceFeed(universeId, priorityFee) Anyone, within their own caller-address namespace The universe has a REP token and has not forked. A repeated call must match the first call’s REP token and priority fee. The feed starts without an accepted price. This call neither registers a question nor allocates REP, and registration does not authenticate the caller as a portfolio. The first call creates the caller namespace’s oracle configuration from the universe’s REP token and the priority fee, and appends the caller to the portfolio registry. Each call creates the namespace’s feed for that universe or returns the existing one. Portfolio setup calls this in its last stage, before any question is admitted. PortfolioRegistered on the first call from a namespace; returns the canonical feed address. No question-deployment event. initializePortfolioPriceFeedForUniverse(universeId) A portfolio, within its own caller-address namespace The caller has configured its namespace; the caller’s portfolio clock recognizes the target universe; the target REP token exists. Creates or retrieves the caller namespace’s feed for an already registered child universe, using the oracle configuration from the namespace’s first initializePortfolioPriceFeed call. No question needs to be deployed first. Returns the canonical feed address; no question-deployment event. preparePortfolioPriceFeed(source, targetUniverse) Anyone Canonical portfolio source; the portfolio clock recognizes the target; the target REP token exists. Creates or retrieves a target universe feed in the canonical source pool’s portfolio namespace without moving provider capital. Returns the canonical feed address. deployPortfolioSecurityPool(universeId, questionId, statoblastSecurityMultiplierBps, initialReportPriorityFeeAttoEthPerGas) Anyone, within their own caller-address namespace The ordinary origin deployment checks apply; no lineage for this question/configuration has been claimed in the caller namespace. The namespace is derived from msg.sender and cannot be supplied on behalf of another pool. Creates a separate origin ledger and share token in the caller namespace, attaching its coordinator to the namespace/universe shared REP/ETH feed. Portfolio admission calls this from the portfolio contract, after its immutable admission policy allows the question; it then enforces the question horizon, with failure reverting deployment. SharedPriceFeedSet , feed CoordinatorRegistered , SecurityPoolRegistered , and DeploySecurityPool preparePortfolioChildPriceFeed(parent, outcomeIndex) Anyone The parent is a canonical portfolio ledger in PoolForked state. Its namespace and oracle configuration determine the feed; Zoltar determines the selected child universe and its REP token. Prepares the child universe’s REP token and shared feed in a separate transaction before creating a portfolio child ledger. Repeated preparation returns the same feed without moving capital or selecting any owner’s migration. Returns the canonical child feed address; child REP deployment may emit Zoltar events. prepareChildPoolDependencies(parent, outcomeIndex) Anyone The parent is a canonical ledger in PoolForked state. A portfolio must first prepare its child feed. Missing child REP is deployed through Zoltar. The authenticated factory chain exclusively controls subsequent coordinator binding. Predeploys the canonical child coordinator separately from the question ledger. Repeated calls return the same coordinator; the auction result is zero because collateral repair auctions belong to shared portfolio branches. Returns coordinator and auction addresses; no DeploySecurityPool until the child ledger is deployed. deployChildSecurityPool(parent, shareToken, universeId, questionId, statoblastSecurityMultiplierBps, currentRetentionRate, settlementCollateralAttoEth) SecurityPoolForker only ( Only security pool forker ) Parent is the canonical pool for its lineage; supplied share token equals the parent share token; no pool has already been claimed for the parent origin in the target universe; a portfolio child feed has already been prepared; deployment arguments satisfy downstream constructors and wiring. Creates and registers a canonical child question ledger with the parent share token and a coordinator attached to its portfolio branch feed. It creates no per-question auction. SecurityPoolRegistered , then DeploySecurityPool","title":"SecurityPoolFactory","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["portfolio","question","complete set","reporting","settlement"],"path":"reference/contracts/securitypool.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Question-ledger reads, complete-set purchases and redemptions, wallet reporting, and shared-fork entrypoints.","text":"Question ledger for one shared portfolio, question and universe. Shares and reporting are question-specific; provider capital, settlement cash and fees are accounted centrally. SecurityPool source contract","title":"SecurityPool","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/securitypool.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Question-ledger reads, complete-set purchases and redemptions, wallet reporting, and shared-fork entrypoints.","text":"Read portfolio owner positions from the capital ledger and question fee streams from the settlement book. After portfolio binding, question storage mirrors settlement cash, share supply and lastSettlementPortfolioTime , the portfolio time of its last checkpoint. Fee stream deadlines use native time; question storage does not enumerate owners. getTotalPoolHeldAttoRep is always zero because provider capital is central. getCurrentMintingCapacityAttoEth is zero after reporting starts; otherwise it includes current question collateral and available shared capacity. attoSharesToAttoEth , attoSharesToAttoRep and attoEthToAttoShares quote central settlement including fork recovery rights. isEscalationResolved recognizes inherited final outcomes and locally finalized reports. getFeeEpochEndTime is the native fee cutoff, bounded by the universe fork time. Additional validated configuration and accounting getters: questionId , universeId , initialEscalationGameDepositAttoRep , zoltar , parent , shareToken , repToken , openOraclePriceCoordinator , openOracle , escalationGameFactory , eventEmitter , escalationGame , questionData , securityPoolForker , collateralRepairAuction , securityPoolFactory , settlementCollateralAttoEth , statoblastSecurityMultiplierBps , shareTokenSupplyAttoShares , awaitingForkContinuation , systemState , portfolioCustody .","title":"SecurityPool","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"security-assumptions","heading":"Security assumptions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/securitypool.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Question-ledger reads, complete-set purchases and redemptions, wallet reporting, and shared-fork entrypoints.","text":"Price-sensitive withdrawal, commitment-increase, and liquidation calls depend on A16 timely inclusion , A21 genesis REP and WETH behavior , A19 observable correctable price , and A06 lifecycle executors . User-initiated pool calls additionally depend on A28 account authority .","title":"SecurityPool","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/securitypool.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Question-ledger reads, complete-set purchases and redemptions, wallet reporting, and shared-fork entrypoints.","text":"Each call lists its parameter names in ABI order. The contract index defines the columns. All deployed question ledgers belong to a portfolio. Providers use its capital ledger for deposits, commitments, health targets, retirement and liquidation. Reporters fund separate reporter custody with wallet REP. Purchases use the registered portfolio route; traders redeem through the question ledger. There is no standalone vault, tick auction or operation queue. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals setPortfolioCustody(custody) The canonical portfolio Nonzero custody; not already bound; caller matches factory registration. Binds its central capital ledger once and registers the question fee stream. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. creditEscalationSurplus(amountAttoRep) The active escalation game Caller matches the configured game; REP has already been transferred. Transfers irrevocable surplus REP to the portfolio for vested provider earnings. Reporter principal remains separate. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. refreshPortfolioFees() Anyone Central accounting is bound. Refreshes the question linear stream and settlement mirrors. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. burnEscalationWinnerHaircut(amountAttoRep) The active escalation game Caller matches the configured game; REP has already been transferred. Burns the winning settlement haircut through Zoltar. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. createCompleteSet() with ETH The registered portfolio purchase route Operational unforked universe, reporting not started, question not ended, authenticated purchase and sufficient healthy backing. Mints question complete sets using central settlement cash and shared backing. The portfolio transfers the resulting shares to the buyer. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. redeemCompleteSet(amountAttoShares) A complete-set holder Operational unforked universe; holder has all outcome shares. Burns complete sets and pays their current ETH and recovery REP entitlement. Released exposure becomes available to the portfolio. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. redeemShares() A winning-share holder Operational question with a final outcome; finalized claims retain their protected settlement path. Burns winning shares and pays their central ETH and recovery REP entitlement. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. depositWalletRepToEscalationGame(outcome, maximumDepositAttoRep) Any reporter Valid outcome, wallet allowance and balance, operational reporting window; no inherited fixed outcome. Transfers accepted wallet REP into separate reporter custody and records the chosen outcome; creates the game if needed. EscalationGameSet when the game is created, followed by reporter deposit and REP transfer events. withdrawFromEscalationGame(outcome, depositIndexes) Anyone, for one recorded depositor Operational question and final outcome; unresolved parent claims follow the child settlement path. Settles the supplied local deposit indexes. Payout goes to the recorded owner; losing claims receive no REP. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. withdrawForkedEscalationDeposits(outcome, proofs) Anyone, for one recorded depositor Operational finalized question, valid unconsumed proofs, one depositor per batch. Settles authenticated inherited leaves without migrating every reporter. Bounded checkpoints prepare deep history; payouts go to the recorded owner. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. updateSettlementCollateral() Anyone Central accounting is bound. Checkpoints central question fees and updates the local settlement mirror. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. updateRetentionRate() Anyone Central accounting is bound. Refreshes the central linear fee stream. No standalone retention curve is maintained. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. setStartingParams(currentRetentionRate, initialCollateral) The factory Factory authentication; deployment initialization. Initializes the question fee clock and settlement mirror. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. activateForkMode() The canonical forker No inherited fixed outcome; the central fork checks apply. Freezes shared portfolio accounting and marks the question forked. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. initializeForkedEscalationGame(startBondAttoRep, nonDecisionThresholdAttoRep, elapsedAtFork, fixedQuestionOutcome) The canonical forker No existing game. Deploys a continuation game with the inherited bond, threshold, elapsed time and outcome. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. initializeForkCarryOutcome(sourceGame, snapshotId, peaks, leafCount, carryTotalAttoRep, resolutionBalanceAttoRep, nullifierRoot) SecurityPoolForker only A game is configured; it is a fork continuation with fewer than three initialized outcomes; leaf counts fit the MMR; every call supplies the same source and nonzero snapshot ID, with the next outcome’s data; the third call verifies the full commitment. Installs the next outcome of the continuation game’s canonical snapshot. Three calls complete its immutable carry peaks, counts, totals, resolution balances, and normalized nullifier roots. ForkCarryCheckpoint on completion of the third outcome resumeForkedEscalationGame() Anyone Complete authenticated carry snapshot and funded central reporter custody. Resumes a prepared continuation at its fixed portfolio time anchor. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. setAwaitingForkContinuation(shouldAwait) SecurityPoolForker only No lifecycle or value-change guard. Stores whether complete-set minting must wait for continuation initialization, then refreshes bound portfolio fee accounting. AwaitingForkContinuationSet , including for a repeated value; bound pools then emit central checkpoints and stream replacement when applicable setSystemState(newState) SecurityPoolForker only No transition or value-change guard. Replaces the pool lifecycle state directly, then refreshes bound portfolio fee accounting. SystemStateSet , including for a repeated state; bound pools then emit central checkpoints and stream replacement when applicable setShareTokenSupplyAttoShares(newShareTokenSupplyAttoShares) The canonical forker Caller is the immutable forker. Initializes the child question share-supply mirror. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. authorizeChildPool(pool) SecurityPoolForker only This parent pool is already authorized; candidate reports this share token; candidate universe has no different canonical pool. No pool-lifecycle guard. Asks the lineage share token to establish pool as the canonical authorized pool for its universe; reauthorizing the same pool is a no-op. AuthorizationUpdated only on first authorization; no event when already authorized Direct ETH transfer to receive() Portfolio transfers and permitted initialization senders Bound portfolio, or the configured parent, forker or auction sender before binding. Accepts ETH; unsolicited ETH creates no settlement or provider claim. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting.","title":"SecurityPool","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["fork","migration","child pool","auction"],"path":"reference/contracts/securitypoolforker.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface, the child-game trust boundary, and the fork initiation, child creation, vault migration, and Collateral Repair Auction entrypoints.","text":"Routes shared-portfolio question forks and lazy child question preparation. SecurityPoolForker source contract","title":"SecurityPoolForker","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/securitypoolforker.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface, the child-game trust boundary, and the fork initiation, child creation, vault migration, and Collateral Repair Auction entrypoints.","text":"zoltar identifies the protocol root. getQuestionOutcome returns the fixed or finalized question outcome, or None while unresolved; the shared portfolio clock protects pre-fork final claims.","title":"SecurityPoolForker","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"child-game-trust-boundary","heading":"Child-game trust boundary","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/securitypoolforker.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface, the child-game trust boundary, and the fork initiation, child creation, vault migration, and Collateral Repair Auction entrypoints.","text":"The immutable delegate is validated against Zoltar at deployment. Portfolio paths authenticate the source question through its factory namespace and central books before changing capital or installing claims. Caller-supplied module addresses are never used as delegate targets.","title":"SecurityPoolForker","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/securitypoolforker.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface, the child-game trust boundary, and the fork initiation, child creation, vault migration, and Collateral Repair Auction entrypoints.","text":"Each call lists its parameter names in ABI order. The contract index defines the columns. Owner capital and reporter custody migrate through the shared portfolio coordinator. Child question ledgers and their fixed-size reporter snapshots are prepared lazily. No owner migrates a vault for each question. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals preparePortfolioQuestion(source, targetUniverse) Anyone Canonical bound source; its universe has forked; the portfolio recognizes a ready target branch and the source reporter identity. Factory and share-token bindings are verified. Deploys and binds the canonical target question contract and share-token namespace separately from report-history installation. A forked target can be prepared for frozen share recovery without activating a game. Canonical pool deployment and authorization events when the target is new. materializePortfolioQuestion(source, targetUniverse) Anyone The preparation guards apply. A target whose native universe has already forked can activate only a protected final reporter or trader claim owned by that universe. Already operational targets return without duplicate installation. Prepares the target if absent. With inherited reporting, installs one outcome of the fixed 64-peak snapshot per call; the third call activates the question and resumes reporting at the fixed portfolio time anchor. Without reporter history, activates immediately. Late installation does not postpone finality. An already-final game must agree with the inherited reporter and outstanding-share settlement deadlines, including the minimum response period, or activation reverts. Preparation may be a separate transaction to bound deployment gas. Canonical deployment events when needed, then game continuation and carry checkpoint events when a reporter game is installed. initiateSecurityPoolFork(securityPool) Anyone The universe has forked and the question was not final before that fork. Marks the unresolved question forked and freezes its shared books. Shared fork, question deployment and reporting continuation events as applicable. forkZoltarWithOwnEscalationGame(securityPool) Anyone Canonical reporter question has non-decision and sufficient admission REP; universe is not already forked. Uses this question’s reporter custody to pay native fork admission and freezes its portfolio. Shared fork, question deployment and reporting continuation events as applicable. createChildUniverse(securityPool, outcomeIndex) Anyone Canonical source and prepared shared branch; target universe and question identity must match. Materializes the immediate portfolio child question using the shared branch and lazy carry installation. Shared fork, question deployment and reporting continuation events as applicable.","title":"SecurityPoolForker","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["escalation","dispute","deposit","non-decision"],"path":"reference/contracts/escalationgame.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the start, deposit, withdrawal, claim, fork-carry, escrow export, and fallback-routed entrypoints, with accounting examples.","text":"Escrows outcome REP, raises the running resolution cost, detects non-decision , and settles local or carried deposits. EscalationGame source contract","title":"EscalationGame","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/escalationgame.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the start, deposit, withdrawal, claim, fork-carry, escrow export, and fallback-routed entrypoints, with accounting examples.","text":"Base getters are securityPool , repToken , activationTime , nonDecisionThresholdAttoRep , startBondAttoRep , nonDecisionTimestamp , nonDecisionState , forkContinuation , forkElapsedAtStart , forkResumedAt , fixedQuestionOutcome , nodes , disputeStakedRepByVaultAttoRep , totalDisputeStakedAttoRep , ordinarySettlementInitialized , ordinaryWinningReserveAttoRep , ordinarySettlementForkThresholdAttoRep , creditedSurplusAttoRep , and winnerHaircutPaidByFork . Ordinary settlement fixes the winner liability and threshold; losing local deposits and escrow read as zero afterward, while raw historical nodes remain stored. The fallback forwards every selector other than the two signed deposits to EscalationGameClaimDelegate , which provides the reads rootClaimSourceGame , getInheritedClaimAllocation , and getUnresolvedClaimInterval , plus getClaimCheckpointProgress , getClaimCheckpointState , getClaimCheckpointWitnessSegment , getReusableClaimCheckpoint , getClaimIntervalTransform , and isClaimHistoryFrozen . Allocation reads inspect at most eight ancestors; deeper histories require permissionless checkpoint preparation. Central portfolio reporter claims keep identity coordinates across live branches and resolve their interval without ancestor traversal; their membership proofs and per-branch nullifiers still apply. getClaimCheckpointProgress distinguishes stored completion from readiness through the bounded read path. getClaimCheckpointState returns initialization, the pending commitment, the reused ancestor, and the current four-coordinate interval. getClaimCheckpointWitnessSegment returns the canonical source, prior commitment, first step, and length of the active segment; at most eight local transform reads reconstruct the batch from current state. getReusableClaimCheckpoint exports a completed interval and its excluded immediate-source anchor; descendants select caches only along their canonical lineage for the same claim tuple. The allocation read returns source principal, retained principal, reward-interval length, and the cumulative reward endpoint after compaction of consumed principal prefixes. The reward interval is the deposit’s range within its outcome’s cumulative deposits: its start is the returned endpoint minus the returned length. It determines which portion enters the reward calculation; the length is not a payable reward. Exported principal intervals compact consumed prefixes, while reward positions remain unshifted by prior claims. Allocations within a game stay fixed in every claim order. disputeStakedRepByVaultAttoRep is nominal reporter principal, not collateral value. getDisputeBackingAttoRep returns actual game REP; getVaultDisputeBackingAttoRep credits ordinary reports only for their contribution retained after source surplus, and returns zero for continuation claims. Inherited carry remains aggregate commitment state until proof settlement. Use previewDepositOnOutcome , computeIterativeAttritionCostAttoRep , computeTimeSinceStartFromAttritionCostAttoRep , totalCostAttoRep , getEscalationGameEndDate , getQuestionResolution , getFinalQuestionResolution , hasReachedNonDecision , canTriggerOwnFork , getBindingCapitalAttoRep , getOutcomeBalancesAttoRep , getDepositsByOutcome , getDepositsByOutcomeLength , forkCarrySnapshotInitialized , forkCarrySnapshotOutcomesInitialized , getOutcomeState , getForkCarrySnapshot , getForkCarryRoots , isForkCarryFundingComplete , getCarryLeafPageByOutcome , getProofConsumedCarriedDepositIndexesByOutcome , getLocalUnresolvedPrincipalByVaultAndOutcome , and getForkedEscrowByVaultAndOutcome for calculations, lifecycle authorization, pages, carry state, and escrow. getCarryLeafPageByOutcome limits visited nodes by maxEntries , including consumed nodes; empty or short pages may return a nonzero continuation cursor. Wallet reports and finalized withdrawals route through SecurityPool ; after an ordinary game starts, wallet-funded deposits use depositRepOnOutcome , depositRepOnOutcomeWithPermit , or depositRepOnOutcomeWithAuthorization and mint no pool backing units.","title":"EscalationGame","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/escalationgame.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the start, deposit, withdrawal, claim, fork-carry, escrow export, and fallback-routed entrypoints, with accounting examples.","text":"Each call lists its parameter names in ABI order. The contract index defines the columns. Rows marked \"through fallback() \" are not in the compiled ABI: the fallback forwards the two signed deposit selectors to EscalationGameDepositDelegate and every other unknown selector to EscalationGameClaimDelegate by delegatecall , and returns or reverts with the delegate output. A call restricted to the owning pool or its forker reverts without reason data for any other caller. For central portfolio custody, report REP is held in the reporter ledger, independently of provider REP. Unrelated resumed continuations accept wallet-funded deposits. Fixed-outcome continuations end exactly three days after the child response anchor, which is the native parent fork time plus nine weeks; late materialization does not extend it. Other continuation deadlines retain their inherited elapsed time and minimum response period. The signed elapsed offset is negative when the parent fork interrupts the three-day activation delay; that remaining delay is preserved, and attrition stays zero until it expires. A later native fork interrupts unresolved reporting before any fixed outcome can be returned. totalCostAttoRep freezes attrition at the recorded universe fork time; a continuation whose universe forked before resume retains its inherited elapsed offset without adding wall-clock time. The scheduled deadline is the last second before the forward curve strictly exceeds binding capital, including integer-rounding plateaus; reporter and settlement books schedule that same cutoff. An unresolved parent cannot become final later. Per-question REP exports reject central custody. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals start(startBondAttoRep, nonDecisionThresholdAttoRep) Immutable owner, the EscalationGameFactory , only ( Only owner ), during atomic deployment Game not already started, and nonDecisionThresholdAttoRep > startBondAttoRep > 0 ( Invalid game start ). Initializes a local game and sets activation three days after deployment. For ordinary pool games, the factory lowers an oversized configured bond to nonDecisionThresholdAttoRep - 1 before this call. GameStarted startFromFork(startBondAttoRep, nonDecisionThresholdAttoRep, elapsedAtFork, fixedQuestionOutcome, winnerHaircutPaidByFork, forkCarryInitialBackingAttoRep) Immutable owner, the EscalationGameFactory , only ( Only owner ), during atomic continuation deployment Game not already started, and nonDecisionThresholdAttoRep > startBondAttoRep > 0 ( Invalid game start ); elapsedAtFork is a signed number of seconds between minus three days and seven weeks, inclusive ( Invalid fork elapsed time ). Initializes a paused continuation with inherited elapsed time, an optional fixed matching child outcome, and immutable fork-time escalation-haircut and backing accounting. It does not start the remaining clock until resumeFromFork . GameContinuedFromFork resumeFromFork() Owning SecurityPool only ( Only pool ) Fork-continuation mode; not previously resumed; aggregate REP funding complete ( isForkCarryFundingComplete ). All three carry outcomes must be initialized ( Fork snapshot incomplete ), even for empty history. When the canonical factory records nonzero initial backing, the live reporter balance must cover that actual escrow after source surplus and native fork burn, minus valid direct pre-resume exports. Without recorded initial backing, it must cover effective inherited unresolved principal plus local unresolved principal across all three outcomes. Resumes once carry funding is complete. Standalone continuations use the transaction timestamp and deadline max(rebasedCurveEnd, forkResumedAt + 3 days) . Bound continuations use the fixed portfolio response anchor; when their outcome is fixed, the deadline is exactly anchor plus three days, otherwise inherited attrition and the minimum response interval apply. Late materialization never moves the anchor. Final resolution also checks whether the native universe forked before that deadline. ForkContinuationResumed depositRepOnOutcome(outcome, maximumDepositAttoRep) Any REP holder Current game is ordinary or a resumed portfolio continuation without a fixed outcome; game unresolved; pool operational; universe unforked; outcome non- None ; caller allowance to this game covers the accepted REP; preview accepts a positive amount within the remaining threshold room. Transfers accepted REP from the caller into dispute escrow and appends its nominal deposit and carry leaf, without minting reporter backing units. Ordinary games release increases in the minimum outcome balance to vested portfolio REP income. Continuations do not release this ordinary surplus. REP Transfer , LocalDepositAppended , DepositOnOutcome , EscalationSurplusAccrued when the ordinary minimum increases, and NonDecisionReached when the deposit reaches non-decision depositRepOnOutcomeWithPermit(outcome, maximumDepositAttoRep, deadline, v, r, s) through fallback() Any child-universe REP holder The depositRepOnOutcome game, pool, and preview prerequisites; the pool universe is not genesis ( Genesis REP does not support permit ); the permit succeeds or the caller allowance to this game covers the previewed amount ( Game permit and allowance insufficient ). Calls the REP token permit for the previewed amount from the caller to this game, ignores a failed permit when the existing allowance already covers that amount, then performs the depositRepOnOutcome deposit. REP Approval when the permit succeeds, then the depositRepOnOutcome signals depositRepOnOutcomeWithAuthorization(owner, outcome, maximumDepositAttoRep, validAfter, validBefore, nonce, v, r, s) through fallback() Anyone relaying the owner's ERC-3009 signature The depositRepOnOutcome game, pool, and preview prerequisites; the pool universe is not genesis ( Genesis REP does not support authorization ); the signature authorizes the token nonce keccak256(abi.encode(nonce, operationHash, owner)) , where operationHash is keccak256(abi.encode(depositRepOnOutcomeWithAuthorization.selector, owner, securityPool, universeId, questionId, outcome, maximumDepositAttoRep, previewedAmount)) . Pulls the previewed amount from owner to this game through the REP token receiveWithAuthorization and records the deposit for owner . REP AuthorizationUsed and Transfer , LocalDepositAppended , DepositOnOutcome , EscalationSurplusAccrued when the ordinary minimum increases, and NonDecisionReached when the deposit reaches non-decision recordDepositFromSecurityPool(depositor, outcome, amountAttoRep, expectedCumulativeAttoRep) Owning SecurityPool only ( Only pool ) Explicit non-decision state is None ; game unresolved; valid outcome; preview and accepted cumulative amount match; room remains below threshold. Appends the nominal deposit and carry leaf. In ordinary games, it releases any increase in the minimum outcome balance through the same portfolio-income path as depositRepOnOutcome ; only the new report’s retained contribution receives collateral credit. Continuations release no ordinary surplus. LocalDepositAppended , DepositOnOutcome , EscalationSurplusAccrued when the ordinary minimum increases, and NonDecisionReached when the deposit reaches non-decision withdrawDeposit(depositIndex, outcome) , the uint256 overload Owning SecurityPool only ( Only pool ) Explicit non-decision state is None ; non- None supplied outcome; game final; game and pool final outcomes match; valid local deposit index, unsettled unless it is a retired ordinary loser. Settles one local deposit after resolution. An ordinary first claim freezes the winning liability and burns terminal excess beyond the surplus already paid at source; ordinary losers retire together and a valid losing index returns zero repeatedly. A winner consumes its reserved payout and burn and pays the immutable depositor. Continuation losers still consume their individual escrow. OrdinarySettlementInitialized on first ordinary settlement. Winners and continuation losers emit CarryDepositConsumed and VaultEscrowUpdated ; winners also emit ClaimDeposit and positive payout or burn signals. Retired ordinary losers emit no per-deposit settlement event. initializeForkCarryOutcome(sourceGame, snapshotId, peaks, leafCount, carryTotalAttoRep, resolutionBalanceAttoRep, nullifierRoot) Owning SecurityPool only Fork-continuation mode; fewer than three initialized outcomes; each leaf count fits the MMR; every call supplies the same source and nonzero snapshot ID, with the next outcome’s data. The third call requires the hash of all normalized data to match that ID. Installs one outcome’s inherited peaks, leaf count, carry total, resolution balance, and normalized nullifier root. The nonzero full snapshot identity and source are fixed on the first call. The third call verifies the installed data against that identity before marking the snapshot complete. Two or more threshold-full inherited balances then set nonDecisionState to InheritedThresholdTie without creating a local timestamp. ForkCarryCheckpoint after all three outcomes; additionally InheritedThresholdTie when the completed balances meet the non-decision threshold processClaimCheckpoint(outcomeIndex, amountAttoRep, cumulativeAmountAttoRep, leafIndex, maxSteps, baseCommitment, transforms) through fallback() Anyone Complete fork snapshot when applicable; valid outcome and interval; maxSteps from 1 to 8 and no more transforms than that budget ( Invalid checkpoint budget ). An empty transform array requires a zero base commitment ( Invalid checkpoint base ). Discovery requires empty transforms ( Checkpoint discovery pending ) and each committed source to be PoolForked with its migration window closed. Applying queued transforms requires a nonempty batch no longer than the pending step count ( Checkpoint transforms required ), whose forward hash matches the pending commitment bound to this game and claim tuple ( Invalid checkpoint batch ); invalid or stale batches revert atomically. Completed calls and successful parent-cache adoption do not apply supplied transforms. Claims still require authenticated membership and an unspent nullifier. An empty transform array with a zero base commitment discovers at most eight frozen ancestor transforms per call and commits them to a hash stack. A completed canonical ancestor checkpoint for the same claim tuple replaces its already-prepared prefix; the excluded source transform is still processed. After discovery, a batch supplies one base commitment and at most eight transforms in forward hash order. The complete batch must hash to the pending commitment before those transforms apply in reverse order, preserving every generation’s integer rounding. Each transform contains repBeforeAttoRep , repRemainingAttoRep , and consumedBeforeAttoRep . Recovery anchors store a source and prior hash for each eight-transform segment. Every unfinished call checks the immediate parent’s completed cache; if available, it replaces even partially applied preparation with that prefix plus its one frozen excluded-source transform. Otherwise initialization reads the immediate source. The target and immediate parent remain live reads. Preparation does not consume a proof or move REP; completed calls are idempotent. ClaimCheckpointProgress ; returns whether the stored checkpoint is complete. Read getClaimCheckpointProgress for readiness and remaining traversal state initializeForkClaimCheckpoint(sourceGame) through fallback() Owning SecurityPool only ( Only pool ) sourceGame equals the source game recorded by the installed carry snapshot ( Claim source ). Records the root claim source game: the value sourceGame reports from rootClaimSourceGame() , or sourceGame itself when that value is zero. The game runs the same step internally while installing a carry snapshot with a nonzero source game, and no pool function calls this selector. No event claimDepositForWinning(depositIndex, outcome) Owning SecurityPool or its SecurityPoolForker Non- None supplied outcome and valid unsettled local deposit with sufficient escrow. An ordinary non-fork game requires final settlement and the actual winning outcome. Other paths rely on the trusted caller to select the winner. Consumes a selected local deposit as a winner, consumes its vault escrow, burns the computed escalation haircut when nonzero, and transfers the remaining positive REP payout to the deposit's immutable depositor. CarryDepositConsumed , VaultEscrowUpdated , ClaimDeposit with transferredRep = true ; REP payout Transfer and escalation-haircut burn signals only when their amounts are positive exportUnresolvedDeposit(depositIndex, outcome) Owning SecurityPool or its SecurityPoolForker Non- None outcome and a valid unsettled local deposit. Ordinary settlement must not have initialized ( Final claims not exportable ); final resolution is not otherwise required. Returns deposit identity and amount to the trusted caller while consuming the local deposit from unresolved/escrow accounting without transferring REP. CarryDepositConsumed and VaultEscrowUpdated ; no ClaimDeposit or REP transfer withdrawDeposit(proof, outcome) , the CarriedDepositProof overload Owning SecurityPool or its SecurityPoolForker Non- None supplied outcome; game final and matching the pool final outcome; supplied outcome is the winner; parent deposit was not directly claimed; valid unconsumed Merkle/nullifier proof. Consumes an inherited proof, transfers any positive winning payout, and burns the positive escalation haircut unless the fork already paid it. CarryDepositConsumed and ClaimDeposit with transferredRep = true ; REP payout Transfer and escalation-haircut burn signals only when positive exportVaultUnresolvedTotalsWithoutTransfer(vault) Owning SecurityPool or its SecurityPoolForker vault is nonzero and has not exported before; ordinary settlement has not initialized ( Final claims not exportable ). Marks the vault's local unresolved totals exported exactly once, clears each outcome amount, and consumes aggregate unresolved and escrow accounting when positive, but leaves token movement to its caller. Always VaultUnresolvedTotalsExported with transferredRep = false , including when every amount is zero; VaultEscrowUpdated only for a positive total; no REP transfer drainAllRep(receiver) Owning SecurityPool only Noncentral custody and a nonzero receiver. Unreachable through current portfolio question adapters. Noncentral-custody helper that transfers the game REP balance. The portfolio-only protocol has no caller; central reporter custody rejects this operation. REP Transfer for a positive balance; no event at zero balance recordForkedEscrowForOutcome(depositor, outcome, sourcePrincipalAttoRep, childRepAmountAttoRep) Owning SecurityPool or its SecurityPoolForker Outcome is not None ; depositor is nonzero. Source principal and child REP may independently be zero; when both are zero, the call is a no-op. Accumulates source principal and child REP escrow for the depositor and outcome. The depositor remains the immutable payout owner; inherited claims remain in the carry commitment and are not copied into child-local ownership state. When both amounts are zero, returns without changing state or emitting an event. ForkedEscrowRecorded for a nonzero record; no event when both amounts are zero exportForkedEscrowByOutcome(vault, repReceiver) Owning SecurityPool or its SecurityPoolForker vault and repReceiver are nonzero. Marks every remaining per-outcome escrow amount exported and transfers its positive child REP. When all outcomes were already empty or exported, returns zero arrays without state change, token transfer, or event. ForkedEscrowExported when any source principal or child REP remains; VaultEscrowUpdated and REP Transfer when positive child REP is transferred; no event for an already-empty export exportForkedEscrowByOutcomeWithoutTransfer(vault) Owning SecurityPool or its SecurityPoolForker vault is nonzero. Marks every remaining per-outcome escrow amount exported without transferring child REP. When all outcomes were already empty or exported, returns zero arrays without state change or event. ForkedEscrowExported with transferredRep = false when any source principal or child REP remains; VaultEscrowUpdated when positive child REP remains; no REP transfer; no event for an already-empty export settleOrdinaryGame() Anyone Ordinary game, not a fork continuation, with nonDecisionState = None and a final outcome. Game REP must cover the initial winner reserve ( Winner reserve underfunded ). Freezes the final outcome and effective fork threshold, reserves the cumulative winning payout and burn liability, and retires all losing local escrow without visiting deposits. Each later winning claim subtracts its payout and burn from that reserve. Burns terminal REP above the reserve; the ordinary minimum-outcome surplus was already paid irrevocably as deposits arrived. A repeated call with no surplus succeeds. See reporter liability invariant . OrdinarySettlementInitialized once; OrdinaryResidualRepBurned and REP Transfer only when surplus is positive sweepResidualRepToSecurityPool() Anyone Final outcome and positive releasable surplus ( No sweepable REP for an ordinary no-op). Ordinary settlement preserves all unclaimed winning liabilities. Continuations require no effective unresolved principal and, after retiring inherited aggregate escrow, no remaining local stake or vault escrow. Applies ordinary winner-reserve settlement and burns its terminal excess. Also burns fork-continuation residual so pre-child capital cannot accrue to late or nonexistent child owners. OrdinarySettlementInitialized if first ordinary settlement, then OrdinaryResidualRepBurned ; ForkContinuationResidualRepBurned for a fork continuation","title":"EscalationGame","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"accounting-examples","heading":"Accounting examples","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/escalationgame.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the start, deposit, withdrawal, claim, fork-carry, escrow export, and fallback-routed entrypoints, with accounting examples.","text":"","title":"EscalationGame","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"required-support-threshold","heading":"Required support threshold","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/escalationgame.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the start, deposit, withdrawal, claim, fork-carry, escrow export, and fallback-routed entrypoints, with accounting examples.","text":"The contract waits ACTIVATION_DELAY (3 days) after start() before the escalation clock begins. From day 3 the cumulative binding-capital requirement rises from the configured start bond S to the non-decision threshold T over the 49-day ESCALATION_TIME_LENGTH , reaching T on day 52. The contract evaluates a fixed-point curve; this expression is its readable idealization. A ( d ) = 0 for 0 ≤ d ≤ 3 = S × exp ( ln ( T S ) × d − 3 49 ) for 3 < d < 52 = T for d ≥ 52","title":"EscalationGame","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"winning-deposit-payout","heading":"Winning-deposit payout","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/escalationgame.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the start, deposit, withdrawal, claim, fork-carry, escrow export, and fallback-routed entrypoints, with accounting examples.","text":"Binding capital is the median outcome balance at settlement; no outcome balance can exceed the non-decision threshold. The reward-eligible cap is binding capital plus binding capital divided by EXCESS_REWARD_WINDOW_DIVISOR (2), so only the portion of winning deposits up to 1.5 × binding capital participates in the bonus. Of the binding capital, 3 / 5 funds the reward pool and 2 / 5 funds the escalation-haircut pool; both are shared pro rata across the reward-eligible principal, which is the smaller of the winning outcome balance and that cap. Let binding capital be 10 REP , so the reward-eligible cap is 15 REP , the reward pool is 6 REP , and the escalation-haircut pool is 4 REP . If the winning outcome holds 15 REP and one deposit contributed 5 REP inside the eligible range: Principal returned: 5 REP . Bonus: 5 × 6 / 15 = 2 REP . Winning payout: 5 + 2 = 7 REP . Haircut burned: 5 × 4 / 15 = 1.333333333333333333 REP at attoREP precision. A winning deposit position above 15 REP returns its principal without a bonus. When Zoltar's fork threshold at settlement is below the game's non-decision threshold, the whole withdrawal is scaled by their ratio. If this game's non-decision threshold were 20 REP and the fork threshold 16 REP , the 7 REP payout becomes 7 × 16 / 20 = 5.6 REP , rounded down to attoREP. Fork scaling can therefore reduce the final transfer below principal.","title":"EscalationGame","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["OpenOracle","price","coordinator","staged operation"],"path":"reference/contracts/openoraclepricecoordinator.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the price request, staged operation, recovery, and openOracleCallback entrypoints.","text":"Per-question adapter to the portfolio universe’s shared REP/ETH feed. It exposes reporting and canonical price reads without staging capital operations. OpenOraclePriceCoordinator source contract","title":"OpenOraclePriceCoordinator","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/openoraclepricecoordinator.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the price request, staged operation, recovery, and openOracleCallback entrypoints.","text":"priceFeed and sharedPriceFeed identify the shared universe feed. It owns accepted price, freshness and report candidates. lastSettlementTimestamp records settlement eligibility, not delayed execution time. isPriceValid checks the accepted price and canonical priceValidUntil ; pending or rejected reports do not extend expiry. pendingReportId and pendingReportSponsor identify the latest candidate, without blocking independent candidates. Reports use separate escrows. reportDelegate implements authenticated shared report creation. securityPool is bound once by the coordinator factory. Capital operations execute directly against an accepted price; there is no operation queue. Additional validated getters: lastPrice , pendingReportMaxSettlementBaseFeeAttoEthPerGas , minimumToken1ReportAttoEth , getRequestPriceCostAttoEth , getSettlementCallbackGasLimit , reputationToken , openOracle , weth , gasConsumedOpenOracleReportPrice , gasConsumedSettlement , gasUnitsForOneDispute , initialReportPriorityFeeAttoEthPerGas , targetPriceErrorForDispute , openOracleSecurityMultiplierBps , settlementTime , disputeDelay , protocolFee , feePercentage , multiplier , timeType , trackDisputes , protocolFeeRecipient , escalationHaltMultiplierBps , maxSettlementBaseFeeMultiplierBps , minLiquidationPriceDistanceBps .","title":"OpenOraclePriceCoordinator","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"security-assumptions","heading":"Security assumptions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/openoraclepricecoordinator.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the price request, staged operation, recovery, and openOracleCallback entrypoints.","text":"Report liveness depends on A16 timely inclusion , A17 corrector capability , A18 independent correction incentive , A19 observable correctable price , and A06 lifecycle executors . When lastPrice is zero, the official client currently needs an offchain market quote to propose the first report; quote availability is a client limitation rather than a protocol security assumption. Proposals copied from a nonzero cached price do not use that quote path.","title":"OpenOraclePriceCoordinator","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/openoraclepricecoordinator.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the price request, staged operation, recovery, and openOracleCallback entrypoints.","text":"Each call lists its parameter names in ABI order. The contract index defines the columns. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals requestPrice(proposedRepPerEthPrice, requestedInitialAttoWeth, bountyAttoEth) with report funding Anyone; shared candidates may coexist and renew before the accepted price expires Nonzero proposed REP-per-ETH price, a bounty of at least 101 attoETH covered by msg.value, and sufficient WETH/REP funding and approvals for the shared feed’s live exposure and inventory bounds. Zero requested WETH uses the computed minimum. Funding is isolated in a new report escrow; the caller must accept any excess-ETH refund. Opens and atomically funds a fresh WETH/REP report without staging a new operation, retains the whole committed bounty as the settler reward, then refunds any ETH above the bounty through a low-level caller callback. Callback rejection rolls back the report and initial position. PriceRequested and ReportFundingAssigned from the shared feed. recoverSettledPendingReport() Anyone A pending report ID exists and its stored OpenOracle storedGame(reportId).settlementTimestamp is nonzero. Recovers the feed’s latest settled candidate without accepting its price. Use feed.recoverReport(reportId) for another unprocessed settled candidate. Recovery marks only that candidate rejected and returns its isolated escrow credits to its original sponsor. PendingReportRecovered from the shared feed. setSharedPriceFeed(feed) Deployment worker only No prior shared feed or ledger; nonzero feed with matching REP, WETH, and OpenOracle. Binds the shared report source once, before the question ledger is assigned. SharedPriceFeedSet syncExposure() Anyone; pool accounting checkpoints also invoke it A shared coordinator must already be registered by its canonical factory. Reads the live portfolio aggregate, max(standing commitments, funded demand), and emits a checkpoint if it changed. Question finality does not independently clear this shared exposure. Feed ExposureCheckpoint when the portfolio aggregate changes setSecurityPool(securityPool) The coordinator’s deployer only; production binding passes through the authenticated factory and deployment-worker chain Current securityPool is zero ( Security pool already set ); the argument itself is not required to be nonzero. A nonzero value binds the pool permanently. A zero value emits SecurityPoolSet with zero but leaves the setter callable by its deployer. SecurityPoolFactory supplies the nonzero canonical pool immediately after deploying the pool. A prepared coordinator cannot be claimed by another caller between transactions. SecurityPoolSet","title":"OpenOraclePriceCoordinator","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["shares","ERC-1155","migrate","outcome"],"path":"reference/contracts/sharetoken.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the ERC-1155 transfer, migrate, authorize, and complete-set entrypoints.","text":"Stores universe-aware ERC-1155 outcome shares and materializes a holder's persistent source entitlement in selected fork branches. ShareToken source contract","title":"ShareToken","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/sharetoken.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the ERC-1155 transfer, migrate, authorize, and complete-set entrypoints.","text":"Base and relationship getters are name , symbol , zoltar , canonicalPoolByUniverse , recoveredCompleteSetShares , _balances , _supplies , and _operatorApprovals . Standard ERC-1155 reads are supportsInterface , balanceOf , totalSupply , balanceOfBatch , and isApprovedForAll ; protocol-specific reads are isAuthorized , totalSupplyForOutcome , maximumOutcomeSupply , balanceOfOutcome , balanceOfShares , getMigratedShareAmountAttoShares , getTokenId , getTokenIds , unpackTokenId , isPortfolioRecoveryEligible , and balanceOfPortfolioRecoveryRights . The eligibility getter applies the source recovery rules , preserving ordinary source-pool redemption for protected finalized questions. The balance getter returns unspent Invalid, Yes and No recovery rights, or all zeros when ineligible; the minimum bounds complete-set cash recovery. Received rights are included independently of the holder’s source-token balances.","title":"ShareToken","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/sharetoken.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the ERC-1155 transfer, migrate, authorize, and complete-set entrypoints.","text":"Each call lists its parameter names in ABI order. The contract index defines the columns. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals setApprovalForAll(operator, approved) Any token account setting its own operator approval The operator differs from the caller. Sets or clears the operator's authority over all of the caller's outcome-token balances. ApprovalForAll safeTransferFrom(from, to, id, value) and safeTransferFrom(from, to, id, value, data) Share holder or approved ERC-1155 operator Caller holds the source balance or has operator approval; the source account has neither materialized that token into a child branch nor spent source-backed recovery rights by transferring or consuming them; destination is nonzero; the source balance is sufficient; under A22 asset-recipient compatibility , a contract recipient accepts the ERC-1155 callback. Transfers one outcome-token balance without changing supply. TransferSingle safeBatchTransferFrom(from, to, ids, values) and safeBatchTransferFrom(from, to, ids, values, data) Share holder or approved ERC-1155 operator for a nonempty batch; any caller for an empty batch ID and value array lengths match. A nonempty batch also requires holder or operator authority, no listed source token already locked by child materialization or spending source-backed recovery rights, a nonzero destination, sufficient source balances, and, under A22 asset-recipient compatibility , an accepting ERC-1155 callback from a contract recipient; the empty-batch no-op performs none of those checks. A nonempty batch transfers each listed outcome-token balance without changing supply. Equal empty ID and value arrays return as a no-op without an event. TransferBatch for a nonempty batch; no event for an empty batch transferPortfolioRecoveryRights(sourceUniverse, recipient, amountsAttoShares) Recovery-right holder transferring only their own rights; ERC-1155 operator approval does not authorize this call Canonical source is a portfolio question in a forked universe with isPortfolioRecoveryEligible(sourceUniverse) == true ( No live source recovery right ); recipient is a different nonzero account; at least one amount is positive; every amount fits its corresponding unspent recovery-right balance. Transfers the specified Invalid, Yes and No recovery rights independently. Source and child token balances and every branch migration entitlement remain intact. Received credits are spent first; spending source-backed rights locks that source token against transfer. No token-receiver callback is invoked. PortfolioRecoveryRightsTransferred consumePortfolioRecoveryCompleteSets(sourceUniverse, holder, amountAttoShares) The canonical source pool’s portfolio or its configured fork coordinator Canonical source is a portfolio question in a forked universe with isPortfolioRecoveryEligible(sourceUniverse) == true ( No live source recovery right ); positive amount no greater than any of the holder’s three unspent recovery-right balances. The coordinator authenticates the holder and calculates the corresponding cash payout. Consumes equal amounts of Invalid, Yes and No recovery rights, using independently received credits before source-backed rights. Spending a source-backed right locks that source token against transfer; spending received credits alone does not. Source balances and every branch’s migration rights remain intact. Returns cumulative consumed shares. PortfolioRecoveryRightsConsumed migrate(fromId, targetOutcomeIndexes) Holder of the source token ID Source universe forked; canonical source pool is Operational or PoolForked , and an Operational source has no inherited fixed outcome because auto-fork activation rejects one; positive source balance; nonempty, strictly increasing, well-formed outcomes; every target in a multi-target call already has a canonical child pool; after the branch-creation window, a single target must also already exist; each target is an immediate native child, including when its bound question contract skips intermediate reporter ancestors; at least one selected child has an unmaterialized balance; under A22 asset-recipient compatibility , a contract holder accepts onERC1155Received for every target mint. If needed, first freezes the operational source pool and records its fork snapshot. A single-target call may lazily create that child while the branch-creation window is open. It keeps and locks the holder's source entitlement, then mints each selected child-universe token ID up to the current source balance. Later source additions materialize only the unminted delta. A contract holder receives the ERC-1155 single-receiver callback for each mint; rejection rolls back the mint and preceding fork or child setup. Canonical child preparation may emit SecurityPoolRegistered , DeploySecurityPool , and share-token AuthorizationUpdated . Successful materialization emits ERC-1155 TransferSingle and Migrate for each target. authorize(securityPoolCandidate) Initially authorized SecurityPoolFactory for an origin pool; an authorized parent SecurityPool for a child pool Caller is already authorized; the candidate reports this exact share token; its universe has no different canonical pool. Establishes the candidate as canonicalPoolByUniverse for its universe and adds it to the set allowed to mint, burn, and authorize descendants. Reauthorizing the same candidate is a no-op. AuthorizationUpdated on first authorization; no event when the same candidate is already authorized mintCompleteSets(universeId, account, amountAttoShares) An authorized SecurityPool Caller is authorized; account is nonzero; amountAttoShares is positive; under A22 asset-recipient compatibility , a contract account accepts onERC1155BatchReceived . Mints amountAttoShares each of Invalid, Yes, and No to account , then invokes its ERC-1155 batch-receiver callback when it is a contract. Rejection rolls back the mint and the authorized pool's surrounding transaction. TransferBatch on a successful callback burnCompleteSets(universeId, owner, amountAttoShares) An authorized SecurityPool Caller is authorized; owner is nonzero and has at least amountAttoShares of every outcome. Each remaining source balance must still cover its already-separated recovery rights. Burns amountAttoShares each of Invalid, Yes, and No from owner ; global outcome supplies may differ. TransferBatch burnTokenIdAndGetRemainingSupply(tokenId, owner) An authorized SecurityPool owner is nonzero; caller is authorized; no source-backed recovery rights have already been separated for this token. Burns owner 's full balance of tokenId and returns the burned amount and that token ID's remaining supply. TransferSingle , including when the burned balance is zero","title":"ShareToken","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["invariants","safety","liveness","properties"],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"An invariant is a statement that must remain true at every successful external-call boundary, across every legal lifecycle transition, or under the economic assumptions named beside it. Reverts may interrupt a transition, but they must roll back all of its state and asset effects. The catalog separates local contract guards, automated release-tooling guards, cross-contract properties, and economic assumptions. Each entry names the strongest enforcement and evidence currently present; a documented implementation choice is not automatically a safe invariant. See the Statoblast security model for assumptions A01–A28. Funded and underfunded clearing are explained in the Collateral Repair Auction clearing guide . Four connected layers run from local authority guards through cross-contract conservation and lifecycle liveness to economic security. A failure in an earlier layer invalidates the safety claims built above it. Invariant Dependency Correct callers are necessary but insufficient. Their actions must conserve assets, every mandatory state transition must remain reachable, and the named participation and inclusion assumptions must hold where the protocol deliberately relies on economic incentives. Matches All invariants listed below Filter invariants Type Any type Enforcement status Any status Subsystem Any subsystem Expand visible Collapse all Reset filters No invariants match the selected filters.","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":1},{"fragment":"standing","heading":"Classification and scope","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Each entry separates the kind of property from its current enforcement status. A status describes the strongest claim supported by the local implementation and tests; it is not a formal proof. Type Meaning Safety A forbidden caller, state, transition, or replay cannot succeed. Conservation Assets, liabilities, supplies, and claims reconcile without duplication or loss. Liveness A required permissionless action remains executable under the stated conditions. Enforcement status Meaning Contract guard A direct authorization, bound, or state check enforces the property locally. Reviewed preservation Several contracts cooperate to preserve the property in reviewed flows and tests. External assumption Solidity cannot guarantee the property without honest participation, liquidity, transaction inclusion, or external token behavior. Automated tooling guard Release, deployment, or CI tooling rejects a mismatched artifact, address, or runtime-code identity before it is accepted. The index lists the core universe properties and portfolio accounting boundaries. Identifiers name the property being reviewed; they are not contract entrypoints.","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"standing","heading":"Terms used in the catalog","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Pool question The question whose outcome determines the SecurityPool 's winning outcome shares and Escalation Game payouts. Fork question The ended question recorded by the universe fork. It may differ from the pool question. Child branch A well-formed answer encoding for the fork question, used to derive one child universe from its parent. SecurityPool's binary pool-question paths specifically use Invalid, Yes, and No. Continuation game A child Escalation Game initialized from an unresolved parent game's fork snapshot. Its child pool is in fork continuation until the game is fully resumed; the game itself remains the continuation game afterward. Carry The unresolved parent escalation deposits exported into a fork snapshot and paid out by continuation games against Merkle Mountain Range proofs . Nullifier A continuation game's record that one carried deposit identity has been consumed, so its proof cannot be replayed. Fixed payout outcome The pool-question outcome a continuation game must use after its deadline, regardless of its copied balance leader. Direct fork A caller invokes Zoltar's universe-fork entrypoint directly rather than through a pool's own non-decision path. Recursive fork A fork of a child universe created by an earlier fork. Tracked balance An amount recorded by protocol accounting; it excludes unsolicited assets unless a named transition explicitly credits them. Consumed claim A deposit, bid, or proof that has already been settled, refunded, exported, or otherwise made unusable.","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"invariant-index","heading":"Invariant index","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Identifier Property Type Enforcement status UNI-01 One fork per universe Safety Contract guard UNI-02 Valid fork questions Safety Contract guard UNI-03 Fork threshold burn and credit Conservation Contract guard UNI-04 Deterministic child universe IDs Safety Contract guard UNI-05 Well-formed child outcomes Safety Contract guard UNI-06 Per-child migration mint limit Safety Contract guard UNI-07 Branch-specific child REP Conservation Reviewed preservation UNI-08 Child theoretical supply snapshot Conservation Reviewed preservation UNI-09 Child REP supply coherence Conservation Reviewed preservation QST-01 Question registry and classifier coherence Safety Reviewed preservation AUTHORITY Portfolio identity and question admission Safety Contract guard and regression tests POOL-ACCOUNTING Separate capital, settlement and reporter custody Safety Contract guard and regression tests CAPITAL-HEALTH Owner commitments and loss isolation Safety Contract guard and regression tests CAPITAL-EXIT Retirement and withdrawal accounting Safety Contract guard and regression tests SHARE-SETTLEMENT Question shares and backing floor Safety Contract guard and regression tests ESCALATION Reporter liabilities and historical proofs Safety Contract guard and regression tests FORKS Shared fork choices and lazy questions Safety Contract guard and regression tests AUCTIONS Uniform branch auction and automatic owners Safety Contract guard and regression tests AUCTION-REFUNDS Bid and refund ownership Safety Contract guard and regression tests ORACLE Shared price acceptance and exposure Safety Contract guard and regression tests LIFECYCLE Bounded work and explicit recovery Safety Contract guard and regression tests EXTERNAL-CALLS Authority before external effects Safety Contract guard and regression tests","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"zoltar-invariants","heading":"Zoltar invariants","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"These properties govern core universe creation, REP supply, universe forks, deterministic child identities, and branch-specific REP migration.","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"universes","heading":"Universes, REP supply, and migration","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-01","heading":"UNI-01 One fork per universe","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property An initialized universe records at most one fork time and one fork question. Example: After a universe forks on question 17, a second call attempting to fork it on question 22 reverts. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard Primary evidence Zoltar.forkUniverse","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-02","heading":"UNI-02 Valid fork questions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property forkUniverse accepts only a question that exists in ZoltarQuestionData and whose end time has been reached ( block.timestamp >= endTime ). A pool may separately require the fork question to equal its pool question. Zoltar intentionally imposes no question-creation age, universe registration, or pool-relevance condition. Example: At exactly a known question's end time, a caller may fork a universe even when that question came from another universe or was created with an already-past end time; an unknown or still-open question is rejected. Requiring a universe-relevant or sufficiently aged question is not a Zoltar invariant. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard Primary evidence forkUniverse , question model , question-end fork boundary test Related assumption A15 intended question selection","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-03","heading":"UNI-03 Fork threshold burn and credit","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property The fork threshold is ⌈parent theoretical supply / forkThresholdDivisor⌉ for nonzero supply, and zero only when supply is zero. Starting a fork burns that amount of the initiator's parent REP, reduces the parent theoretical supply by the same amount, and credits the threshold minus ⌊threshold / forkBurnDivisor⌋ for migration. The configured forkBurnDivisor is at least 5 , so the uncredited fork haircut cannot exceed 20%. Example: With a 1,000 REP theoretical supply, threshold divisor 20, and burn divisor 5, the initiator commits 50 parent REP, pays a 10 REP fork haircut, and receives 40 REP of migration credit. Type Conservation Enforcement status Contract guard Primary evidence getForkThresholdAttoRep and forkUniverse , fork-threshold rounding test","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-04","heading":"UNI-04 Deterministic child universe IDs","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property A child universe identity is exactly the truncated hash of its parent universe and fork outcome; one parent-outcome pair maps to one child. Example: Repeatedly deriving the Yes child of the same parent returns the same universe ID, while deriving its No child returns a different ID. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard Primary evidence getChildUniverseId , deterministic child-ID test","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-05","heading":"UNI-05 Well-formed child outcomes","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Only well-formed outcomes of the recorded fork question can deploy child universes or receive migrated REP. Example: For a binary categorical fork question whose valid indexes are 0, 1, and 2, passing index 3 cannot deploy a child or receive child REP. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard Primary evidence deployChild and _splitRep , malformed-outcome rejection tests","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-06","heading":"UNI-06 Per-child migration mint limit","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property For one migrator and one child, cumulative child REP minted never exceeds that migrator's parent migration balance. Example: A migrator with 40 REP of migration credit cannot mint 25 REP and later mint another 20 REP into the same child. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard Primary evidence childMigrationRepAmountsAttoRep","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-07","heading":"UNI-07 Branch-specific child REP","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property One burned parent migration balance may mint the same credited amount into several selected children. Each minted balance belongs to a different child REP token, and migration never restores the burned parent REP. Example: A 40 REP migration credit may mint 40 Yes-child REP and 40 No-child REP, but neither balance is parent REP and the burned parent balance remains zero. Type Conservation Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence _splitRep , ReputationToken","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-08","heading":"UNI-08 Child theoretical supply snapshot","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property The child theoretical-supply snapshot equals the parent's theoretical supply after the threshold burn plus the initiator's migration credit net of the fork haircut. Each child therefore excludes the uncredited fork haircut. Example: If a fork removes 50 parent REP and credits 40 migration REP, each child's theoretical snapshot is 10 REP below the pre-fork supply. Type Conservation Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence childUniverseTheoreticalSupplySnapshotsAttoRep , common child supply snapshot test","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-09","heading":"UNI-09 Child REP supply coherence","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property For each child REP token, total minted supply equals the sum of holder balances, never exceeds the child's theoretical-supply snapshot, and uses the same theoretical maximum recorded by the child universe. Example: If two migrators mint 12 and 7 child REP, the child token supply is 19 REP, both balances sum to 19 REP, and minting does not change the child's theoretical maximum. Type Conservation Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence splitMigrationRep and child universe accounting , ReputationToken supply , aggregate child-supply invariant test","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"questions","heading":"Questions and answer classification","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"qst-01","heading":"QST-01 Question registry and classifier coherence","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Every successful question creation appends its deterministic ID exactly once and leaves prior question data and labels immutable. For every stored question and answer, the malformed classifier is true exactly when the public answer name is Malformed , except that a categorical question may use the literal label Malformed for a well-formed outcome. Example: Appending a scalar question after two categorical questions preserves the first two records and their order; a scalar answer with reserved bits is both classified and named malformed. Type Safety Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence createQuestion , getQuestions , and answer classification , registry and classifier invariant test","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"statoblast-invariants","heading":"Shared portfolio invariants","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"These properties apply to every portfolio, including fixed one-question portfolios. The module reference describes the corresponding accounting and authority boundaries.","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"authority","heading":"AUTHORITY Portfolio identity and question admission","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property The canonical factory namespace binds every question ledger to its portfolio. The immutable admission policy controls registration. A fixed one-question portfolio uses the same capital, settlement and reporting contracts as an open portfolio. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard and regression tests Primary evidence SecurityPoolPortfolio.sol","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"pool-accounting","heading":"POOL-ACCOUNTING Separate capital, settlement and reporter custody","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Provider REP and commitments belong to the capital ledger; question cash and outcome supply belong to the settlement book; reporter REP belongs to the reporter ledger. A reporting deposit does not acquire underwriting rights, and a trader purchase does not create a provider position. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard and regression tests Primary evidence SecurityPoolPortfolioSettlement.sol","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"capital-health","heading":"CAPITAL-HEALTH Owner commitments and loss isolation","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Each owner authorizes its commitment and health target. Health counts that owner’s free and pledged principal against its full commitment. A liquidation affects the target’s position; other owners do not absorb its penalty. Unvested REP income is excluded from principal health. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard and regression tests Primary evidence SecurityPoolCapitalLedger.sol","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"capital-exit","heading":"CAPITAL-EXIT Retirement and withdrawal accounting","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property An owner cannot withdraw principal needed by its live commitment or pending risk. Retirement immediately removes capacity available to new purchases while the settlement book tracks when the old demand becomes releasable. Owners do not claim separately for every question. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard and regression tests Primary evidence SecurityPoolCapitalLifecycleDelegate.sol","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"share-settlement","heading":"SHARE-SETTLEMENT Question shares and backing floor","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Each question has its own outcome shares and cash entitlement. Purchases check healthy aggregate capacity and the configured minimum backing floor. Complete-set returns release their demand; fees stop before settlement backing would fall below the floor. Finalized claims use their protected settlement state. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard and regression tests Primary evidence SecurityPoolSettlementBook.sol","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"escalation","heading":"ESCALATION Reporter liabilities and historical proofs","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Ordinary settlement fixes winning payout liabilities and required burns before paying individual claims. Inherited claims authenticate frozen Merkle-mountain-range membership and per-branch nullifiers. Consumed principal compacts exported principal intervals; reward coordinates remain unchanged. Replaying a consumed proof cannot pay again. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard and regression tests Primary evidence EscalationGameSettlement.sol","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"forks","heading":"FORKS Shared fork choices and lazy questions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Each owner records one branch choice before the fixed cutoff. Shared books snapshot the branch once; question ledgers and reporter snapshots are materialized on demand. Materialization cannot extend the native choice, auction or activation deadlines. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard and regression tests Primary evidence SecurityPoolPortfolioForkCoordinator.sol","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"auctions","heading":"AUCTIONS Uniform branch auction and automatic owners","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Only freshly unchosen provider REP is auction inventory; reporter escrow and previously owed recovery REP are excluded. Winners pay the same grid-rounded REP-per-lot price. Winning principal and obligations enter aggregate accounting at closing even if a bidder delays activating individual controls. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard and regression tests Primary evidence SecurityPoolUniformPriceAuction.sol","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"auction-refunds","heading":"AUCTION-REFUNDS Bid and refund ownership","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Early refund eligibility must be proven by the auction’s irreversible losing condition. Refund credits belong to the bidder and are withdrawn independently of winner activation. The auction cannot require an unbounded scan of losing bids before honest winners can act. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard and regression tests Primary evidence SecurityPoolUniformPriceAuction.sol","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"oracle","heading":"ORACLE Shared price acceptance and exposure","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Independent report candidates cannot reserve exclusive access. Acceptance checks price freshness, funded coverage and the portfolio reporting window and atomically checkpoints capital under the previous price. A rejected or disputed renewal cannot extend the accepted price’s expiry. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard and regression tests Primary evidence SharedPoolPriceFeed.sol","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"lifecycle","heading":"LIFECYCLE Bounded work and explicit recovery","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Capital and settlement operations use fixed-width indexes and aggregate accounting rather than scanning every owner or question. User-supplied claim batches are divisible. Deep inherited claims may require bounded checkpoint transactions, but do not force unrelated owners to process their history. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard and regression tests Primary evidence EscalationGameClaimDelegate.sol","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"external-calls","heading":"EXTERNAL-CALLS Authority before external effects","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Canonical module bindings and caller guards protect delegated accounting. Transaction failures roll back coupled state changes. External REP and oracle assumptions remain explicit in the security model; these invariants do not guarantee an honest market price or transaction inclusion. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard and regression tests Primary evidence SecurityPoolPortfolio.sol","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["assumptions","security","threats","guarantees"],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Statoblast and Zoltar enforce accounting, authorization, and lifecycle rules onchain. Their economic safety and practical liveness still depend on the participant, market, asset, deployment, client, data, Ethereum, and cryptographic assumptions below. An assumption is not a guarantee enforced by the contracts. Each entry says what must remain true, what can fail if it does not, and where the related mechanics are explained. The Statoblast and Zoltar invariants catalog lists contract-enforced properties and their implementation evidence. Participation and coordination Forks, truth, value, and liquidity Oracle correction, inclusion, and operational data Assets, clients, deployment, Ethereum, and cryptography Guarantees deliberately not made","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":1},{"fragment":"orientation","heading":"Security-model orientation","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"The assets and state at stake are REP , ETH settlement collateral , outcome shares , vault backing, Escalation Game deposits, child-universe accounting, and the integrity of event-derived state. Boundary Examples Trusted protocol components Zoltar, SecurityPools , share tokens, price coordinators , forkers , and their configured delegates. External dependencies OpenOracle , WETH/REP token behavior, Ethereum execution and finality, RPCs, wallets, and proof data. Untrusted actors Traders, vault owners, liquidators, disputers, bidders, settlers, keepers, and indexers. Failure outcomes Reverted calls, delayed transitions, underfunded child repair, stale prices, incorrect external data, or unreconstructable history. The assumptions below explain what must remain true for these assets and transitions to retain their intended safety or liveness properties; they are not additional contract guarantees.","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"security-review-orientation","heading":"Reviewing an operational concern","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"To classify an economic or liveness concern, find its external dependency in the grouped assumptions below and follow that entry's mechanics links. Contract-enforced properties are in the invariant catalog; fork-depth gas behavior, for example, is bounded lifecycle work . Operator work relies particularly on these assumptions: A06 lifecycle executors A15 intended question selection A20 chain-data and proof availability A22 asset-recipient compatibility A23 verified deployments A24 client, RPC, and wallet integrity A25 safe immutable parameters A26 Ethereum execution and finality A27 cryptographic security A28 account authority","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumptions-participation","heading":"Participation and coordination","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"These assumptions explain why someone performs the economically useful action instead of merely being permitted to perform it.","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a01","heading":"A01 Participants act on risk-adjusted economic incentives","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Enough participants prefer actions with greater expected value after gas, fees, delay, capital lockup, and risk. The model does not rely on altruistic defense. Failure boundary: A nominally profitable liquidation, dispute, bid, migration, or fork defense may not happen when its risk-adjusted return is unattractive. Mechanics: economic roles , liquidation incentives , auction clearing , and oracle incentives .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a02","heading":"A02 Required actions remain worth their operating costs","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"For each time-sensitive action, transaction fees, opportunity cost, coordination cost, and capital lockup remain small enough relative to the value protected or earned. Failure boundary: An action can be technically executable but economically abandoned before its deadline. Mechanics: Collateral Repair Auction lifecycle , oracle gas parameters , and Escalation Game lifecycle .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a03","heading":"A03 Each economic role has enough independent participants","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Trading, liquidation, escalation, migration, auction bidding, price correction, and settlement have enough non-colluding participants for the relevant action. Secondary-market trading is external to Statoblast. Failure boundary: Thin or controlled participation can remove price discovery, competitive bidding, or an opposing outcome deposit. Mechanics: economic roles , fork coordination , and auction participation .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a04","heading":"A04 Participants can mobilize REP to raise an alarm","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Market participants can mobilize enough REP, from wallet balances or withdrawable portfolio principal, to fund an early competing Invalid , Yes , or No deposit and keep the local contest active while additional capital reacts. Resumed, unresolved continuation games can accept wallet REP. Failure boundary: A wrong local outcome can become final before better-capitalized participants enter. Mechanics: escalation resolution , escalation accounting , and escalation guardrails .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a05","heading":"A05 Participants value continued access to the supported Statoblast lineage","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"The participants whose coordination determines durable use value continued access to the supported Statoblast lineage at least as highly as its configured statoblastSecurityMultiplierBps basis points of the value secured by that lineage. BPS_DENOMINATOR = 10_000 converts that stored value to its human multiplier. Value Statoblast access × BPS_DENOMINATOR ≥ statoblastSecurityMultiplierBps × Value secured Compare one lineage’s continued-use value and secured value at the same pre-attack time. Failure boundary: Participants may rationally abandon, grief, or coordinate away from a lineage whose continued-use value is too small. Mechanics: fork security , fork migration , and Statoblast security boundary .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a06","heading":"A06 At least one executor advances every required public transition","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"An adequately funded participant or service monitors and calls each needed permissionless transition, including funded takeovers, local escalation deposits and settlement, fork activation, child creation, vault and share migration, auction start and finalization, bid settlement, OpenOracle report settlement, settled-report callback recovery, branch finalization, and proof-based claims. Failure boundary: Permissionless state can remain stalled even though no contract authorization prevents progress. Mechanics: lifecycle liveness , fork migration guardrails , and oracle guardrails .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumptions-forks","heading":"Forks, truth, value, and liquidity","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"These assumptions connect onchain branching and accounting to offchain truth judgments, asset value, liquidity, and migration.","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a07","heading":"A07 A supported lineage preserves enough aggregate child value","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"For each protected Statoblast origin lineage, evaluate the parent and its child-pool continuations in one numeraire at the fork-security decision horizon, before long-run value has fully concentrated on one branch. The sum of traded child-lineage values remains large enough compared with that parent lineage’s pre-fork value under the origin’s immutable statoblastSecurityMultiplierBps . Each child asset is valued in its own branch; unrelated external collateral is not counted again. Value parent lineage ≤ ∑ child ∈ Lineage continuations Value child × BPS_DENOMINATOR statoblastSecurityMultiplierBps lineage Parent and child values use the same origin lineage, valuation source, numeraire, and decision horizon. For multiplier 20_000 BPS , aggregate child-lineage value must be at least twice parent-lineage value; multiplier 30_000 BPS requires three times that value. Failure boundary: Value destruction or fragmentation can make a malicious or unnecessary fork cheaper than the harm it causes. Mechanics: fork economics , fork coordination , and pool migration .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a08","heading":"A08 REP market capitalization approximates economic value","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"At the stated valuation time and system scope, REP market capitalization is a sufficiently accurate observable proxy for the discounted cash flow participants expect from REP. Failure boundary: Thin, manipulated, or speculative pricing can overstate the economic value securing open interest. Mechanics: REP economics and economic backing claims .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a09","heading":"A09 Users value the universes they judge truthful","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Users prefer the universe or universes they judge truthful, so economic activity and durable asset value concentrate there. Unlike A07’s fork-security decision horizon, this assumption describes the later, durable allocation of value after users coordinate. Value before fork ≈ Value truthful universe When several branches remain plausibly truthful, document the value split instead of assuming one winner. Failure boundary: Coordination on a false branch, or durable fragmentation across branches, defeats the value-concentration security argument. Mechanics: branch non-guarantees and pool fork migration .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a10","heading":"A10 Participants can access timely outcome evidence","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Users can obtain enough reliable real-world evidence before the relevant protocol deadline to judge which universe is truthful. Failure boundary: Missing, delayed, or conflicting evidence can split honest capital or let a false local outcome become final. Mechanics: question encoding , invalid outcomes , and local resolution .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a11","heading":"A11 Migration is operationally practical","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Users, exchanges, interfaces, custodians, and other integrations are willing and able to migrate assets and state into the child universe or universes they support during the applicable windows. Failure boundary: Supported branches can remain illiquid or undercollateralized because economic claims and backing do not move there. Mechanics: REP splitting , pool migration , and share migration guardrails .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a12","heading":"A12 Collateral Repair Auctions can convert supported child REP efficiently","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"When repair is needed, competitive Collateral Repair Auction entry can convert supported child REP into ETH at an acceptably small discount and operating cost. This is a participation assumption, not a contract guarantee of revenue or full repair. Failure boundary: Weak or strategically withheld demand leaves the child operational with impaired collateral. A shared portfolio auction can sell all fresh inventory for one funded lot. Ordinary surplus withdrawal and retirement remain available to winners, so selling the inventory does not guarantee permanent backing. Mechanics: shared portfolio auctions , and repair accounting .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a13","heading":"A13 REP is sufficiently liquid","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Participants can acquire or sell the required REP quantities without price impact or delay large enough to invalidate reporting, disputing, liquidation, auction, or migration economics. Failure boundary: Capital may exist in principle but cannot be assembled at a usable price before the deadline. Mechanics: economic roles , escalation , and oracle positions .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a14","heading":"A14 REP economic value exceeds the open interest it secures","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"For a stated pool, lineage, or system scope at one timestamp, open interest and REP discounted cash flow are valued in the same numeraire, and REP discounted cash flow remains strictly greater. For a pool-level evaluation, open interest means the pool’s protocol-accounted ETH collateral unless the analysis explicitly names a broader exposure measure. The REP supply and price basis must also be stated; market capitalization is the observable proxy in A08 . This external relationship is separate from each pool’s onchain multiplier-adjusted REP backing check. Open Interest < Discounted Cash Flow REP Record scope, timestamp, common numeraire, price source, REP supply basis, and open-interest definition when evaluating this strict inequality. Failure boundary: REP holders may rationally accept or cause losses larger than the durable REP value placed at risk. Mechanics: security pools , liquidation backing , and oracle exposure analysis .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a15","heading":"A15 Question selection remains aligned with user intent","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Interfaces and users verify the immutable question ID and metadata when selecting a market or pool, and the question has evidence and wording from which participants can reach an outcome or intentionally choose Invalid . Failure boundary: Unverified selection can route users and capital to an unintended market or pool, while ambiguous wording or unavailable evidence can split local outcome coordination. Mechanics: UNI-02 and question identity .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumptions-oracle","heading":"Oracle correction, inclusion, and operational data","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"These assumptions cover truthful REP/ETH price discovery, deadline access, capital, independent correction, and the offchain information required to act. OpenOracle's optional flexible-escalation and halt-only-fee flags can change a corrector's required capital and net incentive, so a deployment that enables them must evaluate the same assumptions against its configured flag set.","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a16","heading":"A16 Deadline transactions can obtain Ethereum inclusion","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Honest users can submit competitively priced Ethereum transactions and obtain canonical inclusion within every relevant dispute, escalation, migration, auction, and settlement window. Each onchain comparison assigns the exact deadline timestamp to one phase. Same-block ordering cannot move an action across that boundary: users must target the phase valid at the block timestamp rather than rely on transaction ordering to extend an expired window. Failure boundary: Censorship or congestion lasting through a deadline can finalize a wrong report or prevent a supported action. Mechanics: deadline invariants , auction windows , and oracle censorship model .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a17","heading":"A17 OpenOracle has an available, adequately capitalized corrector","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"At least one OpenOracle participant continuously monitors pending REP/ETH reports, can identify candidate errors using the reference market in A19 , can fund the scenario-dependent WETH and REP replacement position plus transaction costs, and can obtain inclusion before settlement. The participant's net incentive to perform the correction is the separate boundary in A18 . Failure boundary: An incorrect report can settle and authorize price-sensitive pool actions when no capable participant detects, funds, and includes a correction. Mechanics: REP/ETH oracle , correction incentives , and attack model .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a18","heading":"A18 At least one available corrector has an independent net incentive","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"At least one participant satisfying A17 is not under common control with the sponsor-funded coordinator reporting path, does not share the manipulation payoff, and is not bribed or compensated. The participant's net payoff from correcting is strictly greater than its net payoff from leaving the harmful report uncorrected. Two external submissions are not required for every request. Failure boundary: A sponsor-controlled, bribed, or economically indifferent corrector can make the contestable report path behave like a single trusted reporter. Mechanics: settlement validation scope , economic tradeoffs , and oracle invariants .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a19","heading":"A19 A robust reference market makes harmful REP/ETH errors correctable","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Independent participants can observe a manipulation-resistant reference REP/ETH price. Every price error large enough to unlock a security-relevant pool action also leaves a correction opportunity that remains profitable after actual gas, prevailing priority fees, OpenOracle fees, price impact, and required capital lockup. Failure boundary: A harmful deviation below the profitable correction threshold, or a manipulated/illiquid reference market, can settle without an economically rational challenge. Mechanics: report sizing , external-payoff model , and settlement limits .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a20","heading":"A20 Canonical chain data and proof construction remain available","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Participants can access reorg-aware Ethereum history, recognized contract addresses, OpenOracle report state, and the event data needed to construct carry and nullifier proofs before use. Failure boundary: A valid dispute, settlement, migration, or inherited claim can become practically unavailable even while its contract entrypoint remains live. Mechanics: event replay , carry proofs , and operator orientation .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumptions-technical","heading":"Assets, clients, deployment, Ethereum, and cryptography","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"These assumptions define the technical foundation below the protocol’s onchain guards.","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a21","heading":"A21 Genesis REP and configured WETH obey the required accounting model","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Genesis REP and configured WETH move exactly the requested base units, use 18 decimals, return supported ERC-20 values, and do not rebase, charge transfer fees, invoke unexpected callbacks, blacklist protocol contracts, pause required transfers, or permit unauthorized minting. WETH remains redeemable 1:1 for ETH, the genesis theoretical supply is accurate, and the hard-coded Constants.BURN_ADDRESS burn sink remains inaccessible. These are external assumptions on both networks, with one exception: on Sepolia the reviewed GenesisReputationToken enforces genesis REP issuance and transfer behavior, provided A23 verifies its deployed bytecode and wiring and A26 preserves Ethereum execution. Sepolia WETH is Uniswap's published contract, which the deployer verifies only by runtime-code hash. Canonical child REP is the reviewed ReputationToken contract and adds no external-token assumption. Failure boundary: Origin-pool, genesis-fork, or oracle accounting can overstate balances, backing, burned supply, or WETH value. Mechanics: bounded lifecycle work and oracle token pair .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a22","heading":"A22 Participant-controlled recipients can accept required asset deliveries","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Vaults, oracle sponsors, traders, share holders, and other caller-controlled recipients use addresses that accept native ETH when claiming fees, collateral, or proceeds. Collateral repair auction settlement instead credits bidder-specific pull escrow without calling the bidder; the bidder must accept ETH only when withdrawing that credit. Contract recipients also return the required ERC-1155 receiver selector when complete-set creation, share migration, or an outcome-share transfer delivers tokens to them. A rejected ETH pull or push reverts that transfer, and a rejected ERC-1155 callback reverts the mint or transfer and its surrounding protocol action. No protocol path can force a rejecting address to accept a later delivery or redirect every payout or mint. Failure boundary: A contract account that rejects ETH or the required ERC-1155 callback can strand its own payout or make its complete-set, migration, transfer, or claim action unavailable even while protocol-wide accounting remains safe. Mechanics: ETH callback safety , ERC-1155 callback safety , share-token receipt , auction refunds , oracle request refunds , and security pool guardrails .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a23","heading":"A23 Users select verified canonical deployments","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"The reviewed release is built reproducibly with its pinned compiler and toolchain configuration. The deterministic manifest freshness check rejects address drift, while the deployment plan verifies pinned runtime-code hashes for planned contracts and bootstrap descendants. Users and integrations must still verify post-deployment wiring, factory and forker provenance, pool lineage, question ID, and the manifest against the intended network. Constructor admission alone is not a safety proof. Failure boundary: Toolchain drift, unavailable or mismatched reviewed hashes, permissionless lookalike instances, wrong dependencies, or unintended lineage and question wiring can bypass the reviewed protocol even when the selected bytecode functions as configured. Mechanics: release posture , deployment status , compiler configuration , OpenOracle build provenance , and authority invariants .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a24","heading":"A24 Clients, RPCs, and wallets preserve user intent and canonical state","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Interfaces, indexers, RPC providers, and wallet software return canonical, reorg-aware state; construct and display the intended chain, target, calldata, parameters, and ETH value; and submit only transactions the user authorizes. Users verify transaction previews rather than treating an application label as an onchain guarantee. Failure boundary: Compromised or stale offchain software can misroute signatures or capital, hide deadlines or events, or cause users to authorize valid but unintended calls without violating contract guards. Mechanics: deployment discovery , contract interactions , and operator verification .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a25","heading":"A25 Immutable parameters preserve economic and executable security margins","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"The selected parameter relationships keep the fork threshold and fork haircut economically meaningful, the start bond affordable, the pool multiplier and the pool-held-REP branch multiplier derived from it ( capital health ) large enough for their distinct coverage roles, the oracle target error profitable to correct after fees and gas, dispute and settlement windows usable, and callback and transition gas executable. The Statoblast pool statoblastSecurityMultiplierBps is distinct from openOracleSecurityMultiplierBps . Failure boundary: Weak or internally inconsistent immutable parameters can defeat the economic or liveness argument even when the canonical contracts function exactly as configured. Mechanics: Statoblast pool parameters , fork economics , liquidation economics , oracle parameters , and oracle attack model .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a26","heading":"A26 Ethereum preserves the execution environment the protocol relies on","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Ethereum continues to provide correct EVM execution and atomic rollback, sufficient canonical finality, usable timestamp and block.basefee semantics, and block gas limits and gas schedules under which every mandatory immutable transition remains executable. Failure boundary: Deep reorganizations, invalid state execution, adversarial time behavior, or gas repricing can reverse accepted state or strand required transitions. Mechanics: contract interactions , atomicity and deadlines , and Escalation Game lifecycle .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a27","heading":"A27 Ethereum cryptographic primitives remain secure","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Keccak-256 remains collision- and preimage-resistant at the truncated widths used by the protocol, and CREATE2 identity remains sound. secp256k1 ECDSA signatures remain unforgeable with correct EOA signer recovery for Ethereum transactions and EIP-712 authorizations. Contract paths using SignatureValidation additionally rely on correct EOA or ERC-1271 contract-account signature validation; OpenOracle also relies on its fixed Permit2 singleton. OpenOracle fixes the Permit2 address, requires the exact permitted amount, sends the requested tokens to itself, and constructs the witness from the beneficiary, relayer, token owner, and intent. Permit2 defines the EIP-712 domain, deadline, permitted-amount ceiling, unordered-nonce replay protection, and EOA/ERC-1271 signature validation. Verification under A23 must cover the pinned Permit2 implementation at that fixed address. Failure boundary: Forged identities, colliding questions or universes, false carry proofs, forged EOA transactions, incorrect EOA recovery or ERC-1271 acceptance, or broken domain and nonce enforcement can violate otherwise-correct guards or authorize an unintended token transfer. Mechanics: universe identity , proof hashing , Permit2 deposit authorization , Permit2 signature interface , liquidation-approval signature validation , pinned Permit2 implementation provenance , and deterministic address guardrails .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a28","heading":"A28 Participants retain control of account authority","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Participants protect the private keys, signing devices, session permissions, and delegated ERC-20 or ERC-1155 approvals that authorize their accounts. Account recovery and custody arrangements do not give an unintended party effective control during a security-sensitive window. Failure boundary: A stolen key, compromised signer, or overbroad approval can authorize valid but unwanted transfers, deposits, bids, migrations, or configuration changes without violating a contract guard. Mechanics: transaction authority , security pool guardrails , and account-authority monitoring .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"excluded-guarantees","heading":"Guarantees deliberately not made","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"These outcomes remain possible without contradicting the security model. They should be included in user, operator, and integration risk disclosures. EG01. Zoltar creates valid branches but does not enforce that the fork question is relevant to a user, establish the objectively truthful branch, obtain user consent to a fork, or force users to coordinate on one branch. EG02. A Collateral Repair Auction may raise less than its repair target; the child then activates with migrated collateral plus accepted auction ETH. EG03. Shared portfolios bound ordinary commitment growth by the accepted report’s exposure limit and require a separate funded proof for additional growth. Settlement does not prove external price correctness. EG04. Shared portfolios isolate concurrent report escrows, but cannot guarantee that any particular disputed report settles within a bounded time. A participant with enough capital can keep correcting one candidate without blocking other independently funded candidates. EG05. The immutable protocol has no administrator who can pause, upgrade, roll back, or rescue an unsafe deployment after launch. EG06. Reporter escrow is separate from saleable auction inventory. A losing escalation claim pays its committed depositor nothing, and reporting claims remain subject to the game and fork rules; segregation does not guarantee a successful report or a particular REP value.","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["testnet","Sepolia","deploy","private key","spending limit"],"path":"how-to/deploy-testnet-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Install the deterministic infrastructure on Sepolia from GitHub Actions or a local shell.","text":"How-to guide The testnet deployer installs the complete deterministic Zoltar and Statoblast infrastructure in one run. It is safe to rerun: a contract already at its target address is skipped only when its runtime bytecode matches, and an unexpected contract at a target address stops the deployment.","title":"Deploy testnet contracts","topic":"Operations","weight":1},{"fragment":"before-you-deploy","heading":"Before you deploy","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/deploy-testnet-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Install the deterministic infrastructure on Sepolia from GitHub Actions or a local shell.","text":"Complete the repository setup in the README . Use a dedicated testnet account. To size its balance without deploying, run the deployer once with --max-total-cost-eth=0.000001 : its preflight aborts and the abort message reports the upper-bound cost of the missing steps. A normal run prints the same figure in its Estimated maximum cost log line. Use an HTTPS RPC endpoint. Loopback HTTP endpoints are accepted for local test networks. Make sure the deployer account has no pending transactions. If the testnet rejects the fixed raw legacy transactions that install the canonical CREATE2 deployer and the proxy deployer, make sure both are already present as predeploys. Sepolia is the default target (chain ID 11155111 ). A different testnet must support EIP-1559, the Cancun opcodes used by Zoltar and Uniswap V4, and the Osaka CLZ opcode used by the compiled contracts. The deployer rejects Ethereum mainnet (chain ID 1 ).","title":"Deploy testnet contracts","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"deploy-with-github-actions","heading":"Deploy with GitHub Actions","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/deploy-testnet-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Install the deterministic infrastructure on Sepolia from GitHub Actions or a local shell.","text":"Use the Deploy Testnet Contracts workflow for a deployment from main . Create a protected GitHub environment named testnet-deployment . Add the deployer's private key as the environment secret TESTNET_DEPLOYER_PRIVATE_KEY . Optionally add ETHERSCAN_API_KEY as well; without it post-deploy verification skips Etherscan. Open Actions → Deploy Testnet Contracts → Run workflow . Select main , complete the inputs, and enter DEPLOY as the confirmation. Review the job summary for each planned deployment's result and address. It includes transaction hashes for contracts deployed during the run. The job runs only when the workflow is started from main and the confirmation is exactly DEPLOY . Otherwise GitHub skips it without an error and no summary is produced; if the run shows a skipped job, check the branch and confirmation and start it again. Use a public RPC URL without credentials. Workflow inputs are stored in GitHub metadata and are not secret.","title":"Deploy testnet contracts","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"deploy-locally","heading":"Deploy locally","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/deploy-testnet-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Install the deterministic infrastructure on Sepolia from GitHub Actions or a local shell.","text":"Load PRIVATE_KEY from a secret manager or a hidden prompt. Never paste the key into a command, because the command may be saved in shell history. In Bash: read -rsp 'Testnet deployer private key: ' PRIVATE_KEY \\ && echo && export PRIVATE_KEY Run the deployer with an explicit RPC endpoint and spending limits: bun run deploy:testnet -- \\ --rpc-url=https://rpc.example \\ --chain-id=11155111 \\ --max-fee-per-gas-nanoeth=100 \\ --max-total-cost-eth=20 Remove the key from the shell when the command finishes: unset PRIVATE_KEY The deployer reads the exported PRIVATE_KEY automatically. You can instead pass --private-key=0x... directly, but then the complete command, and therefore the key, may be saved in shell history. Run bun run deploy:testnet -- --help for all options. Options other than --private-key also accept uppercase arguments after -- or environment variables. Input Default Purpose RPC_URL / --rpc-url Required RPC endpoint for the target network CHAIN_ID / --chain-id 11155111 Expected decimal chain ID MAX_FEE_PER_GAS_NANO_ETH / --max-fee-per-gas-nanoeth 100 Rejects higher RPC fee suggestions. If a canonical deployer is missing, the run aborts when this is below 100 , the fixed gas price of the deployers' raw transactions. MAX_TOTAL_COST_ETH / --max-total-cost-eth 20 Caps the conservative preflight estimate and transaction budget PRIVATE_KEY / --private-key Required 0x -prefixed 32-byte deployer key The defaults are authorization limits, not a spend forecast or a required balance. Before sending a transaction, the command checks the RPC chain ID, EVM features, EIP-1559 support, canonical deployer compatibility, expected bytecode, and fee limits. It then estimates only the missing deployment steps. If the conservative estimate exceeds MAX_TOTAL_COST_ETH , it exits; on a live network nothing has been funded or deployed at that point, while an Anvil development node may already have received the replayed Uniswap contracts. Per-transaction checks enforce the same budget while the deployment runs. The run logs each planned contract with Status: deployed , Status: already deployed , or Status: ready (installed without a submitted transaction), then verifies the bootstrap support contracts. A step that fails logs Status: failed and stops the run. When deployment succeeds, the command runs source verification for the target chain. Only Sepolia ( 11155111 ) is verified; any other chain logs that it has no known explorer support and succeeds. Verification submits the addresses in the tracked Yes and No manifests, docs/sepolia-deployment-addresses.json and docs/sepolia-no-deployment-addresses.json , not addresses discovered during the run. Blockscout and Sourcify run without a key; Etherscan runs only when ETHERSCAN_API_KEY is set and is otherwise skipped with a message. A verification failure makes the command exit nonzero even though the contracts are deployed , and prints the command to retry on its own: bun run verify:contracts -- --chain-id=11155111 . Treat a nonzero exit as \"check which stage failed\" rather than \"nothing was deployed\".","title":"Deploy testnet contracts","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"recover-an-interrupted-run","heading":"Recover an interrupted run","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/deploy-testnet-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Install the deterministic infrastructure on Sepolia from GitHub Actions or a local shell.","text":"Wait for all pending transactions to settle, then rerun the same command. The deployer revalidates completed contracts and resumes with the first missing step.","title":"Deploy testnet contracts","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"what-gets-deployed","heading":"What gets deployed","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/deploy-testnet-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Install the deterministic infrastructure on Sepolia from GitHub Actions or a local shell.","text":"separate Yes and No genesis REP tokens, each with the reviewed allocations the canonical CREATE2 deployer, the proxy deployer, and Permit2 a deterministic Uniswap V3 SwapRouter on chains other than Sepolia, deterministic WETH and a complete Uniswap deployment (V3 factory, QuoterV2, V4 PoolManager, and V4 Quoter) Zoltar and Statoblast deployments and their bootstrap support contracts for both genesis outcomes a Trading factory and router for each genesis outcome, at the canonical 0.30% fee The command does not create Uniswap pools or add liquidity; protocol factories create market-specific contracts later, when those features are used. Uniswap sourcing per chain is listed in the contract inventory , and the deployment status reference explains how clients read which steps are installed. The bots use the Yes deployment in the tracked mainnet and Sepolia manifests, so on testnets they work only against Sepolia and Sepolia replays such as a local Anvil node with chain ID 11155111 . A deployment to another testnet is not usable by the bots until that chain has a generated manifest.","title":"Deploy testnet contracts","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["release","launch","checklist","genesis REP","manifest"],"path":"how-to/launch-a-release.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"The pre-tag checklist, runtime-hash review, and the testnet genesis REP review.","text":"How-to guide Follow this guide before tagging a Zoltar and Statoblast release. It lists the checks that must pass before the tag and, for Sepolia and deterministic testnets, the genesis REP allocation review. Mainnet uses Augur V2's existing REP Yes and REP No tokens and deploys no genesis REP tokens.","title":"Prepare a protocol release","topic":"Operations","weight":1},{"fragment":"immutable-protocol-release-posture","heading":"Know what a release cannot do","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/launch-a-release.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"The pre-tag checklist, runtime-hash review, and the testnet genesis REP review.","text":"The contracts are immutable: no operator can pause, upgrade, roll back, or rescue them after deployment, so a release publishes verifiable provenance instead (commit and tag, deterministic addresses, CI and QA results, production UI artifact hash, known limitations) and never describes a UI deployment as an emergency control. See EG05 .","title":"Prepare a protocol release","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"launch-release-checklist","heading":"Run the release checklist","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/launch-a-release.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"The pre-tag checklist, runtime-hash review, and the testnet genesis REP review.","text":"From a fresh dependency install ( bun install --frozen-lockfile ), run bun run check:mainnet-deployment:generated . It fails when any of the four generated deployment manifests is stale. If so, regenerate them with bun ./tooling/contracts/check-mainnet-deployment.mts --write . Review the Yes manifests for mainnet and Sepolia , and the No manifests for mainnet and Sepolia , then rerun the check. Run bun run ui:build:prod . It repeats the manifest check, so it fails if a manifest is still stale. Confirm the build used the pinned compiler configuration in solidity/ts/compile.ts , the exact tool versions in solidity/package.json , and the imported OpenOracle profile in UPSTREAM.md . Review the pinned runtime-code hashes: the EXPECTED_*_RUNTIME_CODE_HASHES maps in ui/zoltarShared/ts/protocol/deployment.ts and ui/statoblastShared/ts/protocol/deployment.ts . Check them in three ways, then publish the reviewed hashes with the release: Run bun ./tooling/testing/bun-test.mts --preload ./bun-test-setup-ui.ts ui/zoltar/ts/tests/protocol/deployment.test.ts ui/statoblast/ts/tests/protocol/deployment.test.ts . It confirms that a clean local build still matches the pinned hashes of the directly compiled steps (the deployment status oracle, Multicall3, ZoltarQuestionData, and five Statoblast artifacts); it does not cover the other pinned contracts. On Sepolia, rerun bun run deploy:testnet . It checks the runtime code of every installed contract against its pinned hash. It is not read-only: it needs PRIVATE_KEY , deploys any missing step, and then runs explorer verification. For any other deployed address, compare the keccak-256 hash of its eth_getCode result with the pinned hash by hand before marking the release canonical. Push the final tag only after the CI gate succeeds on the same commit. The tag must match v<number> (for example v12 ); the Version deploy workflow rejects any other form. That workflow reruns CI, builds the InterPlanetary File System (IPFS) publisher image, reads the content identifier (CID) from that image, checks only that it has the bafy… form, and creates or updates the GitHub release with that CID in its notes. Confirm the release exists and records the CID before announcing. In the production UI release notes, state that ?simulate=1 is a browser-local sandbox. Treat a stale, unavailable, or unsupported live quote as a blocker for the affected client action; never substitute a simulation price for it.","title":"Prepare a protocol release","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"genesis-rep-deployment","heading":"Review genesis REP allocations (Sepolia and deterministic testnets)","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/launch-a-release.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"The pre-tag checklist, runtime-hash review, and the testnet genesis REP review.","text":"Sepolia genesis REP allocations are canonical source data in sepoliaRepAllocations.ts . Each of the Yes and No tokens mints the same allocations. The configured 11 million REP mint cap per token is divided equally among the listed holders; adding a holder automatically reduces every holder's allocation. Integer division may leave the total minted supply below the cap by less than one attoREP per holder. Review both the holder list and the computed total before deployment. The constructor of GenesisReputationToken rejects an empty list, a zero address, a repeated holder, a zero balance, and a total above the cap. There is no administrator, post-deployment mint, allocation correction, or recovery entrypoint, so fix an incorrect allocation in the source list before deployment and then regenerate the deterministic manifests. Because constructor arguments are part of CREATE2 init code, any allocation change produces a new genesis REP address and new addresses for every deployment step whose constructor wiring depends on it. Never continue a deployment from a manifest generated for a different allocation list.","title":"Prepare a protocol release","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["monitoring","assumptions","operators","signals"],"path":"how-to/monitor-security-assumptions.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Check the assumptions behind an action: what to read, how often, and how to respond when one is weak.","text":"How-to guide Check that the economic and liveness assumptions behind a deadline-bearing or price-sensitive action still hold before you take it, and keep checking until its last deadline. The contracts cannot tell you when such an assumption has stopped holding.","title":"Monitor security assumptions","topic":"Operations","weight":1},{"fragment":"how-to-use","heading":"Check an action's assumptions","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/monitor-security-assumptions.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Check the assumptions behind an action: what to read, how often, and how to respond when one is weak.","text":"List the assumptions the action depends on. The security model lists which assumptions protect which mechanism. Find the rows for those assumptions in the table below . Take each row's reads for the same lineage , pool, and block as the action. Express the values in one numeraire and cover the action horizon . If a read is weak or unavailable, apply the row's response before proceeding. Repeat the reads on the row's cadence until the action's last deadline has passed. Most on-chain reads are also indexed by augurScan under /operations/… (see the augurScan README ). The liquidator and OpenOracle arbitrager bots in bots/ show live pool, vault, and report state in their dashboards.","title":"Monitor security assumptions","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"terms","heading":"Terms used in the table","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/monitor-security-assumptions.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Check the assumptions behind an action: what to read, how often, and how to respond when one is weak.","text":"Numeraire The one unit of account, such as ETH, in which you express every value you compare. Action horizon The period until the action's last deadline. Corrector An account that disputes an incorrect OpenOracle report; see A17 . Callback-recovery candidate A price coordinator whose pending report OpenOracle has already settled; see settlement recovery . Overflow operation A staged operation outside the next settlement callback's batch of four; see execution batches .","title":"Monitor security assumptions","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-monitoring","heading":"Reads, cadence, and response for each assumption","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/monitor-security-assumptions.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Check the assumptions behind an action: what to read, how often, and how to respond when one is weak.","text":"Assumptions What to read Review cadence Response when the boundary is weak or unknown Executor incentives and participation — A01, A02, A03, A06 Liquidation candidates and net reward: liquidator bot dashboard Dispute opportunities and net reward: OpenOracle arbitrager dashboard Active reporters, correctors, escalation depositors, and Collateral Repair Auction bidders: augurScan /operations/reports , /operations/escalations , /operations/auctions Callback-recovery candidates: coordinator pendingReportId , then the settlement timestamp in OpenOracle storedGame(reportId) ; anyone can clear one with recoverSettledPendingReport() Overflow operations: coordinator getActiveStagedOperationCount() minus getPendingSettlementOperationCount() Continuously for deadline-bearing flows; before launch and after parameter or gas-regime changes Fund or arrange independent executors, reduce exposed value, or disclose that the affected transition may stall Fork lineage value and migration readiness — A05, A07, A09, A11 Fork start: Zoltar getForkTime(universeId) Migration progress: augurScan /operations/forks Parent and child REP prices: market quotes in one numeraire Custodian, exchange, interface, and proof-tool readiness: each party's published support statement (no on-chain read) Before a fork-security claim and throughout migration and coordination windows Do not claim fork deterrence, practical migration, or value concentration; narrow supported lineage and exposure REP value and liquidity versus secured exposure — A08, A13, A14 Theoretical REP supply : Zoltar getUniverseTheoreticalSupplyAttoRep(universeId) Pool open interest : settlementCollateralAttoEth in getPoolAccountingSnapshot() Pool-held REP: getTotalPoolHeldAttoRep() Cached REP/ETH price: coordinator lastPrice REP depth and price impact: arbitrager Uniswap venue quotes Pool and vault risk: augurScan /operations/risk REP discounted cash flow: off-chain valuation (no on-chain read) At launch, continuously while open interest exists, and after material price, supply, liquidity, or exposure changes Cap or stop new exposure in clients and disclosures; do not describe REP backing as economically sufficient Outcome evidence and escalation funding — A04, A10 Escalation Game deadline: getEscalationGameEndDate() Outcome funding: getOutcomeBalancesAttoRep() and getBindingCapitalAttoRep() , or augurScan /operations/escalations REP available to fund a correction: wallet REP balanceOf (wallet deposits, except in a game carried across a fork) and vault REP backingUnitsToAttoRep(securityVaults(vault).repBackingUnits) of each ready account Outcome evidence: where each source publishes (off-chain) From question creation through local resolution and any fork window Mark the market or action impaired, surface the deadline, and avoid asserting that honest local correction remains fundable Collateral Repair Auction demand — A12 Collateral Repair Auction bids: auction engine() , then engine bidCount() , bids(index) , and previewFinalization(maximumFilledLots) , or augurScan /operations/auctions REP/ETH depth and expected discount: arbitrager Uniswap venue quotes Bidder gas cost: RPC eth_feeHistory Before and during every repair auction Disclose likely incomplete repair and plan operation of the child at only migrated collateral plus accepted auction ETH Transaction inclusion and Ethereum execution — A16, A26 Base and priority fees: RPC eth_feeHistory Block gas limit: latest block Reorg history: augurScan /operations/integrity Escalation deadline: getEscalationGameEndDate() Collateral Repair Auction close: auction closesAt() Migration close: forker forkData(pool) forkActivationTime plus eight weeks Margin: each deadline minus expected inclusion delay Continuously while a deadline or mandatory transition is live Raise transaction fees where rational, use independent submission paths, and disclose missed-deadline or stranded-transition risk REP/ETH price correction — A17, A18, A19 Pending report: coordinator pendingReportId , then OpenOracle storedGame(reportId) Report outcomes: coordinator PriceRequested , PriceReported , and PriceReportRejected events, or augurScan /operations/reports Independent correctors: whether any, such as an operator running the OpenOracle arbitrager , reports disputes profitable after fees, gas, and price impact Corrector inventory: wrapped Ether (WETH) and REP balances Continuously while a report is pending or a cached price can authorize operations Block or discourage new price-sensitive operations and disclose that an incorrect report may settle Token behavior and recipient compatibility — A21, A22 Sepolia genesis REP code: keccak-256 of eth_getCode against reputationToken in EXPECTED_SEPOLIA_DEPLOYMENT_RUNTIME_CODE_HASHES ( ui/zoltarShared/ts/protocol/deployment.ts ) Sepolia WETH code: the same against sepoliaPublished.weth.runtimeCodeHash in scripts/artifacts/uniswap-deployment.json Mainnet REPv2 and WETH code: an externally sourced reference hash (no repository pin) Sepolia genesis REP supply: shared/zoltar/ts/deployment/sepoliaRepAllocations.ts against getTotalTheoreticalSupply() WETH redeemability and recipient ETH and ERC-1155 acceptance: a test transfer from a disposable account At deployment verification and before first use of a new recipient or integration Reject the deployment or incompatible recipient; do not rely on redirected recovery Deployments, clients, data, and keys — A20, A23, A24, A27, A28 Manifest freshness: bun run check:mainnet-deployment:current Explorer source verification: bun run verify:contracts -- --chain-id=<chain ID> Code presence: getDeploymentMask() Runtime hashes: release checklist Event replay: a second RPC or augurScan /operations/integrity Signers, typed-data domains, nonces, and approvals: each operator's key store At release, on every environment or account change, and continuously for indexers Stop signing or operating, reject an unverifiable deployment, revoke compromised authority where possible, and re-establish canonical state from independent sources Question selection — A15 Question creation, end date, and labels: ZoltarQuestionData questionCreatedTimestamp , getQuestionEndDate , and getOutcomeLabels Evidence plan and pool relevance: off-chain review Before pool selection Reject or warn on the selection; do not represent an admitted question as relevant or resolvable Immutable parameters — A25 Deployed configuration: manifest protocolConfig Fork and non-decision thresholds: Zoltar getForkThresholdAttoRep(universeId) and getNonDecisionThresholdAttoRep(universeId) Oracle costs: coordinator getRequestPriceCostAttoEth() and getSettlementCallbackGasLimit() Required relationships: A25 Before deployment and after any change in external gas or market conditions used by the model Treat the immutable deployment as unsupported; there is no administrative parameter repair","title":"Monitor security assumptions","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["operators","guardrails","recovery","edge cases"],"path":"reference/operator-guardrails.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"What each subsystem will and will not accept, mapped to contract sources.","text":"Reference What a Statoblast security pool , share migration, Escalation Game , fork migration, Collateral Repair Auction , or REP/ETH price coordinator will and will not accept, one subsystem at a time. Statoblast is the prediction-market layer built on the Zoltar fork oracle; see the system overview . Every rule names the contract source that enforces it, so operators, indexers, and interface maintainers can check it against the exact Solidity. The invariant catalog connects these local guardrails to cross-contract conservation, liveness, and the stated economic assumptions; the security model is normative for external assumptions. Release procedure lives in Prepare a protocol release , assumption monitoring in Monitor security assumptions , and the remaining support contracts in the contract inventory .","title":"Subsystem guardrails","topic":"Safety","weight":1},{"fragment":"security-pool-guardrails","heading":"Portfolio capital operations","keywords":[],"path":"reference/operator-guardrails.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"What each subsystem will and will not accept, mapped to contract sources.","text":"Every question belongs to a shared portfolio. Verify the factory registration, capital ledger, settlement module and universe before sending funds. Deposits add principal; setting a commitment separately authorizes risk. Withdrawals and retirement check current owner principal and demand. Complete-set purchases use the registered portfolio route and recheck fees, available healthy capacity and backing floor. The Statoblast portfolio view includes Liquidation takeover . Its transaction uses takeOverWithBounds : the receiver supplies a minimum REP award and maximum own REP, total principal and total commitment. Price epoch, health target and retirement cohort must still match. The award stays pledged; it is not an immediately withdrawable keeper payout. The preliminary cure can make a quoted takeover unnecessary or unavailable. See the canonical module and accounting reference and the contract entrypoints .","title":"Subsystem guardrails","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"share-migration","heading":"Share migration","keywords":[],"path":"reference/operator-guardrails.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"What each subsystem will and will not accept, mapped to contract sources.","text":"Share tokens retain one question lineage and separate outcome balances per universe. A forked source uses its canonical child question. Prepare the shared branch and materialize a required child question before migrating shares. Finalized claims retain protected source settlement; do not assume every source claim must migrate. See the canonical module and accounting reference and the contract entrypoints .","title":"Subsystem guardrails","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"escalation-resolution-and-deposits","heading":"Reporting and settlement","keywords":[],"path":"reference/operator-guardrails.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"What each subsystem will and will not accept, mapped to contract sources.","text":"For reports through the question ledger, approve wallet REP to that ledger; direct deposits into an existing Escalation Game require approval to the game. The accepted deposit enters separate reporter custody and never becomes provider capital. Finalized local claims use deposit indexes; inherited claims use authenticated frozen-leaf proofs. Payouts go to recorded owners. Batches can be split, and deep lineage proofs use bounded preparation transactions. See the canonical module and accounting reference and the contract entrypoints .","title":"Subsystem guardrails","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"fork-migration","heading":"Shared forks","keywords":[],"path":"reference/operator-guardrails.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"What each subsystem will and will not accept, mapped to contract sources.","text":"Owners choose one branch before the native cutoff. The shared coordinator snapshots and prepares each chosen branch once, then lazy owner activation and question materialization apply that state. A missing keeper cannot extend choice or auction deadlines. Native migration still requires inclusion during its finite window. See the canonical module and accounting reference and the contract entrypoints .","title":"Subsystem guardrails","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"collateral-repair-auction-operations","heading":"Collateral repair auctions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/operator-guardrails.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"What each subsystem will and will not accept, mapped to contract sources.","text":"The branch auction sells fresh unchosen provider REP against its aggregate ETH shortfall. Buyers bid ETH lots with minimum REP-per-lot limits and their health targets. Winning backing and commitments enter aggregate accounting immediately at closing. Activating a winner enables individual controls; it is not required to count the winner’s obligation. Losing-bid credits and refunds remain independently withdrawable. See the canonical module and accounting reference and the contract entrypoints .","title":"Subsystem guardrails","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"repeth-oracle-operations","heading":"REP/ETH price reports","keywords":[],"path":"reference/operator-guardrails.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"What each subsystem will and will not accept, mapped to contract sources.","text":"The shared feed accepts independent candidates. A pending report cannot reserve a sponsor lane or block unrelated reports. Sponsor funding and public settlement bounties are separate from provider capital. Capital transactions execute directly and recheck the accepted price, owner authorization, current health and exposure. There is no coordinator operation queue or liquidation approval registry. See the canonical module and accounting reference and the contract entrypoints .","title":"Subsystem guardrails","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["inventory","factories","delegates","SecurityPoolUtils","trust boundary"],"path":"reference/contract-inventory.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Every production Solidity source by area, the caller and trust boundaries of deployment helpers and delegate modules, SecurityPoolUtils previews, and testnet Uniswap dependencies.","text":"Reference This page lists every production Solidity source by area. The contract interaction reference documents the entrypoints of the contracts users and keepers call; this page adds the caller and trust boundaries of the deployment helpers, delegate modules, and libraries that reference does not cover, the SecurityPoolUtils previews, and the Uniswap contracts a testnet deployment uses.","title":"Contract inventory","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"caller-and-trust-boundaries","heading":"Caller and trust boundaries","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contract-inventory.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Every production Solidity source by area, the caller and trust boundaries of deployment helpers and delegate modules, SecurityPoolUtils previews, and testnet Uniswap dependencies.","text":"Deployment helpers are not interchangeable with canonical protocol discovery. Several are intentionally permissionless and bind the caller into each deployed address, through a caller-namespaced salt or the caller in the init code; an application must discover canonical instances through SecurityPoolFactory , recognized security pools , and recognized forker events rather than treating every factory-created address as canonical. Module Direct caller boundary Canonical-use boundary SecurityPoolPortfolio An immutable admission policy, question horizon, economic configuration and canonical configuration seed define the portfolio. After construction, four permissionless prepareNext() transactions initialize settlement, reporter/fork modules, capital, then the shared feed and activation. setupStage == 4 is required before accepting user funds or risk. Anyone may register an allowed, open question in the unforked origin universe. getPool(questionId, universeId) resolves the registered lineage through the configured factory. Each universe has one capital ledger and one shared REP/ETH feed. The central settlement book owns question cash and supply accounting; the capital ledger owns each provider’s REP and commitments; the reporter ledger separately owns report escrow. Native question contracts expose shares and reporting. Verify the factory and module bindings independently. See the portfolio model . Question admission policies ISecurityPoolQuestionAdmissionPolicy.isQuestionAllowed(portfolio, universeId, questionId) is a read-only approval check. SecurityPoolOpenAdmissionPolicy always approves. SecurityPoolFixedAdmissionPolicy(universeId, questionIds) fixes a nonempty, duplicate-free list at construction with no mutation authority. Its paginated getter returns at most 64 entries. Policy approval is required for registration and purchase, including reuse of a registered question. Denial or a failed policy call does not block exits, claims or fork recovery. The UI offers the open and fixed policies; the contract API permits other policies. Portfolio economic configuration EconomicsConfig fixes purchaseFeeBps (at most 10,000), annualFeeBps , minimumPurchaseFeeAttoEth , positive minimumCommitmentAttoEth , minimumOutcomeRate , positive rewardVestingSeconds . Read these immutable settings from the portfolio’s corresponding getters. The security multiplier comes from the canonical seed. Each provider selects its own health target through setHealthTarget . Raising it on a live position requires a fresh price and sufficient backing. depositAndSetHealthTarget(amount, targetHealthFactorBps) adds REP and updates the target atomically, applying the same backing check to live target increases. Lowering a target does not increase commitment. The protocol’s liquidation requirement remains unchanged. SecurityPoolCapitalLedger : provider accounts depositFor(owner, amount) is callable only by that owner and adds REP to their entire live position, or to idle principal when they have no commitment. setCommitment(commitment) sets the owner’s whole ETH commitment K, backed by all deposited REP C. Without a pending retirement request, raising or keeping K applies at once, subject to a fresh price and the owner’s health target; a raise must also fit the accepted report’s funded exposure limit. setCommitmentWithReport(reportId, commitment) can instead prove coverage for a raise using an independently settled, fresh report. Its conservative collateral floor stays attached to the owner until the next canonical price epoch, which must cover actual aggregate exposure. Each owner has at most one live position in a portfolio universe, active or retiring, with exact C/K amounts. ownerCollateral(owner) returns deposited principal, commitment and currently withdrawable principal, excluding ETH and REP earnings. The chosen health target controls authorization, takeover receipt and pledged-principal withdrawal. Liquidation uses the protocol floor, whose security multiplier is at least 10,500 basis points. SecurityPoolCapitalLifecycleDelegate Each capital ledger creates its own immutable delegate for authorization, owner withdrawals, earnings claims and fork-choice export, import and backing release. It shares the ledger’s storage layout through SecurityPoolCapitalState . Mutations execute in the bound ledger’s context; direct mutation calls and delegate calls from another host revert. The ledger’s entrypoints retain caller permissions, reentrancy guards and price/checkpoint requirements. Principal and earnings stay in the ledger. Preparation reports cache prices without starting imported positions’ health history. finishForkImport seals the funded imports and records a health observation only if the cached price is still valid. The delegate’s ledger() getter identifies its sole host. Principal withdrawal and retirement withdrawPrincipal(amount, recipient) withdraws idle principal without a price requirement. A live position must be unforked and have a fresh price; its remaining REP must cover K at ownerConservativePrice(owner) , the protocol multiplier and the saved health target. That price includes an unexpired growth-proof floor when present. Lowering K with setCommitment at a fresh price first cures an underwater position at once and queues the rest of the reduction for retirement; at a stale price the whole reduction is queued. Zero requests a full exit; a nonzero residual and the queued reduction must each meet minimumCommitmentAttoEth . The owner cannot change or cancel a pending request through setCommitment : until it is served, setCommitment accepts only the pending value, which leaves the request in place and cures an underwater position at a fresh price. serveRetirement(0) serves the closed current cohort before the next. Full-exit subtrees close lazily; it updates at most one partial-retirement boundary per call. serveRetirement(positionId) completes that position’s entire queued reduction only when spare healthy capacity also covers all healthy queued reductions ahead of it; otherwise it makes no progress. This avoids processing other owners individually without spending their reserved capacity. Partial retirement retains all C and updates that position’s health key once. On reaching the requested K, that position returns to active status. The request does not change with prices. Full retirement releases C, settled lazily by its owner. previewRetirement(positionId, acceptedPrice, demand) on the commitment index returns a status, effective current cohort, healthy and spare commitment, queued reductions ahead, requested reduction and releasable REP. It is advisory: the caller supplies price and demand, and execution checkpoints fees first. A ready result does not guarantee progress at inclusion. New purchases can use healthy active commitments and the retained portion of healthy pending partial requests, without consuming queued reductions or clearing every request. Purchases, redemptions and earnings createCompleteSet(universeId, questionId, cashAttoEth, recipient, minimumShares) requires admission and fresh healthy capacity. Every purchase pays the configured backing-based fee, including capacity reuse, plus a prepaid resolution premium. purchaseQuote(universeId, questionId, cash) returns the complete fee and its premium component; purchaseFee(cash) returns only the backing-based part. Capacity must cover purchase cash. Ordinary holding fees continue past calendar end until reporting starts or funded units exhaust. Excess ETH is refunded after accounting and share delivery. Redeeming complete sets reduces that question’s cash and aggregate backing demand; the purchase fee is not refunded. Released capacity can serve retiring providers without a claim for every question. Earned fees accrue through aggregate indices; the backing-based purchase fee and reporter surplus vest over the configured duration. A healthy position earns its fraction of total K, including unhealthy K in the denominator. Unassigned ETH and REP are burned. claimEarnings collects the owner’s ETH and REP earnings. SecurityPoolCapitalFeeStreams Only the bound capital ledger may mutate this accounting module; asset custody remains in that ledger. addStream(ethAmount, repAmount) funds a schedule after checkpointing, using the immutable rewardDuration . Equal-second additions share an endpoint; later funding appends a cumulative checkpoint. Lookup excludes released history and searches at most 48 timestamp bits, with the live range bounded by the duration. checkpoint(eligibleThrough) separates releases before and after the eligible cutoff. The ledger immediately consumes takeDeferred() : stale ETH and REP are burned. It never restarts those amounts for future providers. fundedOutstanding() returns scheduled, unreleased income. Forks preserve existing dates and credit eligible frozen parent commitments when the price was fresh at the fork; unassigned parent income burns. Principal and earned claims are excluded. SecurityPoolPortfolioServices and SecurityPoolResolutionPremiumBook The service module owns prepaid resolution premiums. It holds no shared budget for keepers or price sponsors. Each question earns its premium only during its funded unresolved interval. A persistent expiry index removes earned units and burns the unused terminal remainder without scanning questions, because the book does not record which buyer paid it. Changes checkpoint earned income under the capital ledger’s old weights. Reporting updates replace only the affected question’s schedule. Forks copy the schedule roots and divide funded cash by chosen commitment; they do not replenish it. Before imports become operational, the fork coordinator calls checkpointPreparationPremium(child) to burn elapsed inactive premium and any unused terminal remainder. The portfolio authenticates native pools, reporters and accepted price reports. The service module may credit already-earned capital fees, fund vesting streams and synchronize demand; it cannot change the accepted price or withdraw provider principal. Personal liquidation and bounded accounting Apart from a takeover, only the owner cures, through setCommitment ; takeOver(positionId, commitment, minimumAward, maximumOwnRep) transfers a selected liability to a healthy, consenting replacement. takeOverWithBounds adds maximum final receiver principal and commitment, and requires the quoted receiver health target, accepted price epoch and source cohort. These bounds are checked after fee checkpointing and cure, within the same transaction. A receiver’s existing live position must belong to the source cohort; a receiver with no commitment inherits that cohort. The target’s REP funds takeover awards. No ETH deposit or execution reimbursement is required. A takeover first cures its target, and a cure pays no one. A cure removes the deficit, rounded up, so the remaining commitment is fully backed at the liquidation price. It never cuts below the owner’s new value. Removal is at least minimumCommitmentAttoEth unless it closes the position, never exceeds spare commitment above demand, and never leaves a queued partial reduction below that minimum. Because it removes only unhealthy commitment, it uses none of the healthy spare that retirements wait for. When spare is too small, the position stays unhealthy and eligible for takeover. Takeovers retain their time-dependent REP award, which becomes backing for the receiver’s assumed commitment. A partially taken-over private position retains every unawarded REP behind its remaining K. Admission, fee eligibility and surplus withdrawals use price / (1 − error) , rounded up; cure, takeover and adverse observations use price / (1 + error) , rounded down, where error is the configured oracle dispute target. An owner’s health setting remains separate. Other providers’ C/K balances are unchanged by a target liquidation. Balanced trees index live commitments and health. Finality processing subtracts the previously processed prefix from the current timestamp prefix, using modular arithmetic only for historical totals; each pending deadline’s amount remains checked. SecurityPoolFrozenHistory appends one immutable checkpoint with constant work and resolves claims through at most 48 forest steps and 48 tree steps. Forks copy its head and the processed prefix. Work follows bounded paths and the owner’s single position, without scanning questions, providers or expired streams. Native share migration still requires one action per intervening universe. SecurityPoolCommitmentIndex : liquidation accounting Only the bound capital ledger calls cure(id, price, multiplier, minimumCommitment, demand, target) or clearLiquidation(id) . The index updates commitments and records healthy observation epochs; the ledger retains custody. Each accepted operating price stores one observation timestamp, without visiting individual owners; preparation prices do not start that history. liquidationStarted(id) derives the first adverse observation after the latest healthy epoch or fresh-price recovery mutation. It returns zero for healthy or retired positions. The capital ledger exposes the same getter. No starter transaction is required; uninterrupted distress retains its original timestamp, including across stale-price deposits. SecurityPoolPortfolioForkCoordinator : choices choose(sourceUniverse, outcome) freezes and exports the caller’s single position and its entire deposit, including surplus collateral, into one selected branch. Idle principal without a remaining commitment and old earnings remain in the parent. Choices close eight weeks after the native fork. startBranch(sourceUniverse, outcome) then copies aggregate settlement and reporter roots and migrates REP once for that branch. Choices are immutable once exported and preserve the position’s retirement cohort and requested remaining commitment. The native clock allows activation from week nine and prevents a child fork before week ten. Late preparation does not extend either phase. Parent cash is assigned as sourceCash × chosenCommitment / sourceCommitment , rounded down, independently of excess REP. Unselected child REP belongs to inherited outcome claims; the coordinator never chooses a branch on the owner’s behalf. Shared branch claim recovery prepareBranch deploys child capital separately from migration. startBranch migrates pledged REP and live recovery REP once, splits the requested child once, and assigns chosen collateral to its providers. Fresh unchosen backing may enter the branch auction; old recovery REP remains reserved for inherited claims. finalizeBranch creates the settlement book with assigned parent cash plus accepted auction ETH, installs funded winning commitments, and activates after the fixed deadline. Unsold REP joins claim recovery. An absent or stale price does not prevent activation; eligible holding-fee streams start only with a fresh price in an unforked child. Preparation reporting follows the fixed reporting window . The child settlement book preserves the inherited question units. ETH redemption uses the question’s settlement units. REP has a separate Q128 entitlement accumulator and per-question corrections, so a partial claim receives its share of REP even when its ETH-unit burn rounds to zero. Finality freezes both entitlements. Live units and funded child REP are each bounded by uint128 ; cumulative REP indices use modular arithmetic across repeated forks. attoSharesToAttoEth and attoSharesToAttoRep quote the two assets through the native question pool. Recovery REP or cash below outstanding units disables new minting and ordinary holding fees. If no live claim units exist, surplus child REP is burned. Finalized source claims retain their source assets. Source recovery eligibility and rights ShareToken.isPortfolioRecoveryEligible(sourceUniverse) returns true only for a forked canonical portfolio question with live source liabilities. The settlement book’s hasLiveForkRecoveryRight(bookId, questionId, forkTimestamp) requires outstanding outcome supply outside frozen claims. Before sealing, a nonzero finalAfter < forkTimestamp excludes the question once forkTimestamp > minimumFinalAfter , even if finality has not yet been checkpointed. After sealing, the frozen-claim assignment is authoritative. Protected finalized shares retain ordinary source-pool redemption; transferring or consuming their recovery rights reverts with No live source recovery right . balanceOfPortfolioRecoveryRights(sourceUniverse, holder) returns unspent Invalid, Yes and No rights, or all zeros when ineligible; their minimum bounds complete-set cash recovery. Holders combine rights using transferPortfolioRecoveryRights(sourceUniverse, recipient, amountsAttoShares) without transferring source shares or child migration rights. recoverUnchosenCash(source, questionId, shares) consumes one right per outcome for each requested share. Protected finalized claims remain in the universe where they finalized. Reporter custody and native question materialization SecurityPoolReporterLedger holds funded per-question report balances in persistent books. Only canonical games can debit their own balances. A fork charges admission to the triggering question only; unrelated report balances cannot fund it. Strictly finalized escrow remains in its source universe; live books migrate once per branch. SecurityPoolForker.preparePortfolioQuestion(source, targetUniverse) creates the canonical question namespace separately from materializePortfolioQuestion , which installs at most one of three fixed outcome snapshots per call when needed; the question activates after the third. Bound questions do not deploy individual Collateral Repair Auctions or migrate provider vaults. Trader tokens retain their immediate-parent migration checks, so skipped native universes still need explicit token migration. SecurityPoolUniformPriceAuction Only its immutable controller starts bidding, submits funded bids, finalizes and settles refunds. Each lot contributes the same fixed ETH amount. Lower minimum REP per lot ranks first. For inventory I , finalization selects the largest available prefix of F lots, capped by target lots, whose marginal minimum m satisfies F × m ≤ I . At the target, clearing REP per lot q = m and sold REP is F × q . Below target with F > 0 , q = ⌊I / (F × grid)⌋ × grid and sold REP is all I . With no feasible lot, the sale is zero. Bids cannot be cancelled. Each winning lot pledges q REP. Any remainder I − F × q in a below-target sale enters the frozen bid tree as REP fee credits belonging only to winning lots. Marginal equal-price bids use cumulative proportional fills rounded to whole lots at m , even when q > m . The engine holds bid ETH; the controller holds sale REP. Refunds are pull credits for the bidder. Before finalization, earlyRefundAttoEth returns the full bid ETH only when strictly better-priced demand B ≥ min(T, ⌊I / p⌋) , where T is target lots and p is the bid’s minimum REP per lot; otherwise it returns zero. Settled, unknown and finalized bids return zero. Refunded bids remain in the tree, permanently outside every winning prefix. The same exclusion condition rejects a new bid at submission. Equal-price demand alone never makes a bid eligible. settleAndWithdrawRefund lets the controller credit one of a bidder’s own refund-eligible bids, unless already credited, and pay that bidder’s entire credit in one transfer; it reverts when nothing is payable, and a rejecting recipient reverts the new credit while older credit stays withdrawable. After finalization, nextUnmaterializedBid(afterBidId) returns the next winner still sharing fee ownership in price order. Zero starts a sweep and also marks its end. A nonzero cursor is excluded from the result and remains valid after its winner is materialized. Zero-weight subtrees skip both losing and detached bids without a history scan. Clearing, individual settlement and fee allocation use logarithmic tree paths without scanning bidders; numeric escrow and fee budgets are bounded by uint128 . SecurityPoolPortfolioAuction The fork coordinator deploys one controller per eligible branch. Its ETH lot is minimumCommitmentAttoEth ; target lots are the ETH shortfall divided by that lot, rounded down. Each bid escrows exactly its whole-lot bid ETH. No REP deposit or approval is required. The minimum REP per lot must cover requiredRep(lot, openingConservativePrice, M, health) , where M is securityMultiplierBps and health ≥ 10,000 is the chosen target in basis points. This rounds upward after multiplying lot ETH by price and dividing by 10¹⁸ , then after applying M / 10,000 , then health / 10,000 . Bid minima must be positive multiples of grid = M / gcd(M, 10,000) and cannot exceed inventory rounded down to that grid. There is no seller price floor or auction reserve-factor setting. Opening requires a fresh funded branch price and enough inventory to back one lot at 100% health, rounded up to the grid. At the fixed week-nine close, a missing funded quote or a conservative REP/ETH price above the opening price cancels the sale. Finalization delivers all sold REP into capital; winning commitments equal accepted bid ETH. Pledged REP and fee rights enter aggregate accounting immediately, independent of individual materialization or refunds. settleRefund lets anyone credit a provably excluded bid before finalization, or settle the final unfilled-lot refund afterward. Only the bidder can withdraw its credits, to a recipient of its choice, separately or combined with crediting one of its own eligible bids through settleAndWithdrawRefund ; the combined withdrawal includes credits from the bidder’s other bids. Unsold fresh REP and all old recovery REP remain for inherited claims. Sub-lot shortfalls remain unrepaired. SecurityPoolAuctionVault and SecurityPoolCapitalAuctionDelegate Each bid creates a separate vault controlled by its bidder: a minimal proxy to the one implementation the auction deployer publishes, with the bidder, initial ledger, auction and bid identifier fixed in the clone’s own code and the bid identifier as its CREATE2 salt. Each clone keeps its own storage, so per-bid liability isolation is unchanged, and the implementation refuses direct use. Winning principal and commitment first share one protected capital position. materializeAuctionBid splits one exact funded entitlement, preserving health history and historical fees; it pays no keeper reward. The bidder’s vault materializes before its first voluntary operation and then records a permanent flag, so later operations skip the auction lookup and the ledger call; the flag only covers the initial capital ledger, child-universe ledgers after a fork keep their own checks, and it is never set for a zero fill. depositPrincipal pulls approved REP from the controlling wallet. executeAndSweep executes an owner-authorized call and returns the chosen token and ETH balance to the selected recipient. Unmaterialized winners count toward capital, fees and fork snapshots. They cannot avoid liabilities by delaying an individual claim. A winner’s bidder cures through its vault with setCommitment ; takeOverAuctionBid pays its award in the affected position’s REP. Bidding requires no ETH execution reserve. Materialization alone earns no reward. Each vault retains an independent health target, liquidation liability and future branch choice. Portfolio module deployers SecurityPoolPortfolioModuleDeployers supplies separately deployed capital, commitment-index, fee-stream, settlement-book, settlement-engine, service-accounting and reporter-ledger constructors. The fork coordinator has its own deployer. The capital, service-accounting and fork coordinator deployers share SecurityPoolCreationCodeDeployer ; each stores its creation bytecode in two immutable, STOP-prefixed SecurityPoolCreationCode contracts. Each created module binds its authority to the requesting portfolio or configured controller. Deployment permission does not grant authority over another portfolio’s modules. Canonical bindings and initialization must be verified before use. These deployment boundaries keep creation code and individual initialization transactions within EVM limits. EscalationGameFactory deployEscalationGame and deployEscalationGameFromFork have no permission check, but a successful caller must implement the ISecurityPool reads used during construction; an EOA or incompatible contract reverts. The CREATE2 salt is zero; the caller is bound into the init code as the new game's securityPool , so each caller has one game address; the factory itself becomes the game's immutable owner and immediately calls start or startFromFork . Ordinary deployment requires a threshold above one attoREP and lowers a start bond at or above that threshold to threshold - 1 . Its constructor receives separately deployed claim and deposit delegates, in that order, and requires code at both addresses. Games share these immutable helpers while keeping their own accounting through delegatecall . Deploying the deposit delegate separately keeps factory init code within the EVM limit. The factory has no owner role and no later resumeFromFork relay. Accept a game only after EscalationGameSet from a recognized pool. In the supported continuation path, the owning pool calls resumeFromFork , which also verifies aggregate funding. ShareTokenFactory Anyone can call deployShareToken to deploy or retrieve a token keyed by keccak256(abi.encode(msg.sender, salt, questionId)) . The CREATE2 salt is the raw salt ; the caller and question id enter through constructor arguments, and that caller is the token's initial authorized address. Origin lineage tokens are the instances created by SecurityPoolFactory and named in DeploySecurityPool ; children reuse the parent's token. OpenOraclePriceCoordinatorFactory Anyone can call deployOpenOraclePriceCoordinator under a caller-namespaced salt with caller-supplied OpenOracle, REP token, and positive initialReportPriorityFeeAttoEthPerGas ; coordinator construction rejects zero and values that would consume the reserved OpenOracle uint128 report and escalation-halt capacity. The caller therefore chooses this immutable gas-price assumption within that bound. A pool coordinator is the instance named by canonical DeploySecurityPool ; SecurityPoolFactory atomically binds it to that pool before returning, and canonical child deployment inherits the value from the parent coordinator. PriceCoordinatorDeploymentWorker and SecurityPoolDeploymentWorker Deployment workers split large creation code into deployable modules and bind the calling factory. Use canonical factory registration and module bindings; code presence alone is insufficient. SecurityPoolDeployer and SecurityPoolDeploymentWorker Only the configured SecurityPoolFactory may call the deployer; only that deployer may call its worker. They are code-size deployment plumbing. The proxy deployer deploys one deterministic SecurityPoolOperationsDelegate , and its address is passed into the SecurityPoolFactory constructor. The proxy deployer is the deterministic CREATE2 deployment proxy that every canonical deployment goes through; it is not a Solidity source in this repository and appears in the deployment manifests as step proxyDeployer . The factory passes that address through its deployer and worker to every pool. Its deterministic address is published as securityPoolOperationsDelegate . Pools become canonical only when the factory emits SecurityPoolRegistered and DeploySecurityPool . EscalationGameDepositDelegate , EscalationGameClaimDelegate , SecurityPoolForkerPortfolioDelegate , SecurityPoolSettlementDelegate , and SecurityPoolOperationsDelegate EscalationGame delegates share game storage and implement reporting deposits and authenticated historical claims. SecurityPoolOperationsDelegate handles wallet reporting, continuation resumption, and redemption. SecurityPoolForkerPortfolioDelegate prepares child questions against shared branch state. These helpers execute in their recognized host context. Accept resulting state and logs only when the recognized game, pool, or forker executes the matching wrapper through delegatecall . State changes and events then occur in that recognized contract's context. Canonical liquidation enters through the portfolio capital ledger; continuation activation enters through SecurityPool.resumeForkedEscalationGame . SecurityPoolEventEmitter A fixed delegate shares the question ledger storage layout and handles central fee checkpoints, surplus transfers and settlement mirrors. Only events from canonical question addresses describe protocol state. Calling a helper directly does not mutate a registered question. EscalationGameProofVerifier , MerkleMountainRange and SecurityPoolUtils Proof and arithmetic helpers have no independent lifecycle authority. Only an authenticated protocol transaction commits a proof or calculation to state. DeploymentStatusOracle Anyone can call getDeploymentMask ; the constructor alone fixes the ordered address list and emits DeploymentAddressesSet . It reports code presence only, not canonical wiring or readiness. Decode it using its constructor event or the matching deployment manifest order.","title":"Contract inventory","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"securitypoolutils-read-surface","heading":"SecurityPoolUtils read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contract-inventory.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Every production Solidity source by area, the caller and trust boundaries of deployment helpers and delegate modules, SecurityPoolUtils previews, and testnet Uniswap dependencies.","text":"calculateInitialEscalationDepositAttoRep derives the first-report bond from theoretical REP supply. calculateMinimumVaultRepDepositAttoRep applies the configured minimum or the supply-derived default used for capital configuration. Other lifecycle and underwriting math belongs to the corresponding portfolio module.","title":"Contract inventory","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"production-contract-inventory","heading":"Production contract inventory","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contract-inventory.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Every production Solidity source by area, the caller and trust boundaries of deployment helpers and delegate modules, SecurityPoolUtils previews, and testnet Uniswap dependencies.","text":"Every production Solidity source is linked below. The preceding authority and accounting reference defines their canonical use; deploying a helper independently does not create a registered portfolio. Area Sources Core and token support Constants.sol , Context.sol , DeploymentStatusOracle.sol , ERC20.sol , GenesisReputationToken.sol , IERC20.sol , IERC20Metadata.sol , IExternalGenesisReputationToken.sol , ReputationToken.sol , SafeERC20Ops.sol , ScalarOutcomes.sol , Zoltar.sol , ZoltarQuestionData.sol , GenesisUniswapV3Seeder.sol , PredeploymentShareSink.sol , TradingLiquidityToken.sol , TwoWayConstantProductFactory.sol , TwoWayConstantProductMath.sol , TwoWayConstantProductPair.sol , TwoWayConstantProductRouter.sol , AuthorizationSignatures.sol , ERC20Authorization.sol , IERC20Authorization.sol Protocol modules BinaryOutcomes.sol , DelegateCall.sol , DelegateCallForwarder.sol , EscalationClaimCheckpointTypes.sol , EscalationGame.sol , EscalationGameCalculations.sol , EscalationGameCarry.sol , EscalationGameClaimDelegate.sol , EscalationGameDepositDelegate.sol , EscalationGameEscrow.sol , EscalationGameProofVerifier.sol , EscalationGameRepCustody.sol , EscalationGameSettlement.sol , EscalationGameState.sol , EscalationGameStorage.sol , EscalationGameTypes.sol , MerkleMountainRange.sol , Multicall3.sol , OpenOraclePriceCoordinator.sol , OpenOraclePriceCoordinatorTypes.sol , OpenOraclePriceSource.sol , OpenOracleReportDelegate.sol , SecurityPool.sol , SecurityPoolAuctionVault.sol , SecurityPoolCapitalAuctionDelegate.sol , SecurityPoolCapitalFeeStreams.sol , SecurityPoolCapitalLedger.sol , SecurityPoolCapitalLifecycleDelegate.sol , SecurityPoolCapitalState.sol , SecurityPoolCommitmentIndex.sol , SecurityPoolCommitmentTree.sol , SecurityPoolCreationCode.sol , SecurityPoolEventEmitter.sol , SecurityPoolFixedAdmissionPolicy.sol , SecurityPoolForker.sol , SecurityPoolForkerPortfolioDelegate.sol , SecurityPoolFrozenHistory.sol , SecurityPoolLinearFees.sol , SecurityPoolMigrationProxy.sol , SecurityPoolOpenAdmissionPolicy.sol , SecurityPoolOperationsDelegate.sol , SecurityPoolPersistentMap.sol , SecurityPoolPortfolio.sol , SecurityPoolPortfolioAccountingDelegate.sol , SecurityPoolPortfolioAuction.sol , SecurityPoolPortfolioForkCoordinator.sol , SecurityPoolPortfolioModuleDeployers.sol , SecurityPoolPortfolioPurchase.sol , SecurityPoolPortfolioServices.sol , SecurityPoolPortfolioSettlement.sol , SecurityPoolReporterLedger.sol , SecurityPoolResolutionPremiumBook.sol , SecurityPoolSettlementBook.sol , SecurityPoolSettlementDelegate.sol , SecurityPoolStorage.sol , SecurityPoolUniformPriceAuction.sol , SecurityPoolUtils.sol , SharedPoolPriceFeed.sol , SharedPoolReportEscrow.sol , SignatureValidation.sol , WETH9.sol , ERC1155.sol , ShareToken.sol , TokenId.sol Factories and deployment Create2Deployment.sol , EscalationGameFactory.sol , OpenOraclePriceCoordinatorFactory.sol , SecurityPoolDeployer.sol , SecurityPoolFactory.sol , ShareTokenFactory.sol Interfaces ICapitalAccounting.sol , IERC1155.sol , IERC1155Receiver.sol , IERC165.sol , IEscalationGame.sol , IEscalationGameDelegateContexts.sol , IPortfolioServiceRoot.sol , IPortfolioSettlement.sol , ISecurityPool.sol , ISecurityPoolAuctionCapital.sol , ISecurityPoolDeploymentWorkerConfiguration.sol , ISecurityPoolForker.sol , ISecurityPoolForkerChildPoolDeployer.sol , ISecurityPoolPortfolioAccounting.sol , ISecurityPoolPortfolioNative.sol , ISecurityPoolQuestionAdmissionPolicy.sol , ISecurityPoolReporterLedger.sol , IShareToken.sol , IWeth9.sol , IZoltarForkState.sol , ITradingShareToken.sol , ITwoWayConstantProductFactory.sol , ITwoWayConstantProductPair.sol Oracle dependency OpenOracle.sol , ISignatureTransfer.sol , Errors.sol , IERC1363.sol , IERC165.sol , IERC20.sol , IERC20.sol , SafeERC20.sol , Panic.sol , ReentrancyGuard.sol , StorageSlot.sol , IERC165.sol , Math.sol , SafeCast.sol","title":"Contract inventory","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"testnet-uniswap-dependencies","heading":"Testnet Uniswap dependencies","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contract-inventory.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Every production Solidity source by area, the caller and trust boundaries of deployment helpers and delegate modules, SecurityPoolUtils previews, and testnet Uniswap dependencies.","text":"The UI, the bots, and the testnet deployer read Uniswap addresses from one registry, shared/core/ts/deployment/uniswapDeployments.ts . Chain WETH, V3 factory, QuoterV2, V4 PoolManager, V4 Quoter SwapRouter Sepolia ( 11155111 ) Uniswap's published contracts ( V3 , V4 ); the deployer requires each to carry Uniswap's exact runtime code. An Anvil node with the Sepolia chain ID receives them by replaying Uniswap's original creation transactions, vendored byte for byte in scripts/artifacts/uniswap-deployment.json . Deterministic SwapRouter (v1) bound to the published factory; Uniswap publishes none on Sepolia. Any other testnet Deterministic WETH plus a complete Uniswap V3 and V4 deployment from the pinned bytecode. Deterministic SwapRouter from the pinned bytecode.","title":"Contract inventory","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["deployment","bitmask","runtime bytecode","status"],"path":"reference/deployment-status.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment progress bitmask semantics, per-network bit mappings, and the 256-step limit.","text":"DeploymentStatusOracle is a deployment-progress helper. It does not measure protocol readiness or initialization success. It only reports which configured deployment-step addresses currently contain runtime bytecode.","title":"Deployment status oracle","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"mask","heading":"What getDeploymentMask() returns","keywords":[],"path":"reference/deployment-status.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment progress bitmask semantics, per-network bit mappings, and the 256-step limit.","text":"The constructor stores one ordered address[] deploymentAddresses . getDeploymentMask() loops over that array and checks deploymentAddresses[index].code.length . When code exists, the oracle sets bit index in the returned uint256 . This is a code-presence bitmap. A set bit means code exists at the configured address. A clear bit means the address currently has no code. The oracle does not verify constructor arguments, ownership wiring, or post-deployment setup. A row of deployment steps maps by index into bit positions in a uint256 mask. Each step sets its bit only when code exists at that configured address. Deployment status mask Each configured deployment step uses one bit position in the returned word. The oracle sets that bit only if the step's address currently contains code. deployedMask = ∑ i = 0 codePresent ( i ) ⋅ 2 i Each configured deployment step contributes one bit position to the returned word.","title":"Deployment status oracle","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"ordering","heading":"How bits map to deployment steps","keywords":[],"path":"reference/deployment-status.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment progress bitmask semantics, per-network bit mappings, and the 256-step limit.","text":"Bit positions are array positions. Bit 0 maps to the first constructor address, bit 1 to the second, and so on. Off-chain decoding must use the same ordered list that seeded the oracle. The constructor emits DeploymentAddressesSet(address[] deploymentAddresses) with that exact ordered list. It is the on-chain source for recovering a particular oracle instance's bit mapping. The manifest below describes the current planned deployment and must match the constructor event for that instance. The current user interface (UI) and deployment helpers derive the constructor list from deploymentSteps with one exception: the oracle does not include its own address in its constructor array. The manifest still lists deploymentStatusOracle as a deployment step, but the UI tracks that step out of band and starts consuming mask bits from the remaining addresses. The tables below render the complete bit mappings directly from the canonical mainnet deployment manifest and Sepolia deployment manifest , excluding deploymentStatusOracle . These tables use the Yes manifests. The mainnet No manifest and Sepolia No manifest use the same bit order with their own deployment addresses. Each manifest's ordered deploymentSteps array is the canonical mapping for its network and genesis outcome. The orders differ: Sepolia inserts the genesis REP token at bit 1 , so the same mask names different steps on each network. Select the network before decoding. A second, Zoltar-only oracle is deployed by the Zoltar app's deployment flow and by the testnet deployer for each genesis outcome. The four manifests omit these oracles and the Trading factory and router. The testnet plan identifies the oracles as yes:zoltarDeploymentStatusOracle and no:zoltarDeploymentStatusOracle , and applies the same outcome prefixes to tradingFactory and tradingRouter . Each oracle's constructor list is a prefix of its manifest mapping: the proxy deployer, genesis REP on Sepolia, Multicall3, ZoltarQuestionData , and Zoltar . Decode its mask with the same table; only those leading bits exist. Bit Mainnet step id Label Status in entered mask Loading the canonical manifest… Bit Sepolia step id Label Status in entered mask Loading the canonical manifest… If the manifest's constructor order changes, the rendered bit meanings change with it; this page does not maintain a second list. The manifest's derivedContracts list adds no mask bits. Five of its six entries ( securityPoolForker , escalationGameClaimDelegate , escalationGameFactory , securityPoolFactory , and securityPoolOperationsDelegate ) repeat a deploymentSteps address under a different label, so each already has a bit. Only escalationGameProofVerifier , which EscalationGameFactory deploys in its constructor, has no bit. The tables use the deploymentSteps labels. In the Sepolia manifest the genesis REP step has the id reputationToken and the label GenesisReputationToken . It is the token at network.genesisRepTokenAddress , not a child ReputationToken .","title":"Deployment status oracle","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"deployment-mask-decoder","heading":"Decode a deployment mask","keywords":[],"path":"reference/deployment-status.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment progress bitmask semantics, per-network bit mappings, and the 256-step limit.","text":"Decoded status Loading the canonical mapping… Network Ethereum mainnet Sepolia Mask value The decoder maps each set bit to the selected network's constructor address at the same index. Retry loading the canonical mapping","title":"Deployment status oracle","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"limit","heading":"Why the cap is 256 steps","keywords":[],"path":"reference/deployment-status.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment progress bitmask semantics, per-network bit mappings, and the 256-step limit.","text":"The constructor requires deploymentAddresses.length <= uint256(type(uint8).max) + 1 , which is 256 . The limit exists because the result is a single uint256 , and each tracked step consumes one bit. Indices 0 through 255 are supported. A deployment process with more than 256 tracked addresses must split status across multiple masks or use a different representation.","title":"Deployment status oracle","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interpretation","heading":"Interpretation and verification boundaries","keywords":[],"path":"reference/deployment-status.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment progress bitmask semantics, per-network bit mappings, and the 256-step limit.","text":"Evidence What it proves What it does not prove Bit set Runtime bytecode exists at the configured address. Correct bytecode, constructor values, wiring, permissions, or operational readiness. Bit clear No runtime bytecode was observed at that address by this call. Whether the address is intentionally empty or the manifest is wrong. Manifest match The decoder used the intended ordered address list for the network. That deployed code matches the release or that remote procedure call (RPC) responses are honest. Full deployment verification Code hashes, required wiring, permissions, and manifest provenance can be checked together. Economic safety or future operational liveness.","title":"Deployment status oracle","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interpretation","heading":"Static decoder test vectors","keywords":[],"path":"reference/deployment-status.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment progress bitmask semantics, per-network bit mappings, and the 256-step limit.","text":"Mask Meaning 0x0 No tracked constructor address contains runtime code. 0x1 Only constructor address index 0 is present. 0x5 Constructor address indices 0 and 2 are present. 2^255 Only the highest supported bit, index 255, is present. The interactive tables require JavaScript. The ordered source lists are available in the mainnet deployment manifest and Sepolia deployment manifest ; bit i always corresponds to the i th constructor address after excluding deploymentStatusOracle .","title":"Deployment status oracle","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["MMR","carry proof","hashing","nullifier"],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"Statoblast uses a Merkle Mountain Range (MMR) only for inherited escalation carry: the unresolved deposits a parent Escalation Game exports when its universe forks. Each child universe 's continuation game, the child Escalation Game that resumes the dispute during fork continuation , verifies withdrawals against those compact snapshots without replaying the full parent history on-chain. The hashing primitives live in MerkleMountainRange.sol . Snapshot storage, proof structs, peak bounds, and replay protection live in EscalationGameTypes.sol , EscalationGameCarry.sol , and EscalationGameProofVerifier.sol . Local carry appends live in EscalationGameDepositDelegate.sol .","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":1},{"fragment":"leaf-shape","heading":"Leaf fields","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"Each leaf commits to one unresolved deposit. The encoding fixes the field order and hashing. Field Why it is committed depositor Binds the payout to the original depositor; see reporter claims , the per-continuation record of claimed carried deposits that prevents a proof from being replayed. cumulativeAmountAttoRep Preserves payout-order prefix data. sourceNodeId Distinguishes otherwise identical leaves copied from different local nodes.","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"encoding","heading":"Normative encoding","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"The leaf ABI types are (address,uint8,uint256,uint256,uint256,uint256) , in the order depositor, outcome, amountAttoRep, parentDepositIndex, cumulativeAmountAttoRep, sourceNodeId . outcome is the Solidity enumeration ordinal ( 0 Invalid, 1 Yes, 2 No). Leaf hashing uses standard ABI word padding through abi.encode ; parent hashing uses the 64 raw bytes from abi.encodePacked(left, right) , so the order of left and right is significant. There is no additional domain separator. leafHash = keccak256(abi.encode(depositor, outcome, amountAttoRep, parentDepositIndex, cumulativeAmountAttoRep, sourceNodeId)) parentHash(left, right) = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(left, right)) // peaks[0..peakCount-1] are the occupied peaks in ascending height order bagPeaks(peaks, peakCount): if peakCount == 0: return bytes32(0) root = peaks[peakCount - 1] for i = peakCount - 1 down to 1: root = parentHash(peaks[i - 1], root) return root The fold starts at the highest occupied peak and places each lower peak as the left operand. With occupied heights 0 , 2 , and 3 , the root is parentHash(peak0, parentHash(peak2, peak3)) . A single peak is the root itself, and an empty range has root bytes32(0) . Proof verification rejects each of the following with the quoted revert string: an outcome whose snapshot has no leaves: Carry peak absent ; a zero amountAttoRep : Proof amount zero ; leafIndex >= leafCount : Bad carry leaf ; a merkleMountainRangePeakIndex that is not the height of the peak containing leafIndex : Bad carry peak ; a sibling array whose length is not peakHeight + peakCount - 1 : Bad MMR proof length ; a reconstructed root that differs from the bagged snapshot peaks: Bad carry proof ; a nullifier path whose length is not 64 : Bad nullifier length ; a nullifier path that does not reproduce the current nullifier root from an empty leaf, which includes an already-consumed nullifier: Bad nullifier proof .","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"encoding","heading":"Conformance vectors","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"Input or intermediate Value Leaf 0 ABI values 0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000001, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0 Leaf 0 hash 0xb01042d963a0c261d64893dfb7e1f221c93ef608653cb92f9ca2c8899ca4572b Leaf 1 ABI values 0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000002, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1 Leaf 1 hash 0xe0ca39e8852dc96783e8b8f0859cfbf155338f9144ba4b9fdd6af847299fc867 Two-leaf root 0x1c9c7f3c2c8bebf92ce393deb5dccdd253f2b10084af72defb5dcd4486832b27 Leaf 2 ABI values 0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000003, 2, 3, 2, 3, 2 Leaf 2 hash 0x92b2040f7e9b4f1e1b587eda2fb255419704a6ea1693789658adfddbf98389ae Three-leaf root 0x3488c83edd77c5259c9432e90f972d45d0ced819fd3691eddfe41fe05d676564 Empty nullifier root 0xe2c3ed4052eeb1d60514b4c38ece8d73a27f37fa5b36dcbf338e70de95798caa","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"peaks","heading":"Peaks and the 64-peak bound","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"The carry snapshot stores one peak per set bit in the leaf count. The system fixes MERKLE_MOUNTAIN_RANGE_MAX_PEAKS = 64 , and carry initialization requires each snapshot leaf count to be less than 2^64 . The constant caps the number of peak positions, not the number of leaves: any leaf count from 0 up to but not including 2^64 fits inside 64 peak slots. A leaf count of thirteen is shown as binary one one zero one, corresponding to occupied peaks at heights zero, two, and three which are later bagged into one root. MMR peaks. The occupied peak heights are exactly the set bits in the snapshot leaf count. To form one root, the verifier collects the occupied peaks in ascending height order and folds them with bagPeaks . snapshotLeafCount < 2 64 Carry initialization rejects a larger leaf count with Leaf count high . Local carry appends behave like binary addition with carries: a new leaf merges upward through occupied lower peaks until it finds the first empty peak slot. If that upward carry would reach height 64 , the append reverts with MMR too tall .","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"proofs","heading":"Proof structure","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"A carried-deposit proof has two parts. First it proves membership in the inherited MMR snapshot for one outcome. Then it proves that the same parentDepositIndex has not already been consumed in this continuation's nullifier tree. Component Purpose leafIndex , merkleMountainRangePeakIndex , merkleMountainRangeSiblings Proves the carried deposit belongs to the inherited snapshot root for that outcome. nullifierSiblings Proves the nullifier leaf is still empty so the carried deposit cannot be replayed. The membership lane combines the carried deposit leaf with bottom-up siblings inside its selected peak, then with other occupied peak roots in ascending height order to reconstruct the snapshot root. The nullifier lane hashes the stable parent deposit index and combines it with a fixed-depth sibling path to prove the claim has not already been consumed. Carry proof anatomy. The MMR sibling array first reconstructs one selected peak and then supplies every other occupied peak. The independent 64-level nullifier path prevents the same stable deposit identity from being consumed twice. merkleMountainRangePeakIndex is the occupied peak height, not the ordinal position of that peak among occupied peaks. leafIndex is the leaf's global position across the whole snapshot. The verifier derives the peak that contains that position, requires merkleMountainRangePeakIndex to equal its height, and orders the in-peak siblings by the in-peak offset leafIndex - peakStart , where peakStart is the peakStartIndex returned by getCurrentCarryPeakForLeaf . Claim accounting also keys inherited-versus-local provenance and settled principal by the global position. For example, if the full snapshot has 13 leaves, the height- 3 peak holds global leaves 0 through 7 , the height- 2 peak holds 8 through 11 , and the height- 0 peak holds 12 . A proof for the third leaf of the height- 2 peak uses merkleMountainRangePeakIndex = 2 and leafIndex = 10 ; its in-peak offset is 2 . merkleMountainRangeSiblings is also ordered in two phases. The first merkleMountainRangePeakIndex entries are the bottom-up Merkle siblings inside the selected peak. The remaining entries are the other occupied peak roots in ascending peak-height order, skipping the selected peak height. That exact ordering is why the verifier checks merkleMountainRangeSiblings.length == peakHeight + peakCount - 1 .","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"nullifier-tree","heading":"Nullifier tree","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"Each continuation outcome keeps a binary Merkle tree of fixed depth NULLIFIER_DEPTH = 64 , hashed with the same parentHash . The path is uint256(keccak256(abi.encode(parentDepositIndex))) . Only its low 64 bits are used: bit d , counted from the least significant bit, selects the side at depth d , starting at the leaf. A 0 bit hashes parentHash(node, sibling) ; a 1 bit hashes parentHash(sibling, node) . An unconsumed leaf is bytes32(0) ; a consumed leaf is bytes32(uint256(1)) . The empty root is bytes32(0) hashed with itself 64 times: root = parentHash(root, root) . Its value is in the conformance vectors . nullifierSiblings lists the 64 siblings from the leaf upward. The verifier recomputes the current root with the empty leaf, then stores the root recomputed with the consumed leaf.","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"mmr-proof-planner","heading":"Plan a carry proof","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"Choose a snapshot leaf count and one occupied peak. The planner reports the global leafIndex range of that peak, checks an in-peak offset, and reports the exact sibling-array lengths the verifier enforces. It does not construct hashes or replace an off-chain proof builder. Selection Valid in-peak offset Selected peak capacity 4 leaves; leafIndex 8…11 Snapshot Snapshot leaf count Occupied peak height Height 2 Proof position In-peak offset MMR sibling hashes required 4 Nullifier sibling hashes required 64 Binary leaf count 1101₂ Occupied peak heights 0, 2, 3","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"snapshots","heading":"Snapshots in child continuations","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"Each continuation outcome stores both an inherited snapshot baseline and a mutable current state. Field Meaning snapshotLeafCount and snapshotPeaks The immutable inherited carry commitment. currentLeafCount and currentPeaks The descendant carry snapshot after inherited snapshot initialization plus local carry appends and local carry-leaf removal. Inherited proof consumption is tracked by currentNullifierRoot and unresolved totals rather than by mutating these peak fields directly. inheritedUnresolvedTotalAttoRep and localUnresolvedTotalAttoRep The principal totals the current carry state must still represent. currentNullifierRoot The replay-protection root after any carried proof has been consumed. getForkCarrySnapshot() reports the current carry peaks, leaf counts, totals, and nullifier roots after local appends and proof consumption. getCarryLeafPageByOutcome() pages only local unresolved leaves in newest-first order. Its maxEntries parameter bounds visited nodes, including consumed leaves. An empty or short page can return a nonzero nextPageNodeId ; pass that cursor as startNodeId to continue until the cursor is zero.","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"sources","heading":"Sources","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"MerkleMountainRange.sol EscalationGameProofVerifier.sol EscalationGameCarry.sol EscalationGameTypes.sol EscalationGameDepositDelegate.sol","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Statoblast","markets","security pools","overview"],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"Prediction markets Follow one question: “Will the fictional Aurora spacecraft launch before 31 December 2030?” Statoblast creates a collateralized market with Yes, No, and Invalid shares, resolves ordinary disagreement locally, and uses Zoltar branches only when local resolution cannot produce a decision or another fork interrupts the pool.","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":1},{"fragment":"mental-model","heading":"1. Mental model","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"# Statoblast is the market layer. Each SecurityPool ledger serves one question in one universe. Separate portfolios keep separate ledgers even when their question and configuration match. Every ledger belongs to a portfolio and uses its shared capital and settlement accounting. A fixed portfolio containing one question uses the same contracts and rules as a portfolio containing many questions. Zoltar owns the questions, universes, REP, and forks underneath it; Trading and OpenOracle sit beside it. The system overview introduces all four. A portfolio shares capital across registered questions. Questions resolve locally through reporting. A universe fork uses shared owner choices and branch accounting; individual child questions are prepared on demand. Market lifecycle. Local resolution is the ordinary route. An own-question non-decision or an independent universe fork while the pool remains unresolved and operational enters the migration and collateral-repair route.","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"normal-lifecycle","heading":"2. Normal market lifecycle","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"# Register the example question in Zoltar and add it to a portfolio whose admission policy permits it. Vault owners deposit REP; traders deposit ETH to mint complete sets. Traders may exchange shares through an exchange such as Trading . After the question ends, the first REP deposit on an outcome opens the Escalation Game . An unchallenged report wins three days later; a contested game runs until one outcome is the strict leader at its deadline. Holders then redeem winning shares from the pool's settlement collateral. ETH mints Invalid, Yes, and No shares. Operational pools redeem full sets, and finalized winning shares redeem through outcome settlement. Share lifecycle. One unit of ETH mints matching Invalid, Yes, and No claims; the complete set can be burned while the pool is operational or the winning share redeemed after finalization.","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"participants-assets","heading":"3. Participants and assets","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"# Traders and liquidity providers Mint complete sets, hold or trade outcome shares, redeem claims, and optionally provide Yes/No liquidity. Vault owners Deposit REP, set a separate underwriting commitment in ETH, keep it backed, earn fees, and may report or migrate. Reporters Place REP-backed positions on Invalid, Yes, or No in the Escalation Game. Liquidation receivers and operators A funded receiver accepts an underbacked commitment and receives a REP award from that owner’s deposit. Execution is worthwhile only when the award covers its cost. Collateral Repair Auction bidders Supply ETH in exchange for child-pool REP-backed accounting positions when a child needs collateral repair. Asset or claim What it represents Where it is held or recorded ETH settlement collateral Backing for outstanding complete sets The portfolio settlement contract holds ETH; the settlement book records its liabilities. Migration allocates rather than copies collateral. REP Universe-specific reporting and security capital; never settlement collateral Wallets hold liquid REP, the capital ledger holds underwriting principal, and the reporter ledger holds reporting escrow. Escalation Games record the associated claims. Outcome shares Yes, No, or Invalid claims for one pool and universe An ERC-1155 ShareToken deployed once per origin pool and reused by all of its fork descendants Underwriting commitment ETH a vault owner agrees to underwrite; its share of the pool total sets the vault's collateral obligations and fee rights The portfolio capital ledger Escalation claims REP-backed positions on reported outcomes The Escalation Game until claimed or migrated Trading LP tokens A claim on one trading pool 's Yes and No reserves The trading pool's ERC-20 ledger","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"local-resolution","heading":"4. Local resolution","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"# The game opens with the first REP deposit after the question ends. REP-backed balances then accumulate behind Invalid, Yes, and No. Their median is binding capital and determines the moving deadline. After that deadline, exactly one strict balance leader produces the local winner. Winning shares redeem from that pool. If two outcomes reach the non-decision threshold , the game does not choose between them. Anyone can then fork the universe on the market question through Statoblast, which pays the fork threshold from the triggering question’s REP escrow in the portfolio reporter ledger. See the Escalation Game explanation for the required-support curve, deadlines, payouts, and how games continue across forks.","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"forks-migration","heading":"5. Forks and migration","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"A question's non-decision or a fork on another question enters the same shared branch process. Owners choose once per portfolio and universe. Questions, outcome tokens and reporter claims are prepared lazily when used. See the choice windows and recovery rules below .","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"fees-capacity-liquidations","heading":"6. Fees, underwriting, and liquidations","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"Each owner's REP backs their authorized ETH commitment. Purchases require sufficient healthy aggregate commitment and a fresh funded oracle price. Returning complete sets releases demand; it does not require owners to recover REP question by question. Fees accrue separately from principal. Liquidation transfers only the affected owner's commitment and REP.","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"question-portfolios","heading":"7. Managing several questions","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"# A SecurityPoolPortfolio lets traders buy outcomes for many questions against one aggregate backing pool. REP stays in a central capital ledger. Each owner retains an exact claim to their own principal and chooses how much standing commitment to authorize. There is no common REP share price and no purchase from individual vaults. Questions retain separate outcome tokens and reporting games. All REP deposited for an owner in a portfolio universe backs that owner's single commitment, including any surplus above its required backing. Depositing more REP immediately strengthens that backing; there is no separate pledge or top-up step. Each owner chooses a health target for authorizing commitment. A larger buffer supports less commitment with the same REP but gives more protection against price changes. Admission and surplus withdrawals use the conservative edge of the oracle’s configured error band. Paid liquidation uses the opposite edge and compares the entire deposit with required backing, so a small price error alone cannot create an execution reward. Falling below the owner's chosen buffer alone does not trigger it. New purchases require enough healthy commitment at a fresh shared REP/ETH price. New commitments must fit a finalized report’s funded exposure. Independent report candidates prevent one pending report from blocking another sponsor. An owner can authorize growth using an independently finalized, sufficiently funded report; its conservative collateral requirement stays attached to that owner until a new shared report covers the enlarged pool. The creator fixes an admission-policy contract, the annual time-fee rate, the question horizon and economic minimums. The interface offers either open admission or a permanently fixed question list. Other policies work onchain. Registration moves no REP and creates no claim obligation for owners. Every purchase checks admission, pays the configurable backing-based fee and prepaid resolution premium, and delivers its three outcomes atomically. Unused payment budget returns to the buyer. Income is separate from principal. Continuously earned question fees belong to the healthy commitments that served during that interval. Purchase fees and REP income vest over a configured duration. A new deposit cannot collect an earlier owner's earned fees, and borrowing REP for one transaction cannot capture a whole prepaid reward. Owners collect aggregate earnings without visiting questions. See portfolio fees for the earning rules. Reporter REP has its own custody and cannot be taken from a vault's backing. Irrevocably released dispute surplus enters the separate REP income stream. It does not raise an entry or exit exchange rate. Final reporter claims and their required burns remain separately funded. The chosen health target protects voluntary changes: authorizing more commitment, receiving a takeover, or withdrawing backing requires enough REP at that target and a fresh conservative price. Raising the target on a live position also requires sufficient backing; an owner can deposit REP and raise it in one transaction. Lowering the target never increases commitment automatically. A price move that leaves the position below its health target does not itself cause liquidation, and deposits and earned-fee claims remain available. An owner can withdraw surplus REP while keeping the remaining backing sufficient to meet the chosen target. This does not require other owners to be healthy. Principal with no remaining commitment is freely withdrawable. To reduce commitment, the owner sets a lower amount, including zero for a full exit, and the reduction is queued for retirement. Returning complete sets reduces aggregate demand, letting queued commitments retire before new purchases consume the spare capacity. Each owner has at most one live position, either active or in a retirement cohort. Partial retirement keeps the whole deposit behind the smaller commitment and stops at the requested amount, then returns that position to active status. The request fixes a commitment amount; it does not automatically adjust to later prices. Full retirement releases the entire deposit. The pool serves a closed current cohort before the next cohort. The owner cannot change or cancel a pending request: until it is served, setting a commitment accepts only the requested amount, which leaves the request in place and, at a fresh price, cures an underwater position. Full exits settle in groups, while each service call updates at most one partial-retirement boundary. While a partial request waits, its intended remaining commitment stays available to purchases if the whole position is healthy; only the queued reduction is reserved for exit. An owner can complete their own request without processing earlier owners when spare capacity also covers those earlier requests. Otherwise retirement waits for more capacity or progresses through the queue. Purchases do not have to clear the whole queue, and withdrawal does not add a question-by-question recovery task. Liquidation affects the undercollateralized vault. Apart from a takeover, only the owner can cure. Lowering the commitment, or repeating a pending request, at a fresh price cures an underwater vault at once, down to no less than the requested value: the unbacked commitment is removed, subject to the minimum useful action size and the spare commitment above demand, and only the rest of the reduction waits in the retirement queue. The cure can skip the queue because unbacked commitment never counts as healthy capacity, so removing it takes no spare from earlier requests. When the new value is at or below the backed amount and spare capacity covers the deficit, the remaining commitment is fully backed. If the owner does not act, or spare capacity is insufficient, the position remains eligible for takeover by a funded buyer. The buyer's REP reward comes only from the target position. The takeover clock begins automatically at the first accepted adverse price after the position's latest recovery, so no keeper must pay to start it. Cures pay nobody; takeovers transfer the quoted REP with the underwriting obligation and cure the target first. Neither requires an ETH reserve. Other owners' principal is unchanged. Keepers pay transaction gas and act when a takeover award justifies the cost. The contract reference specifies cure sizing and partial cleanup. Health and fee accounting use indexed aggregates. Work follows tree paths and the owner's single position, rather than all questions, owners or expired streams. The healthy-commitment index is balanced. Purchase-fee and REP-income rewards use a fixed vesting duration, so searches exclude already released history and are bounded by that duration. Question resolution premiums use an expiry index that releases only earned fees and burns unused terminal funding, without scanning questions. Finality checkpoints read the newly matured prefix and append one immutable record; they do not copy historical tree paths. Storage still grows with activity. Creating a question, using it for the first time in a branch and processing one's tokens still require transactions, but they do not impose a migration batch on every backing owner. A native universe fork fixes an eight-week choice window. Each owner may make one irreversible branch choice for their commitment and its entire deposit in that universe, including the surplus buffer. Principal with no remaining commitment and old earnings stay separately claimable in the parent. After choices close, one shared migration prepares each requested branch. Each branch inherits the outstanding outcome claims, including tokens not yet materialized there. Parent ETH follows the fraction of total ETH commitment that chose the branch, so choosing excess REP cannot capture extra cash. Chosen providers retain their own REP, commitment and health target. REP in branches they did not choose backs those branches’ inherited outcome claims. A shared collateral repair auction can sell freshly unchosen REP to repair the branch’s ETH shortfall. Bidders, including former owners, supply only their bid ETH; the purchased REP backs their accepted ETH commitment. A positive fill below the auction’s ETH target sells all fresh inventory. With no feasible lot, none is sold; a sale reaching the target can leave unsold REP with the claims. Old recovery REP is excluded from the sale. At week nine, a prepared branch can activate even if no sale succeeds. Returning complete sets or redeeming winning shares then pays the claim’s proportional ETH and remaining recovery REP together. Full repayment in ETH is not guaranteed. A branch with recovery REP or a cash shortfall cannot mint new complete sets or charge ordinary holding fees; later buyers cannot capture its inherited recovery assets. Missing the choice cutoff does not select a default branch: the owner’s committed REP backs inherited claims in every child. For questions still live at the fork, holders recover unassigned parent ETH by combining separate Invalid, Yes and No recovery rights. These rights transfer independently of child-token migration rights. Questions already final retain their ETH and REP claims in the source pool. A later fork migrates only the remaining live recovery REP, once per branch. Child forks cannot begin before week ten; late preparation does not restart these deadlines. Questions and reporter claims are prepared lazily when used, and traders still migrate shares through each intervening universe. The portfolio fork reference specifies the interfaces.","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"security","heading":"8. Security assumptions and non-guarantees","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"# REP-backed incentives do not prove objective truth; users and applications decide which branches retain activity. OpenOracle depends on timely, profitable correction of inaccurate REP/ETH reports. A fork gives each child only the ETH that migrates to it ( Zoltar's non-guarantees ). ETH pushed into a pool outside its accounting is not counted as collateral. Steps that anyone may trigger, such as forking or starting an auction, still need someone to send the transaction. Spot prices from trading pools are manipulable and are never protocol oracles. Normative assumptions and guarantees are listed in the security model and invariant catalog .","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"security","heading":"Design rationale","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"Statoblast's ancestry includes the Arctic Tern Oracle , Sisyphean Exchange , Partial Colored Coins , and Isonzo . Those research ideas influenced the design; the deployed contracts govern current behavior.","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Zoltar","universes","questions","REP"],"path":"explanation/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Universes, question encoding, global forks, and REP splitting.","text":"Branching universes Zoltar records global questions and lets an eligible parent universe branch into outcome-specific child universes . Each child universe has its own REP ledger, and REP holders move into children under fixed migration rules.","title":"Zoltar and branching universes","topic":"Protocol","weight":1},{"fragment":"branching","heading":"A parent becomes separate child ledgers","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Universes, question encoding, global forks, and REP splitting.","text":"# For a binary market question, one forking parent universe creates separate Invalid, Yes, and No child ledgers. An unrelated fork instead follows its own fork question's valid answers. Forking parent Invalid branch Valid outcome branches Branch set. A binary launch question produces Invalid, Yes, and No children. Alice converts 100 parent REP into a migration balance , a parent-universe credit that can mint the same amount in each valid child, and uses it to mint 100 child REP in the Yes universe and 100 child REP in the No universe. These are separate branch-specific ledgers. They are not 200 globally interchangeable REP, they do not copy ETH, and Zoltar does not guarantee equal economic value across branches. Applications and users later determine where economic activity continues.","title":"Zoltar and branching universes","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"questions","heading":"Global questions and fork eligibility","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Universes, question encoding, global forks, and REP splitting.","text":"# Questions are global Zoltar objects and are not bound to a universe. Any account can fork a universe by naming a question when four conditions hold: the universe has not forked before, so each universe forks at most once; the question exists and its end time has passed; the caller supplies the full fork threshold in that universe's REP; and, for a child universe, ten weeks have passed since its parent fork. Zoltar does not check that the question concerns the universe, so applications such as Statoblast enforce any stricter relationship they need. The child cooldown reserves eight weeks for portfolio branch choices, one week before shared branch activation and one week for responses. All deadlines derive from the native fork timestamp; preparing a child late does not restart them. Portfolio branches distribute recovery assets to outcome claims and may auction freshly unchosen REP to repair their ETH shortfall. The fixed response interval gives reporting a chance to proceed before another fork can interrupt the child. The fork question decides which child universes can exist: one for each of its valid answers. A categorical question allows Invalid and each of its labels. A scalar question allows Invalid or a well-formed packed scalar answer. Zoltar sets no migration deadline; REP holders can add to their migration balance at any time after the fork.","title":"Zoltar and branching universes","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"guarantees","heading":"What Zoltar guarantees","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Universes, question encoding, global forks, and REP splitting.","text":"# Universe and child identities derive deterministically from their parent and outcome. Only valid outcomes for the fork question can create child universes or receive child REP. Each account cannot mint more REP into one child than its recorded migration balance permits. Child REP is controlled by Zoltar and remains tied to its universe identity. Every child starts with the parent's pre-fork theoretical REP supply , the per-universe supply figure from which fork thresholds are derived, less exactly the fork haircut .","title":"Zoltar and branching universes","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"non-guarantees","heading":"What Zoltar does not guarantee","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Universes, question encoding, global forks, and REP splitting.","text":"# It does not choose a truthful or canonical child. It does not copy ETH or application state into every child. Each child receives only the collateral that migrates to it, so a Statoblast child pool can remain impaired if too little ETH follows and its Collateral Repair Auction cannot raise the difference. It does not guarantee equal REP value, liquidity, or redemption across branches. It does not prove a supplied global question is relevant to the universe being forked. It does not guarantee that applications continue in every branch.","title":"Zoltar and branching universes","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"accounting","heading":"Deterministic identities and migration accounting","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Universes, question encoding, global forks, and REP splitting.","text":"# A child universe ID is derived from its parent universe ID and selected outcome. Child REP uses a predictable deployment address derived from the Zoltar address and universe ID. Adding parent REP to a migration balance burns or transfers it from the parent ledger. That balance can then materialize the same branch-specific amount in multiple valid children, with per-child accounting preventing replay beyond the source amount. Starting a fork removes the fork threshold from parent supply. The threshold is the live universe theoretical REP supply divided by the threshold divisor, rounded up, so every universe with nonzero supply has a positive fork cost. The fork haircut, the threshold divided by the burn divisor and rounded down, is not credited to migration; the remainder becomes the initiator's migration balance. Both divisors are fixed when Zoltar is deployed. The configured defaults are a threshold divisor of 20 and a burn divisor of 5: the threshold is 5% of supply and the haircut is 20% of the threshold, about 1% of supply. Zoltar rejects a burn divisor below 5, so the haircut never exceeds 20% of the threshold. The full fork threshold leaves parent supply. Most becomes the initiator's migration balance and the fork haircut is not credited. Fork deposit. The default split, subject to integer rounding. fork threshold = ⌈live theoretical REP supply ÷ threshold divisor⌉ fork haircut = ⌊fork threshold ÷ burn divisor⌋ Each fork reduces theoretical supply by its fork haircut, so later thresholds decline along that lineage. Repeated forks. Integer-rounded fork haircuts reduce theoretical supply and therefore later fork thresholds.","title":"Zoltar and branching universes","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"encodings","heading":"Question and outcome encodings","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Universes, question encoding, global forks, and REP splitting.","text":"# Question IDs hash the stored question data together with the outcome labels, which a categorical question must supply in strictly descending order of their hashes. A categorical answer is 0 for Invalid or a number from 1 to n that selects one of the n labels in that order. Scalar questions store a range and tick count; a valid packed answer allocates all ticks between its two payout fields. The all-zero word is canonical Invalid, while reserved-bit or payout-sum violations are malformed and rejected. A scalar answer contains reserved bits, a namespace bit, and first and second payout numerators. Valid payout fields sum to the question's tick count. Namespace bit First payout Second payout Packed scalar answer. Encoding validation keeps Invalid distinct from malformed input. Exact storage fields, permissions, and entrypoints are listed in the contract reference . Security assumptions are listed in the security model .","title":"Zoltar and branching universes","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","AMM","liquidity","prices"],"path":"explanation/trading.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Complete sets, directional liquidity, conditional prices, and market isolation.","text":"Explanation Trading is a separate on-chain exchange for Statoblast outcome shares , one of possibly many that can be built on Statoblast. Statoblast still owns market creation, collateral, migration, resolution, and redemption through each market's SecurityPool .","title":"How Trading works","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"complete-set-entry","heading":"From ETH to a directional position","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Complete sets, directional liquidity, conditional prices, and market isolation.","text":"Each ETH entry first creates a complete set : one Yes, one No, and one Invalid share. A market's trading pool , a Yes/No pair, is an automated market maker (AMM) that holds only Yes and No reserves. The router sends the unwanted directional share into the pair, receives more of the selected directional share, and returns the directional position and the Invalid share to the user. The Invalid share never enters the AMM; it remains a separate claim if the market later resolves Invalid.","title":"How Trading works","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"prices","heading":"What the prices mean","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Complete sets, directional liquidity, conditional prices, and market isolation.","text":"Yes and No prices are conditional on valid resolution. They describe the relative price of the two directional outcomes; they are not estimates of invalidity. Interfaces should therefore label the Yes and No values directly and present Invalid separately. Spot prices come from current reserves and are not an oracle . Settlement uses the SecurityPool's resolved outcome, while solvency-sensitive REP/ETH operations use OpenOracle.","title":"How Trading works","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"market-isolation","heading":"One pair per SecurityPool","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Complete sets, directional liquidity, conditional prices, and market isolation.","text":"Each SecurityPool maps to its own pair. Parent and child pools therefore have separate pairs, reserves, prices, and liquidity-provider tokens; liquidity, like everything else in a fork , is not copied between them.","title":"How Trading works","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"reading-paths","heading":"Where to go next","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Complete sets, directional liquidity, conditional prices, and market isolation.","text":"Learn by doing Your first Trading market and your first trade . Get something done Set up development , configure the UI , deploy the contracts , seed alternative odds , exit a position , remove liquidity , handle resolution , and handle a fork . Understand the design The two-way market , Invalid insurance , conditional pricing , the LP position , early-exit limits , forks and resolution , architecture decisions and limitations . Look something up Contracts , router , events , SDK , deployment configuration , invariants , units and rounding , and the security model .","title":"How Trading works","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Escalation Game","disputes","deadlines","accounting"],"path":"explanation/escalation-game.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How REP deposits resolve a market locally, when they force a fork, and how games continue across forks.","text":"The Escalation Game gives a finished Statoblast market a local, REP-backed resolution path before a universe-wide fork is necessary. A report that nobody challenges wins three days after the first deposit opens the game. For a binary launch question, suppose Yes has 8 REP, No has 5 REP, and Invalid has 2 REP. The median balance, 5 REP, is the game's binding capital : the stake the strongest challenger has committed, and the amount that sets the scheduled deadline. A deposit that raises only the leading Yes balance does not move that deadline; a deposit that raises Invalid above 5 REP raises the median and moves it later. After the deadline, Yes wins only if it remains the unique strict leader. If two outcomes each reach the non-decision threshold before local finalization, the game records non-decision and the SecurityPool follows the Zoltar fork path. The threshold is half of Zoltar's fork threshold , rounded up, and is fixed when the game opens. A tie is never treated as a local winner. For an ordinary game, the scheduled deadline is the last second before the forward attrition curve strictly exceeds the challenger’s balance. A continuation also observes its minimum response period. The game, reporter ledger, and settlement book use the same deadline, including any plateau caused by integer rounding. If a universe forks while its game remains unresolved, the parent’s curve stops at the recorded fork time. Later wall-clock time cannot finalize that parent. A carried game preserves its inherited elapsed time and stops again if its child universe forks, including when it resumes after that fork. Any REP holder can fund a report from their wallet. After an unrelated fork, wallet reporting opens again once the carried game resumes. Reporting deposits are separate from underwriting principal. Each deposit must meet the start bond , the larger of 1 REP and one ten-millionth of the universe's theoretical REP supply when the pool was deployed, unless it exactly fills an outcome to the non-decision threshold.","title":"Escalation Game","topic":"Protocol","weight":1},{"fragment":"escalation","heading":"Escalation","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/escalation-game.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How REP deposits resolve a market locally, when they force a fork, and how games continue across forks.","text":"The first deposit after the question ends opens the game. Each deposit adds to its outcome's cumulative balance; it does not have to exceed the leader. Meanwhile the required support rises along a fixed curve over time. The scheduled deadline follows the strict forward crossing of binding capital, so it moves later only when a deposit raises the median balance. A challenger that stops adding REP lets the rising requirement pass its stake, and the leader wins. Required support is zero through day 3, starts at the start bond just after day 3, and reaches the non-decision threshold on day 52. Required support. Nothing is required for the first three days. From day 3 the requirement climbs from the start bond to the non-decision threshold, reaching it on day 52. The EscalationGame reference gives the exact curve.","title":"Escalation Game","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"escalation-game-example","heading":"Try a simplified escalation round","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/escalation-game.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How REP deposits resolve a market locally, when they force a fork, and how games continue across forks.","text":"This simulator uses the contract model: a three-day activation delay, a 49-day escalation interval, and the fixed-point required-support curve. Change the configuration, then choose an outcome and requested deposit to see clipping, tie adjustment, acceptance, or rejection. Deposit result accepted: 1 REP Contract-model state activation pending Game settings Start bond 1 REP Non-decision threshold 10 REP Player positions Current Yes balance 1 REP Current No balance 1 REP Current Invalid balance 0 REP Player move Deposit outcome Yes No Invalid Requested deposit 1 REP Timeline Days since start 0 days Yes 1 → 2 REP No 1 → 1 REP Invalid 0 → 0 REP Configured start bond 1 REP Configured threshold 10 REP Projected leader Yes Projected median balance 1 REP Deposit before → after Yes 1 / 2 REP; No 1 / 1 REP; Invalid 0 / 0 REP Activation 3 days Scheduled deadline (before → after) day 3.0 → day 3.0","title":"Escalation Game","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"payouts","heading":"How the game ends","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/escalation-game.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How REP deposits resolve a market locally, when they force a fork, and how games continue across forks.","text":"The game ends in one of three ways: Strict local winner. A unique leading outcome can finalize once the scheduled deadline has passed and required support has overtaken binding capital, provided no fork interrupted the game. Local non-decision. Two outcomes reach the non-decision threshold. Statoblast uses its fork path instead of choosing one locally. Interruption by another fork. The pool's universe forks while the game remains unresolved. The game has no local result and continues in the child pools instead; see forks and migration . Settlement rewards the side that held its ground. Winning deposits get their principal back plus a bonus. The runner-up's stake, the binding capital, pays for it: three fifths becomes the bonus pool and two fifths, the escalation haircut , is burned, unless the fork this game triggered already burned it as the fork haircut . As ordinary reports increase the smallest of the three outcome balances, that increase leaves dispute escrow. The portfolio distributes it as separate REP income . The other two balances remain in escrow to cover any winning outcome. Final settlement burns any extra beyond its reserved liabilities; a game carried across a fork also burns its terminal residual. The bonus is only available on the portion of winning deposits up to the reward-eligible cap , one and a half times binding capital; deposits beyond that cap simply get their principal back. Settlement also compares the universe's current fork threshold, in full, with the non-decision threshold the game fixed when it opened. If the current fork threshold is the lower of the two, every winning withdrawal is multiplied by the current fork threshold divided by that non-decision threshold, which can push the final transfer below principal. The comparison is skipped when the universe forked after the game ended. The EscalationGame reference works one payout through with exact numbers.","title":"Escalation Game","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"payouts","heading":"Escalation game continuation","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/escalation-game.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How REP deposits resolve a market locally, when they force a fork, and how games continue across forks.","text":"When a fork interrupts an unresolved game, Statoblast's SecurityPoolForker restarts it in each child pool as a carried game with the parent's balances and elapsed time. In a child created by a fork on the pool's own question, the outcome is already fixed to that child's outcome, so the game only settles deposits on it. After an unrelated fork, the game resumes and can still be contested, with new wallet REP deposits. Depositors claim their positions in a child with a Merkle Mountain Range carry proof against the parent's deposit history, so no child copies every deposit onchain.","title":"Escalation Game","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"payouts","heading":"Design rationale","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/escalation-game.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How REP deposits resolve a market locally, when they force a fork, and how games continue across forks.","text":"Augur V2 cancelled active dispute games when a fork began and refunded their participants. Statoblast instead continues active games across the fork so a fork cannot erase the stakes players already put at risk. See the research note Zoltar forks should not cancel escalation games for the historical motivation.","title":"Escalation Game","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["liquidation","underwriting commitment","backing constraints","operator","receiver vault","approval","reservation","bad debt"],"path":"explanation/liquidations.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Liquidation eligibility, roles, REP awards, bounded receiver approvals, and reservation lifecycle.","text":"Liquidation transfers an underbacked owner's commitment and a quoted REP award to a funded receiver. Only the target's principal pays the award; other owners do not absorb its loss.","title":"Vault liquidation","topic":"Economics","weight":1},{"fragment":"position-model","heading":"Target, receiver, and keeper","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/liquidations.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Liquidation eligibility, roles, REP awards, bounded receiver approvals, and reservation lifecycle.","text":"The target owns an underbacked position. The receiver accepts its commitment and must back the resulting position at the receiver's chosen health target. A keeper can be the receiver and earn the REP award. There is no separate ETH execution reserve or protocol-funded gas reimbursement.","title":"Vault liquidation","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"capacity-and-health","heading":"When a position is liquidatable","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/liquidations.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Liquidation eligibility, roles, REP awards, bounded receiver approvals, and reservation lifecycle.","text":"All free and committed REP deposited for the owner in this portfolio universe counts toward its backing. Earned REP income is separate. The owner's voluntary health target controls new exposure and surplus withdrawals; merely falling below that buffer does not trigger liquidation. The protocol compares backing with the commitment's required REP at the shared oracle's liquidation price, using the protective edge of its error band. Admission uses the opposite, conservative edge. An accepted adverse observation starts the takeover clock automatically. The award grows according to the indexed takeover rules, without a keeper transaction to start the clock.","title":"Vault liquidation","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"rule","heading":"Cure before takeover","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/liquidations.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Liquidation eligibility, roles, REP awards, bounded receiver approvals, and reservation lifecycle.","text":"The owner can deposit REP or reduce commitment. Removing unbacked commitment uses spare capacity above demand, so a cure cannot erase outstanding trader claims. A takeover first performs the available cure, then quotes the remaining obligation and award. The receiver supplies any missing backing. Minimum commitment and award bounds prevent involuntary dust execution, and caller limits constrain the REP the receiver contributes. Existing earned fees remain with their owner. Underbacked commitment stops earning new fees. Principal, fees, and reporter claims use separate accounting. See portfolio capital and retirement for exits and the liquidation reference for exact contract bounds.","title":"Vault liquidation","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"receiver-approvals","heading":"Receiver consent","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/liquidations.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Liquidation eligibility, roles, REP awards, bounded receiver approvals, and reservation lifecycle.","text":"The receiver calls the capital ledger directly. Bounded takeover calls pin the accepted price epoch, target health, source cohort, maximum resulting principal and commitment, minimum REP award, and maximum own REP contribution.","title":"Vault liquidation","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"queue-semantics","heading":"Execution and incentives","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/liquidations.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Liquidation eligibility, roles, REP awards, bounded receiver approvals, and reservation lifecycle.","text":"Quotes are checked against live state in the transaction. A stale quote or insufficient backing reverts atomically. Keepers maintain their own indexes and choose profitable targets; no onchain scan of all owners is required. A small or illiquid REP award may not attract a keeper, so liquidation liveness depends on economically viable participation.","title":"Vault liquidation","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"fork-behavior","heading":"Fork isolation","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/liquidations.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Liquidation eligibility, roles, REP awards, bounded receiver approvals, and reservation lifecycle.","text":"Positions belong to one portfolio and universe. A fork stops ordinary parent operations. Each owner chooses a branch during the shared choice window; the receiver in a child must authorize its own child-universe exposure. See shared fork choices .","title":"Vault liquidation","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["fees","open interest","retention","utilization","vaults"],"path":"explanation/fees.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why vaults earn a fee on open interest, how utilization sets the rate, and when accrual stops.","text":"Each SecurityPool charges an open-interest fee against its outstanding settlement collateral and pays it to the vaults that underwrite that collateral. Every portfolio uses funded linear fee streams so owners receive fees when they are earned.","title":"Open-interest fees","topic":"Economics","weight":1},{"fragment":"why-fees-exist","heading":"Why the fee exists","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/fees.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why vaults earn a fee on open interest, how utilization sets the rate, and when accrual stops.","text":"Vault owners provide REP-backed security, keep their vaults healthy, and may need to take part in reporting and migration. In return, each fee-eligible underwriting commitment earns a share of the fee in proportion to its size. Owners claim aggregate earnings from the portfolio without visiting each question.","title":"Open-interest fees","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"portfolio-fees","heading":"Fees in a shared portfolio","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/fees.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why vaults earn a fee on open interest, how utilization sets the rate, and when accrual stops.","text":"A portfolio holds actual question collateral in a shared settlement contract. Each question has a linear fee stream, and an aggregate expiry index advances all streams without visiting them individually. Fees reduce both funded collateral and its internal accounting units. Outcome token supply remains separate, so a fee changes each remaining token's redemption value. The creator fixes one annual rate, applied to each question's outstanding principal basis. It does not compound against shrinking collateral. Minting and redemption preserve that basis per remaining share. Fees continue after the question's calendar end until reporting starts or the fee budget above the backing floor runs out. Stale-price intervals and recovery books do not charge ordinary holding fees, and later price recovery never backcharges those intervals. The creator also fixes minimumOutcomeRate , the minimum settlement units reserved per outcome share, scaled by 10 18 . The UI defaults to 0.01, or 1% of the initial rate. Holding fees can consume only the units above that rounded-up reserve. The stream ends before another whole second would cross it; no question scan or keeper action is needed. Purchases and redemptions remain proportional at the floor. For example, 1,000 complete sets retain at least 10 ETH in a fully funded book with a 0.01 floor. This caps ordinary fee income; it does not release the remaining underwriting obligation or waive purchase fees and the prepaid resolution premium. Providers must account for a possible period without holding-fee income before redemption or finality. The floor protects against fee depletion, not an underfunded fork: recovery claims may pay less ETH plus REP, and tiny claims still face token-unit rounding. Each purchase also prepays 52 days of fees on its added principal: the maximum ordinary three-day reporting window plus seven-week extension. This resolution premium stays with the question. Once reporting starts, providers earn only the portion corresponding to time the question remains unresolved, up to the prepaid amount. An expiry index stops those earnings at finality without requiring an owner or keeper to visit each question. Unused premium is burned when the question becomes final or all trader complete sets are returned, because the contract does not record which buyer paid it. The premium is nonrefundable; later providers cannot collect an already resolved question’s remaining budget. Before collateral, price or commitment changes, the capital ledger credits earlier income under the old eligibility weights. An owner's entire deposited principal determines health; separately claimable earnings do not count as backing. Each healthy commitment earns its fraction of total outstanding commitment, including unhealthy commitment in the denominator. A tiny healthy position therefore cannot collect every unhealthy owner's income. Deposits without commitment earn no underwriting income, and retirement positions keep earning while their healthy commitment remains outstanding. Every purchase pays the same configurable backing-based fee, including when capacity is reused. The whole backing fee vests over the configured reward duration, as does recognized REP income. These vesting rewards compensate the healthy commitments serving when each portion unlocks, even if the original question has already resolved. A provider who retires gives up future unearned portions. Income released without eligible backing is not saved for a future provider. Unassigned ETH and REP are burned. No shared budget pays keepers or price sponsors: whoever needs a fresh price pays for it, and the owner of an unhealthy position, or a taker that cures it first, pays for the cure . Owners collect their own aggregate ETH and REP earnings without visiting questions. There is no separate ETH reporting charge or bonus. Anyone can report by staking REP in the Escalation Game , whose REP payout rules still apply. Shareholders have an incentive to resolve claims, and backing providers have an incentive to release occupied capacity. These incentives do not guarantee prompt reporting: participants can wait for someone else to pay gas, and a small or abandoned question may remain unresolved. A fork preserves the dates of existing purchase-fee and REP-income streams. If the parent price was fresh at the fork, eligible frozen commitments continue earning them. Parent income that cannot be assigned is burned; principal and earned claims are excluded. Question premiums follow the chosen commitment’s branch allocation. A question’s remaining funded premium can support its child reporting period; repeated forks do not create additional funding. Premium elapsed while the child is inactive is burned, so delaying activation cannot create retroactive provider income. The portfolio reference specifies these custody boundaries.","title":"Open-interest fees","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["collateral repair auction","clearing","settlement","repair"],"path":"explanation/collateral-repair-auctions.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Auction clearing, settlement, and weak-demand loss allocation.","text":"When a universe forks, each Statoblast portfolio prepares branches, one per child universe ( forks and migration ). Each owner chooses one branch for its REP and its commitment-weighted share of the portfolio’s settlement collateral , so a child receives only the collateral of the vaults that chose it. Suppose the parent held 100 ETH of settlement collateral when it forked and a child receives only 70 ETH through migration. The child inherits the parent's full share supply, so its Collateral Repair Auction tries to raise the missing 30 ETH by selling child REP. Full repair: bidders contribute 30 ETH, so the child reaches its 100 ETH target. Partial repair: bidders contribute 12 ETH, so the child operates with 82 ETH and remains impaired. No demand: no qualifying bid contributes ETH, so the child remains at 70 ETH. The auction repairs collateral and never selects truth. It sells REP belonging to an already-defined child universe. The child resumes with only the ETH that migration and accepted bids actually provide.","title":"Collateral Repair Auctions","topic":"Economics","weight":1},{"fragment":"portfolio-auctions","heading":"Shared portfolio auctions","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/collateral-repair-auctions.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Auction clearing, settlement, and weak-demand loss allocation.","text":"A portfolio shares backing across several questions. It holds one auction for the branch’s combined ETH shortfall. Only freshly unchosen provider REP can be sold; chosen providers’ backing and REP already owed to older outcome claims are excluded. A fixed one-question portfolio uses this same auction. Reporter escrow is never auction inventory. A bidder supplies only the ETH for whole auction lots. No REP deposit or approval is required. Each bid specifies the least REP acceptable per lot and a personal health target. That minimum REP must itself fully back the lot’s ETH commitment at the opening conservative price and chosen target. Winning bidders automatically become vault owners, with commitment equal to their accepted bid ETH. Each bid has a separate vault account controlled by its bidder, a small clone of one shared implementation whose owner, ledger, auction and bid are fixed at creation. Lower REP-per-lot bids offer more ETH for the same REP, so they rank first. The auction accepts the largest number of lots that fit both its ETH target and the available REP at the marginal winner’s minimum: Target reached: each winning lot receives the marginal winner’s minimum REP. Unsold REP remains with inherited claims. Positive fill below target: all fresh auction inventory is sold, including when REP availability limits the fill. Winners receive the same grid-rounded REP backing per lot; the indivisible remainder belongs only to winning bids as REP credits. No feasible lot: no REP is sold, and the inventory remains available for claim recovery. Equal bids at the marginal bid limit share the remaining lots proportionally, with cumulative rounding to whole lots. That bid limit still determines which bids share a partial fill when the final REP per lot is higher. The contract reference defines the exact grid and rounding rules. A new bid is rejected if strictly better funded bids already make winning impossible. A bid that could win when submitted can recover its full ETH before closing once later bids permanently rule it out. Anyone can credit that refund; the bidder then withdraws it and decides how to use the ETH, or credits and withdraws in one transaction. That combined withdrawal pays the bidder’s whole refund credit, including refunds already credited for its other bids, and skips a credit someone else already made. A provisional zero fill is not enough: equal-price rounding can change as more bids arrive. Bids that could still win remain binding until closing, and bids cannot be repriced or cancelled. At closing, winning backing and commitments enter capital accounting immediately. A shared position preserves each bid’s funded obligations and fee rights until an owner or keeper splits it out. Delaying that transaction cannot omit a winner from aggregate backing or a later fork snapshot. Refunds are independent of that step. Keepers discover unmaterialized winners directly through the auction tree, skipping losing bids and positions already split out. The controlling wallet must still choose a branch before the next fork-choice deadline. Choices close at week eight and bidding at week nine, even if preparation starts late. Funded prices must cover the maximum auction exposure and fresh inventory. Missing coverage or freshness at closing, or a higher closing conservative REP/ETH price than at opening, cancels the sale. Accepted ETH otherwise repairs inherited claims. Remaining recovery assets or a cash deficit keep the branch in runoff: existing claims can settle, but new purchases and ordinary holding fees stay closed. There is no seller price floor. With weak demand, one funded lot can buy all fresh inventory. Revenue therefore depends on competitive entry; selling everything does not guarantee full repair. Winners take ordinary vault obligations and liquidation risk, without a minimum holding period. Surplus withdrawals and retirement against spare healthy capacity can release REP, so a full sale does not guarantee permanent security. Keeper participation depends on REP rewards covering execution costs.","title":"Collateral Repair Auctions","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["OpenOracle","price","coordinator","callback"],"path":"explanation/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why a contestable REP/ETH price guards solvency-sensitive operations, and where it stops.","text":"Explanation Statoblast needs a fresh REP/ETH exchange rate (quoted as REP per ETH) when it measures whether REP adequately secures ETH obligations. OpenOracle supplies an economically contestable price. A reporter posts a WETH/REP position at the claimed price, and anyone who believes the price is wrong can replace the report by trading against that position, so an inaccurate report is a profit opportunity. OpenOracle never decides whether a prediction-market answer is Yes, No, or Invalid.","title":"Why Statoblast uses OpenOracle","topic":"Protocol","weight":1},{"fragment":"protected-operations","heading":"Protected operations","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why a contestable REP/ETH price guards solvency-sensitive operations, and where it stops.","text":"A shared portfolio feed protects commitment growth, surplus REP withdrawals, purchases and takeovers. The question coordinator is an adapter to that feed. Each capital or settlement operation still checks its own live safety conditions. Reporter deposits use separate REP custody.","title":"Why Statoblast uses OpenOracle","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"cached-and-fresh","heading":"Cached and fresh prices","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why a contestable REP/ETH price guards solvency-sensitive operations, and where it stops.","text":"An accepted report supplies a shared price for a limited freshness window. A sponsor can fund a new independent candidate when it expires. Child universes need their own accepted reports; a copied numeric price is not fresh evidence.","title":"Why Statoblast uses OpenOracle","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"example","heading":"One request, dispute, and settlement","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why a contestable REP/ETH price guards solvency-sensitive operations, and where it stops.","text":"Alice wants to increase her commitment while the shared REP/ETH price is stale. She funds an independent report. Bob can trade against an inaccurate report before it settles; a dispute restarts its settlement clock. Once a valid report settles and is accepted, Alice submits her capital change with explicit bounds. Report settlement does not execute a queued vault operation. A multi-question portfolio shares one accepted price but allows independent reports at the same time. Each report has its own funding escrow, so a disputed report cannot hold another sponsor’s released funds. Capital operations execute separately once a valid shared price is available. Growing underwriting also requires enough challenged report funding; a settled larger report can support an owner’s entry even after a smaller renewal, while retaining that report’s conservative collateral requirement for the owner. The shared coverage reference gives the exact limits.","title":"Why Statoblast uses OpenOracle","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"limitations","heading":"Security limitations","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why a contestable REP/ETH price guards solvency-sensitive operations, and where it stops.","text":"OpenOracle relies on participants noticing and profitably correcting inaccurate reports. Congestion, censorship, incorrect gas assumptions, insufficient economic incentives, or repeated disputes can delay or undermine that process. A fresh price is not a guarantee that an operation will succeed, and a settled price says nothing about the market question's outcome. Portfolios constrain capital growth by challenged report funding, but this does not guarantee an accurate price. The oracle does not impose an absolute deadline on a report game: each valid dispute restarts its settlement clock. Independent candidates can proceed independently when another report remains disputed; their acceptance still depends on a correction opportunity and timely settlement. Each report is sized from a gas-cost assumption. The priority fee in that assumption is fixed when the origin pool is created and inherited by its children; it is not observed from the request block. If prevailing priority fees are higher, the modeled incentive for a disputer is weaker; a value set too high raises the sponsor's initial WETH and REP requirement and can make requests impractical. The OpenOracle integration reference specifies funding formulas, report sizing, sponsor rules, callbacks, recovery paths, configuration, and a report-sizing calculator. The security model states the participation assumptions.","title":"Why Statoblast uses OpenOracle","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["bots","liquidator","arbitrager","augurScan","research","README"],"path":"reference/further-reading.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Operator, indexer, test, and research material that lives beside the code.","text":"Reference This site covers the protocol. The material below lives next to the code it describes and is not searchable from here. Each link opens the current version on GitHub.","title":"Further reading","topic":"Ecosystem","weight":1},{"fragment":"operators","heading":"Run something","keywords":[],"path":"reference/further-reading.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Operator, indexer, test, and research material that lives beside the code.","text":"Document Who it is for What it covers Statoblast liquidator Vault operators and keepers Monitoring pool health, staging and executing liquidations, and the bot's dashboard. OpenOracle arbitrager Price correctors Finding mispriced REP/ETH reports, funding disputes against Uniswap quotes, executor deployment, and settlement tracking. Chaos bot and its operator reference Testnet operators Exercising permissionless protocol paths with real transactions, safety limits, and runtime controls. augurScan , its operations guide , API reference , and state model Indexer operators and integrators The read-only protocol explorer: deployment, backup and upgrade, the HTTP API, and how retained evidence differs from the current view. Local test network and Sepolia Reth node Developers and node operators Docker Compose setups for the repository-pinned Anvil chain and a pruned Sepolia execution and consensus pair.","title":"Further reading","topic":"Ecosystem","weight":0},{"fragment":"code","heading":"Read the code","keywords":[],"path":"reference/further-reading.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Operator, indexer, test, and research material that lives beside the code.","text":"Document Who it is for What it covers Repository README Contributors Setup, local development, simulation mode, common commands, and validation. Contract layout and the Statoblast contract directory Auditors and contract developers Why source paths are stable, which directory owns which contracts, and the OpenOracle vendoring boundary. Security regression suite Auditors The focused tests that reproduce past security findings and how to add one. Shared TypeScript packages and core UI ownership Front-end and tooling contributors Package boundaries, build dependencies, and which layer may import which. Statoblast test slices Contract developers How the split Statoblast suites are run individually and together. Shared portfolio fork workflow tests Front-end contributors How the split security-pool workflow suites of the Statoblast UI are run individually and together. Token authorization provenance Auditors Where the EIP-712 and ECDSA signature helpers come from and what was changed. augurScan backend boundaries Indexer contributors Which backend directory owns HTTP, scanning, storage, and evidence types. UI features , UI protocol client , and UI copy modules UI contributors Where feature components, typed contract reads and writes, and user-facing text live, and which may import which. Visual foundation UI contributors The shared design tokens, product accents, and status treatments used by every interface.","title":"Further reading","topic":"Ecosystem","weight":0},{"fragment":"research","heading":"Understand the design history","keywords":[],"path":"reference/further-reading.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Operator, indexer, test, and research material that lives beside the code.","text":"The oracle-research repository holds the proposals, rejected alternatives, and open questions behind the protocol. It explains why the design looks the way it does; the deployed contracts and this site's reference pages describe what it actually does today.","title":"Further reading","topic":"Ecosystem","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["OpenOracle","parameters","funding","callbacks","estimators"],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"OpenOracle supplies a fresh REP/ETH price for Statoblast's solvency-sensitive operations. It does not decide market truth; local Escalation Games and Zoltar remain responsible for truth and branching. The OpenOracle explanation covers why Statoblast uses a contestable price and which operations depend on it. This page specifies the integration: formulas, parameters, and settlement-callback behavior. Per-function callers, preconditions, and events are in the coordinator contract reference .","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":1},{"fragment":"openoracle-role","heading":"Token pair and cached price","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"Statoblast configures WETH as token1 and REP as token2 . Keeping WETH on the exact side lets the coordinator size the report directly from ETH-denominated gas costs without first assuming a REP/ETH conversion price. When a report settles, the coordinator converts the settled token amounts into REP per ETH: lastPrice = amount2 ⋅ 10 18 amount1 The settled REP amount ( amount2 ) is scaled by the fixed price precision and divided by the settled WETH amount ( amount1 ). This raw-amount ratio is valid because the configured WETH and REP contracts both use 18 decimals. The coordinator does not normalize token decimals in the settlement callback. An accepted report becomes the coordinator's cached REP/ETH price. lastSettlementTimestamp records settlement eligibility: the final report timestamp plus settlementTime . The freshness window starts there, so settling late cannot extend it. The shared feed freshness window is settlementTime plus one hour on Sepolia or five minutes on other chains. With the mainnet eight-minute challenge period, a shared price is usable for 780 seconds after eligibility, or at most 21 minutes after the final report. priceValidUntil() returns the canonical expiry; a price is stale at that timestamp. The coordinator does not meter operation volume during the window. Shared feeds size reports and escalation halts against the largest of current portfolio commitment, backing demand and the maximum commitment a preparing branch auction can add to chosen backing. The feed reads these aggregates without scanning questions or bidders. Unused capacity from an oversized earlier report does not increase the minimum size of the next report. A shared feed permits concurrent candidates, including renewal before the accepted price expires. Each candidate has its own SharedPoolReportEscrow , which holds its reporter identity and funding credits. pendingReportId and the associated pending getters identify only the latest candidate; they do not enumerate or exclude other pending candidates. A pending, disputed or rejected report never extends accepted-price freshness. Shared acceptance atomically checkpoints portfolio fees under the previous price and updates capital eligibility. A failed mandatory checkpoint rolls back acceptance. Capital operations execute separately and recheck current safety constraints against the accepted price.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"portfolio-preparation-reports","heading":"Portfolio branch preparation","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"SecurityPoolFactory.preparePortfolioPriceFeed(sourcePool, child) prepares the canonical child feed without deploying a child question. sourcePool must be a canonical question pool already admitted to this portfolio, with its portfolio binding recorded by the factory. The configuration seed is itself a canonical portfolio question. SecurityPoolPortfolio.isPriceReportingOpen(child) permits requests and acceptance after startBranch , until the fixed native recapitalization deadline at week nine. From that deadline until activation, the accepted observation is frozen. Reporting reopens on activation if the child universe has not forked; a forked universe rejects new prices. Late branch preparation does not extend the window. An accepted preparation report caches the price and funded exposure; it does not activate trading, start holding fees or start imported positions’ liquidation history. Activation uses the cached price only while it remains fresh. A stale or absent price does not block branch activation. Callbacks during a closed window reject the candidate price while preserving report funding recovery .","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"shared-report-coverage","heading":"Shared report coverage and capital prices","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"During auction preparation, minimumToken2ReportAttoRep() requires one hundredth of fresh sale inventory, rounded up. minimumToken1ReportAttoEthForPrice(price) also raises the WETH minimum enough to fund that REP amount at the proposed price. Direct sponsorship through requestPortfolioPrice rejects a signed WETH budget below the required amount. Acceptance checks both sides: acceptedRepExposureLimitAttoRep is final REP funding times 100. An arbitrarily cheap proposed REP price therefore cannot remove the REP inventory challenge requirement. A canonical acceptance sets acceptedExposureLimitAttoEth to (final WETH − priority-fee report component) × 100 and increments acceptedEpoch . An ordinary setCommitment raise cannot lift aggregate exposure above that accepted limit. Pending funding grants no capacity. To raise a commitment, an owner can instead call setCommitmentWithReport(reportId, commitment) with an independently settled, fresh candidate whose final funding covers the projected total exposure. reportAuthorization(reportId, newExposure) validates that proof even if a newer, smaller canonical report superseded it. For accepted price q , target error e , and percentage precision P = 10,000,000 , the upper bound is q × P / (P − e) , rounded up, and the liquidation bound is q × P / (P + e) , rounded down. Capital entry, withdrawals, capacity and fee eligibility use the conservative upper bound; rewarded adverse actions use the lower bound. Proof authorization also retains the higher of the owner’s current conservative price and the proof’s upper bound for that owner’s collateral checks until a later canonical epoch covers the enlarged live exposure. Selecting a smaller report cannot remove this owner-specific floor.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"optional-upstream-capabilities","heading":"Report flags","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"The coordinator always submits flags 7 : FLAG_TIME_TYPE ( 1 , a timestamp clock), FLAG_TRACK_DISPUTES ( 2 , per-dispute history), and FLAG_STORE_ALL ( 4 , the complete report state in storage). It never sets the optional flags 8 , 16 , 32 , or 64 ; the OpenOracle provenance record identifies the vendored source and build profile.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"statoblast-integration","heading":"Integration architecture","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"Each question has an OpenOraclePriceCoordinator adapter attached to one SharedPoolPriceFeed per portfolio and universe. The feed owns the accepted REP/ETH price and freshness clock. Every candidate has its own escrow. The factory authenticates coordinator creation and binds each question once. A child universe gets a new feed for its own REP token, initially with no accepted price. Adding a question does not reset an existing feed. Capital deposits, commitments, retirement and liquidation use the portfolio directly; they are not queued by the coordinator.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"report-lifecycle","heading":"Price request lifecycle","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"The sponsor approves WETH and universe REP, chooses a candidate price and report budget, and opens a report through a question coordinator or the shared feed. OpenOracle accepts valid corrections during the challenge window. A correction replaces the current reporter and restarts settlement timing. After the challenge window, anyone can settle the report. Its isolated escrow validates the result and offers it to the shared feed. The feed accepts only a qualifying, fresh observation with sufficient economic coverage. Rejected candidates cannot invalidate the previously accepted observation. Owners and keepers submit capital operations separately. Each transaction rechecks current state, price bounds, coverage and authorization. Escalation reports use wallet REP in separate reporter custody. They do not consume underwriting REP or use an oracle operation queue.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"request-funding","heading":"Funding and report ownership","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"The sponsor funds its own WETH, REP and transaction gas. The minimum public settlement bounty is 101 attoETH ; it does not reimburse the sponsor’s gas. The sponsor may supply a larger bounty. requestPriceCostAttoEth = 101 REQUEST_BOUNTY_OFFSET_ATTO_ETH is 101 attoETH . The shared callback budget is 4,000,000 gas ; callers pay their own transaction gas. requestPortfolioPrice(proposedRepPerEthPrice, requestedInitialAttoWeth, bountyAttoEth) transfers exactly the reviewed WETH budget and its corresponding REP from the caller. It rejects a budget below the inclusion-time minimum instead of silently increasing it. Only the recorded sponsor receives that candidate’s returned funding. Portfolio settlement collateral does not fund reports. A coordinator request forwards the specified bounty and refunds excess native ETH to the caller. Rejecting that refund reverts the whole request. A direct feed request requires exactly its specified bounty.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"rolling-dispute-exclusivity","heading":"Independent candidates","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"There is no exclusive sponsor lane. Anyone may fund another candidate while earlier candidates are pending or the accepted price is still fresh. Disputing one candidate cannot reserve all reporting access.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"security-guarantee","heading":"Report sizing and correction incentives","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"The coordinator sizes each report so that correcting a price error of at least targetPriceErrorForDispute stays profitable after OpenOracle's fees and the modeled gas cost of one dispute multiplied by openOracleSecurityMultiplierBps . That multiplier is separate from a SecurityPool's backing multiplier, statoblastSecurityMultiplierBps . A report can be corrected in either direction. The formula assumes the less profitable one, in which the true REP/ETH price is (1 + targetError) times the reported price, and models the correction as releasing the fraction (targetError - fees) / (1 + targetError) of the WETH position before gas. The required position is the multiplied gas cost divided by that fraction. The minimum initial WETH position also has a floor of 1% of the portfolio’s aggregate exposure ( aggregateExposureAttoEth() ), and the same floor applies to the escalation halt: reportAttoEth ( gasPriceAttoEthPerGas ) = ⌈ gasPriceAttoEthPerGas ⋅ gasUnitsForOneDispute ⋅ openOracleSecurityMultiplierBps ⋅ ( percentagePrecision + targetPriceErrorForDispute ) 10000 ⋅ ( targetPriceErrorForDispute - protocolFee - feePercentage ) ⌉ exposureReportAttoEth = ⌈ aggregateExposureAttoEth 100 ⌉ minimumToken1ReportAttoEth = reportAttoEth ( initialReportPriorityFeeAttoEthPerGas ) + max ( reportAttoEth ( block.basefee ) , exposureReportAttoEth ) escalationHaltAttoEth = max ( ⌊ initialWethReportAttoEth ⋅ escalationHaltMultiplierBps 10000 ⌋ , exposureReportAttoEth ) All amounts are in attoETH. percentagePrecision is 10,000,000 , the denominator of targetPriceErrorForDispute , protocolFee , and feePercentage . This is the ETH-exposure floor. The feed also requires REP-side funding of at least 1% of its REP exposure. At a proposed price, converting that REP minimum to ETH can increase the required initial WETH above this floor. The coordinator sets initialWethReportAttoEth = max(minimumToken1ReportAttoEthForPrice(proposedRepPerEthPrice), requestedInitialAttoWeth) , uses that value as OpenOracle's currentAmount1 , and derives amount2 = ⌈initialWethReportAttoEth * proposedRepPerEthPrice / 1e18⌉ in the same transaction.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"formula-inputs","heading":"Formula inputs","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"Default report configuration comes from shared/statoblast/ts/initialReport/oracleInitialReport.ts and is copied into the shared feed configuration. Formula input Current value Source block.basefee Current request block EVM block context initialReportPriorityFeeAttoEthPerGas Origin-pool parameter; the UI defaults to 10 nanoETH Immutable lineage configuration inherited by child pools aggregateExposureAttoEth Current aggregate portfolio exposure Configured security pool OPEN_INTEREST_DIVIDER 100 (a 1% floor) Coordinator constant gasUnitsForOneDispute 300,000 gas Factory constructor; shared deployment configuration ORACLE_GAS_UNITS_FOR_ONE_DISPUTE openOracleSecurityMultiplierBps 100,000 bps ( 10x ) Factory constructor; shared deployment configuration OPEN_ORACLE_SECURITY_MULTIPLIER_BPS targetPriceErrorForDispute 500,000 / 10,000,000 ( 5% ) Factory constructor; shared deployment configuration ORACLE_TARGET_PRICE_ERROR_FOR_DISPUTE protocolFee 100,000 / 10,000,000 ( 1% ) Factory constructor; shared deployment configuration ORACLE_PROTOCOL_FEE feePercentage (OpenOracle's reporter fee) 10,000 / 10,000,000 ( 0.1% ) Factory constructor; shared deployment configuration ORACLE_FEE_PERCENTAGE escalationHaltMultiplierBps 100,000 bps ( 10x ) Factory constructor; shared deployment configuration ORACLE_ESCALATION_HALT_MULTIPLIER_BPS","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"security-guarantee","heading":"Construction bounds and parameter effects","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"Coordinator construction requires: positive dispute gas units positive request gas: gasConsumedSettlement or gasConsumedOpenOracleReportPrice is nonzero a nonzero initialReportPriorityFeeAttoEthPerGas a target error no greater than 100%; shared feeds additionally require it to be strictly below 100% an OpenOracle security multiplier of at least 1x and at most type(uint256).max / (percentagePrecision + targetPriceErrorForDispute) combined OpenOracle fees below the target error a nonzero escalationHaltMultiplierBps a timestamp clock with dispute tracking ( timeType and trackDisputes both true) maxSettlementBaseFeeMultiplierBps of at least 1x Construction also bounds initialReportPriorityFeeAttoEthPerGas so that the priority-fee component of the minimum report, reportAttoEth(initialReportPriorityFeeAttoEthPerGas) , does not exceed half of min(type(uint128).max, type(uint128).max × 10000 / escalationHaltMultiplierBps) ; a larger fee reverts with Initial report priority fee exceeds OpenOracle limits . OpenOraclePriceCoordinatorFactory stores the shared configuration without validating it, so an invalid configuration deploys the factory successfully and makes every later coordinator deployment revert. At a 30 nanoETH base fee, a configured 10 nanoETH priority fee, and 100 WETH of aggregate exposure, the modeled gas cost of one dispute is 0.012 ETH , the minimum report is 3.230769230769230770 WETH , and the escalation halt is 32.307692307692307700 WETH .","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"initial-report-estimator-example","heading":"Dynamic WETH report estimator","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"This calculator models the ETH-exposure floor, excluding the separate REP-inventory minimum. The deployment parameters and caller-selected amount are shown as adjustable inputs to make their effect explicit. For a deployed coordinator, the base fee and aggregate exposure can change from one request block to another. Selected initial WETH 3.2308 WETH Fee bound fees below target error Network costs Block base fee 30 nanoETH Initial-report priority fee 10 nanoETH Gas units for one dispute 300,000 gas Pool security Aggregate portfolio exposure 100 WETH OpenOracle security multiplier 10.0x Target wrong-price error 5.0% Oracle fees OpenOracle protocol fee 1.0% OpenOracle reporter fee ( feePercentage ) 0.1% Coordinator policy Escalation halt multiplier 10x Report request Requested initial WETH 0.00 WETH Initial-derived halt 32.307692307692307700 WETH Aggregate-exposure halt floor 1.000000000000000000 WETH Minimum token1 report 3.230769230769230770 WETH Escalation halt 32.307692307692307700 WETH Modeled dispute gas cost 0.012000 ETH Buffered gas target 0.120000 ETH Correction profit fraction after fees 3.7143%","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"callback-rejection-and-recovery","heading":"Settlement validation, rejection, and recovery","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"callback-rejection-and-recovery","heading":"Low-level callback failure","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"OpenOracle invokes the price source through a low-level callback whose success is intentionally neither stored nor emitted. A failed callback does not automatically undo an otherwise valid OpenOracle settlement. A shared escrow authenticates OpenOracle and its own report ID; the feed accepts settlement only from that candidate’s registered escrow. Failure can leave an individually settled candidate unprocessed without blocking other candidates. If the settlement transaction cannot retain enough gas headroom after the callback attempt, OpenOracle reverts settlement with InvalidGasLimit . In that case, the report has not settled and coordinator recovery is not yet available.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"settlement-validation-scope","heading":"Price-source settlement validation","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"A callback that successfully enters the price source can still produce a rejected price. The price source emits PriceReportRejected and leaves the price cache unchanged when the first applicable condition below holds. A shared rejection records reportOutcome(reportId) = 2 and clears the latest-candidate getters only if they still name that report: for a shared feed, the portfolio reporting window is closed: Portfolio reporting window closed ; the settlement block’s base fee exceeds the request-time cap: the feed uses request block.basefee + initialReportPriorityFeeAttoEthPerGas . This is scaled by maxSettlementBaseFeeMultiplierBps / 10000 : Base fee too high ; storedGame(reportId).numReports is saturated at the uint24 maximum: Counter saturated ; numReports is zero, or the settled WETH amount is below reportAttoEth(initialReportPriorityFeeAttoEthPerGas) + reportAttoEth(recordedBaseFee) from the sizing formula , where recordedBaseFee comes from the final disputeHistory(reportId, numReports - 1) . Shared mode also requires the amount above the priority-fee component to cover live aggregate exposure / 100 , rounded up: Report uneconomic ; for a shared feed, settlement eligibility is earlier than the accepted observation ( Report timestamp not newer ), or equal without larger funded coverage ( Report adds no newer observation or capacity ); the freshness window measured from settlement eligibility has already closed: Report stale ; either settled token amount is zero: Empty oracle settlement ; the integer REP/ETH calculation produces a zero price: Oracle price is zero . These checks establish only that the final WETH position meets the modeled security floor and that the callback data is structurally acceptable. They do not prove that the accepted price is externally correct, that an independent corrector exists, or that a correcting transaction will obtain inclusion.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"callback-rejection-and-recovery","heading":"Rejected-price cleanup","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"A rejected candidate leaves the accepted price unchanged. It cannot cancel other reports or reserve execution rights. Capital transactions remain subject to their own fresh state checks.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"callback-rejection-and-recovery","heading":"Recovering a settled report after callback failure","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"Anyone can recover a settled candidate through its report escrow. Shared feeds expose recoverReport(reportId) for any unprocessed settled candidate; recoverSettledPendingReport() is only a convenience for the latest ID. Shared recovery marks that candidate rejected and releases its escrow credits without accepting a price. Independently, anyone may call an escrow’s withdrawFunding() to deliver already-released credits to its original sponsor, including credits released by a dispute before settlement. Each escrow isolates those credits from other reports.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"intentional-economic-tradeoffs","heading":"Liveness and exposure limits","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"Capital growth requires the accepted coverage or an independently funded proof . The feed does not impose an absolute deadline on an individual dispute game; shared candidates can proceed independently when another game remains disputed. See excluded guarantees EG03 and EG04 .","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"dispute-escalation","heading":"Dispute escalation amounts","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"In this integration, WETH is the escalation variable. Before escalationHalt , OpenOracle requires min(⌊oldAmount1 × multiplier / 100⌋, escalationHalt) . With the configured multiplier of 115 , integer flooring leaves token1 amounts from one through six attoETH unchanged; seven attoETH is the first amount that grows. At or above the halt, each replacement instead requires oldAmount1 + 1 , adding one attoETH of WETH.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"parameters","heading":"OpenOracle parameters used","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and a censorship stress model.","text":"A source constant is fixed in the Solidity source. Deployment configuration is the shared configuration that the deployment passes to the coordinator factory, named by its constant in shared/statoblast/ts/initialReport/oracleInitialReport.ts ; a deployed coordinator exposes each configured value through a public getter. The line under each value says where it is set. Parameter Value and source Use in Statoblast token1Address / token2Address WETH / REP The factory's WETH and the pool universe's REP token Defines the REP/ETH price pair used for security-pool solvency checks. minimumToken1ReportAttoEth() See Report sizing and correction incentives and the estimator . Derived per request Minimum initial WETH position for a coordinator report. OpenOracle initial currentAmount1 The greater of the minimum and the sponsor's requestedInitialAttoWeth Derived per request Initial WETH position; the sponsor may fund more than the minimum. escalationHalt See Report sizing and correction incentives . Derived per request Ends multiplicative escalation; see Dispute escalation amounts . settlerReward The committed bountyAttoEth ; at least the request cost Derived per request Paid to the account that settles the report. Sizing inputs: gasUnitsForOneDispute , openOracleSecurityMultiplierBps , targetPriceErrorForDispute , protocolFee , feePercentage , escalationHaltMultiplierBps , initialReportPriorityFeeAttoEthPerGas , OPEN_INTEREST_DIVIDER See Formula inputs . Size the report and the escalation halt. The two fees also set OpenOracle's dispute fee accounting. getRequestPriceCostAttoEth() 101 attoETH Minimum public settlement bounty; sponsors pay their own gas. callbackGasLimit 4,000,000 gas for feed acceptance and funding recovery. Gas forwarded to the settlement callback. Settlement also needs enough surrounding gas to satisfy OpenOracle's callback headroom check. maxSettlementBaseFeeMultiplierBps 30,000 bps ( 3x ) Deployment configuration ORACLE_MAX_SETTLEMENT_BASE_FEE_MULTIPLIER_BPS Scales the request-block base fee plus configured priority fee into the settlement base-fee cap . settlementTime 480 seconds Deployment configuration ORACLE_SETTLEMENT_TIME Report settlement delay on the timestamp clock. disputeDelay 0 Deployment configuration ORACLE_DISPUTE_DELAY Allows disputes immediately after each report and strictly before its settlement deadline. At the deadline, settlement is valid and a dispute is too late. multiplier 115 ( 1.15x ) Deployment configuration ORACLE_MULTIPLIER Growth of the WETH amount per dispute before the halt; see Dispute escalation amounts . protocolFeeRecipient 0x000000000000000000000000000000000000dEaD Deployment configuration ORACLE_PROTOCOL_FEE_RECIPIENT Receives OpenOracle protocol fees for coordinator-created reports. timeType / trackDisputes true / true Deployment configuration; the constructor rejects any other value Timestamps let the coordinator measure freshness from settlement eligibility; dispute history lets settlement validate the final report's recorded base fee. callbackContract The candidate's SharedPoolReportEscrow . Set when the report is created Callbacks reconcile candidate funding and submit the settled price to the feed. Capital operations execute separately. PRICE_VALID_FOR_SECONDS / SEPOLIA_PRICE_VALID_FOR_SECONDS See Token pair and cached price . Source constant Keeper slack added to settlementTime by SharedPoolPriceFeed.priceValidityWindowSeconds() .","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Zoltar","question","registry","screenshots"],"path":"tutorials/zoltar-first-question.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create a yes-or-no question in the Zoltar registry and find it again.","text":"Tutorial You will create a yes-or-no question in Zoltar's global registry and then find it again in the question browser. Every market and every fork starts from a question like this one.","title":"Your first Zoltar question","topic":"Zoltar","weight":1},{"fragment":"before-you-start","heading":"Before you start","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/zoltar-first-question.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create a yes-or-no question in the Zoltar registry and find it again.","text":"Zoltar is the base layer: it records questions and keeps the REP ledger of each universe . Zoltar and branching universes explains the model. Run bun run app:serve:zoltar and open http://localhost:4153/?simulate=1&genesis=yes&simScenario=deployed . That browser-local simulator has the contracts deployed and no questions yet; the screenshots below come from it. It never reflects live chain state.","title":"Your first Zoltar question","topic":"Zoltar","weight":0},{"fragment":"create","heading":"Write the question","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/zoltar-first-question.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create a yes-or-no question in the Zoltar registry and find it again.","text":"Select Create question and keep the Binary question type. Enter a Title , for example Will it rain in Lisbon on 1 June 2027? In Description , name the source and say when the answer is Invalid , for example Resolves Yes if IPMA reports measurable rain at its Lisbon station on 1 June 2027 (UTC). Invalid if IPMA publishes no reading. Enter an End time after the event. Leave Start time blank to start immediately. The question form. The draft preview shows exactly what will be recorded on chain. A question cannot be edited after it is created, so check the preview before you submit.","title":"Your first Zoltar question","topic":"Zoltar","weight":0},{"fragment":"submit","heading":"Create it","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/zoltar-first-question.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create a yes-or-no question in the Zoltar registry and find it again.","text":"Select Create question below the preview and confirm the transaction. The confirmation card shows the Question ID , the end time, and the Outcomes Yes, No, and Invalid. The new question. The question ID identifies the question in every universe.","title":"Your first Zoltar question","topic":"Zoltar","weight":0},{"fragment":"find","heading":"Find it in the registry","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/zoltar-first-question.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create a yes-or-no question in the Zoltar registry and find it again.","text":"Select Browse questions . Your question appears automatically under Downloaded (1) . The registry. Questions are global: any universe can use this one, and anyone can find it here. That is a complete question. To put collateral behind a question, follow Your first shared security portfolio ; each simulator keeps its own chain. Use for fork instead starts a universe fork once the question has ended, which Zoltar and branching universes explains.","title":"Your first Zoltar question","topic":"Zoltar","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Statoblast","portfolio","REP","complete sets","screenshots"],"path":"tutorials/statoblast-first-pool.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Add REP to a simulated portfolio and purchase complete sets for one question.","text":"Tutorial Use the browser simulator to add 1 REP to a shared portfolio, then purchase complete sets for one of its questions. A complete set contains one share of every outcome. You will see your principal rise from 5,000 to 5,001 REP and receive shares in your simulated wallet.","title":"Back a shared portfolio and mint shares","topic":"Statoblast","weight":1},{"fragment":"before-you-start","heading":"Open the prepared portfolio","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/statoblast-first-pool.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Add REP to a simulated portfolio and purchase complete sets for one question.","text":"This exercise uses fake assets in a browser-local chain. From a prepared checkout, run bun run app:serve:statoblast and open http://localhost:12347/#/portfolios?simulate=1&genesis=yes&simScenario=portfolio . No wallet extension or live funds are needed. Wait for Price current in the Shared backing section. The fixture supplies two registered questions, 5,000 REP of your principal, a 10 ETH commitment and a 120% saved health target. The portfolio shares that commitment across its questions; each question has separate outcome shares. See How Statoblast works for the economic model. Your starting account. The fixture has already authorized backing.","title":"Back a shared portfolio and mint shares","topic":"Statoblast","weight":0},{"fragment":"deposit","heading":"Add one REP","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/statoblast-first-pool.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Add REP to a simulated portfolio and purchase complete sets for one question.","text":"In Deposit REP , enter 1 in REP amount . If an approval is requested, approve the displayed amount and confirm its review. Select Deposit REP , review the transaction, and confirm it. Wait for Transaction confirmed . Under Your capital , check that Your total principal is now 5,001 REP. Your commitment remains 10 ETH: depositing does not automatically increase it.","title":"Back a shared portfolio and mint shares","topic":"Statoblast","weight":0},{"fragment":"mint","heading":"Purchase complete sets","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/statoblast-first-pool.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Add REP to a simulated portfolio and purchase complete sets for one question.","text":"In Mint portfolio complete sets , choose a title under Question . Read the question details and verify its full ID. Use Find by question ID to enter an exact identifier instead. Enter 0.01 for Collateral ETH , 0.1 for Maximum purchase fee ETH , and 0 for Minimum complete sets . The zero minimum is suitable for this controlled simulation; a live purchase should set a minimum from its quote. Check Purchase fee ETH . Select Mint portfolio complete sets and confirm the review. Unused fee budget returns to your wallet. Wait for Transaction confirmed . Open the selected question through its link and inspect your outcome balances in the Shares view. Each outcome has the same newly minted quantity. Purchase controls. The fee budget is separate from collateral. You have added capital and bought question shares through the same portfolio. Reloading a new simulation resets its local chain; these balances are not live assets. To understand fees or exiting capital, continue with Fees and the capital guardrails .","title":"Back a shared portfolio and mint shares","topic":"Statoblast","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","liquidity","initialize","pair","screenshots"],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-market.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create and seed the trading pool for a SecurityPool in the UI.","text":"Tutorial You will open trading for a SecurityPool by creating its trading pool and seeding it at a 70% conditional Yes price (the Yes price if the market does not resolve Invalid) with 0.01 ETH. You finish with LP tokens (the trading pool's liquidity-provider tokens) and separately held Invalid shares in your wallet, and the market listed as open.","title":"Your first Trading market","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"before-you-start","heading":"Before you start","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-market.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create and seed the trading pool for a SecurityPool in the UI.","text":"Trading is an exchange for Statoblast outcome shares: a trading pool swaps the Yes and No shares of exactly one SecurityPool, and a SecurityPool without one has no market yet. In the UI, creating the trading pool is called creating a market . To practise without a wallet, run bun run app:serve:trading and open http://localhost:4163/?simulate=1&genesis=yes&simScenario=deployed#/create-market . That simulator scenario has one SecurityPool, at 0x94C1e87e42d24a96174d434eF46b33152eeCE38D , and no trading pool; the screenshots below come from it. For a live network, follow Configure the Trading UI , and if the UI opens the deployment screen, complete Deploy the Trading contracts first. Fund the wallet with the deposit plus gas.","title":"Your first Trading market","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"find-pool","heading":"Open a SecurityPool without a market","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-market.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create and seed the trading pool for a SecurityPool in the UI.","text":"Select the Create tab and paste the SecurityPool address into Search security pools . In the simulator, use the address above; on a live network, copy it from Statoblast. Select Open security pool . The form opens with the badge MARKET NOT CREATED and Create market selected, and the SecurityPool is saved as a favorite. The next section continues from this form. To check the SecurityPool first, select Create again: the list shows your favorite SecurityPools in the selected universe that have no trading market yet, and Create market returns you to the form. Pools without a market. Create market reopens the form; Details opens the details page. The details page tells you whether the SecurityPool can take a market. An enabled Create market button means it can, and selecting it returns you to the form. When it cannot, the button is disabled and names the reason, such as Question ended. This market can no longer be created. Minting capacity shows the collateral already minted against the SecurityPool's ceiling (used / ceiling); your deposit must fit in the difference. An eligible SecurityPool. The SecurityPool is operational, its universe has not forked, and it has capacity for new collateral.","title":"Your first Trading market","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"initialize","heading":"Create and seed the trading pool","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-market.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create and seed the trading pool for a SecurityPool in the UI.","text":"Enter 0.01 in Amount and 70 in Yes price if not Invalid . Read the estimate: You provide 0.01 ETH, You receive ≈ LP tokens, about 0.0089 Invalid after the minting fee, and the Yes shares the trading pool did not need. Select Create market and add liquidity and confirm the one transaction that creates and seeds the trading pool. It reverts if it would mint fewer LP tokens than the estimate allows after your slippage tolerance, which you set under Settings . Seeding at a 70% conditional Yes price. The LP tokens represent only the Yes and No left in the trading pool; the Invalid and spare Yes return to your wallet.","title":"Your first Trading market","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"check","heading":"Check the result","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-market.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create and seed the trading pool for a SecurityPool in the UI.","text":"Open the Markets tab. The market is listed with the badge TRADING OPEN and a conditional price of 70% Yes. Then open Portfolio : the market shows your LP tokens, your Invalid, and the spare Yes as separate holdings. Portfolio after seeding. The new LP tokens and spare outcomes appear alongside the simulation wallet’s existing complete set. Fees reduce the amount minted from the 0.01 ETH deposit. At a 70% conditional Yes price, the trading pool holds roughly 30 Yes for every 70 No. The No reserve is the larger one because in a constant-product market the opposite reserve sets the price; Conditional pricing explains why. Next: make your first trade against the trading pool you just created.","title":"Your first Trading market","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","buy","sell","enterPosition","slippage"],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-trade.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Buy Yes shares in the UI and sell part of them back for ETH.","text":"Tutorial In this tutorial you buy Yes shares in an open market and then sell part of them back for ETH. You will see one ETH purchase become a complete set plus a swap, leaving you Yes shares and Invalid shares, and you will see the sale spend some of that Invalid to pay out ETH.","title":"Your first trade","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"before-you-start","heading":"Before you start","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-trade.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Buy Yes shares in the UI and sell part of them back for ETH.","text":"Trading is an exchange for Statoblast outcome shares: each market trades the Yes and No shares of one SecurityPool in a constant-product trading pool . To practise without a wallet, run bun run app:serve:trading and open http://localhost:4163/?simulate=1&genesis=yes#/market/0x94C1e87e42d24a96174d434eF46b33152eeCE38D . The browser-local simulator is labeled at the top of the page, starts with one funded market, and never reflects live chain state; the screenshots below come from it. To trade for real, open the UI on a supported network as described in Configure the Trading UI and connect a wallet.","title":"Your first trade","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"choose","heading":"Choose a market","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-trade.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Buy Yes shares in the UI and sell part of them back for ETH.","text":"The simulator link above opens the market directly. On a live network, paste a SecurityPool address into Search markets and select Open security pool , or follow a shared market link. Opening a market saves it as a favorite. The Markets tab lists registered markets with trading pools in the selected universe with prices if not Invalid , liquidity, and closing time; select a title to open it. Favorites appear first, and the star removes a market from favorites. Markets. Only markets whose badge reads TRADING OPEN accept new trades.","title":"Your first trade","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"protect","heading":"Set your protection","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-trade.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Buy Yes shares in the UI and sell part of them back for ETH.","text":"Open Settings in the header. Under Trade settings , choose a Slippage tolerance (0.01% to 5%) and how long a transaction stays valid ( Transaction valid for , 1 to 1440 minutes). A transaction reverts if the price moves further than the tolerance or it is included after its deadline, so tighter values mean fewer surprises and more reverts. The defaults, 0.5% and 20 minutes, are fine for this tutorial. Trade settings. Every quote repeats the current values, for example Slippage 0.5% · valid up to 20 min .","title":"Your first trade","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"buy","heading":"Buy Yes","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-trade.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Buy Yes shares in the UI and sell part of them back for ETH.","text":"In the market's Trade tab, select Buy and Yes . Enter 0.002 in You pay . This small simulated trade includes the pool’s minimum minting fee; review its negative profit estimate before continuing. The estimate appears below the field: You receive ≈ shows the Yes and its payout if the question resolves Yes (0 ETH if it resolves No), with your Invalid insurance beside it. Below them are Average price , Profit if the question resolves Yes , and Minimum received , and Trade details lists the Trading fee . Check the Price impact : it reads 4.93%, with a note that smaller trades get a better average price. Larger impacts need a confirmation or are refused; the Trading configuration lists the thresholds. Select Buy Yes and confirm the single enterPosition transaction in your wallet. The client checks the price again before the wallet opens. A Yes purchase. The bar at the top of the estimate shows how far the purchase moves the conditional price. When the transaction confirms, the Yes and Invalid balances under Your position on the market page go up. The ETH became complete sets; the router sold their No for more Yes and sent you all the Yes plus the Invalid. Your Invalid insurance equals the number of complete sets, not the larger Yes total, and you hold no new No.","title":"Your first trade","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"sell","heading":"Sell Yes","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-trade.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Buy Yes shares in the UI and sell part of them back for ETH.","text":"Select Sell and Yes , then enter 0.001 in Shares to sell . The 25% , 50% , and Max buttons fill in larger amounts. Read the estimate: You sell , You receive ≈ in ETH, Average price , Minimum received , and Invalid used . Select Sell Yes and confirm. No approval is needed: the sale is one ShareToken transfer of your Yes and Invalid to the router . A Yes sale. Invalid used is the insurance the sale spends: it is burned with matching Yes and No as complete sets, which pay out the ETH. When it confirms, the ETH is in your wallet, your Invalid has dropped by the amount used, and any Yes the router did not need has come back. If you ask to sell more than your Invalid can insure, the ticket offers the largest amount you can sell now; Exit an insured position covers that case and Why early ETH exits are limited explains the bound.","title":"Your first trade","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","development","commands","Docker"],"path":"how-to/trading-set-up-development.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Compile, test, build, and serve the Trading subsystem locally.","text":"How-to guide Use this guide to get the Trading contracts, SDK, and UI compiled and running on your machine.","title":"Set up Trading development","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"build-and-run","heading":"Build and run","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-set-up-development.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Compile, test, build, and serve the Trading subsystem locally.","text":"From the repository root, confirm dependencies with test -d node_modules . If they are missing, run bun install --frozen-lockfile && bun run setup ; setup installs every project from its lockfile, compiles the contracts, and builds the UI apps and UI tests. Run bun run trading:compile . It compiles the contracts, builds the shared packages, and runs the Trading vendor build, which generates ui/trading/ts/generated/contractArtifact.ts . Never edit generated files. Run bun run trading:test , which runs the SDK, contract-facing, and UI Trading tests, and bun run trading:ui:build . Start bun run app:serve:trading , then open http://localhost:4163/?simulate=1&genesis=yes for the browser-local simulator. Configure the Trading UI lists the Trading scenarios. The setup works when the tests pass and the simulator page loads. To run the standalone UI in Docker instead, complete the repository-root setup first, then from ui/trading : docker network inspect zoltar >/dev/null 2>&1 \\ || docker network create zoltar docker compose up --build --force-recreate Then open http://localhost:4163/#/market . On Windows, ui/trading/start.bat runs the same Compose command. The final image runs as an unprivileged user and exposes a health check at / . The Docker image copies the core deployment addresses from docs/mainnet-deployment-addresses.json and docs/sepolia-deployment-addresses.json .","title":"Set up Trading development","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"external-node","heading":"Test transactions against an external node","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-set-up-development.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Compile, test, build, and serve the Trading subsystem locally.","text":"To exercise the contracts on a local Anvil node, follow Use local Anvil ; for script and integration tests, Deploy for script and integration tests covers bun run trading:deploy:local .","title":"Set up Trading development","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"commands","heading":"Other commands","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-set-up-development.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Compile, test, build, and serve the Trading subsystem locally.","text":"bun run trading:check runs the tests plus the bigint-number-cast guard. bun run trading:coverage:contracts traces Solidity execution and requires at least 99% production-contract line coverage. bun run trading:gas-costs reports bytecode sizes and funded-fixture operation gas. cd ui/trading && bun run docker:build and docker:run build and run the standalone UI container without Compose. After a change that alters what the UI shows, run bun run docs:screenshots -- --app trading to recapture the documentation screenshots.","title":"Set up Trading development","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","UI","simulate","RPC","settings"],"path":"how-to/trading-configure-ui.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Live and simulation modes; network and RPC selection.","text":"How-to guide Use this guide to choose between live and simulated mode and to point the Trading UI at the network and RPC you need.","title":"Configure the Trading UI","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"modes","heading":"Choose a mode","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-configure-ui.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Live and simulation modes; network and RPC selection.","text":"With no mode parameter, the UI reads live RPC and wallet state. ?simulate=1 boots a browser-local simulator with seeded contracts and balances. Add &simScenario=deployed for a SecurityPool with no trading pool , &simScenario=baseline for a chain without Statoblast or Trading contracts, &simScenario=trading-forked for the funded market after its universe forks, or leave it out for the default trading-funded scenario with one funded market. A banner labels the page as a simulation. Set up Trading development lists the commands that serve the UI.","title":"Configure the Trading UI","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"genesis","heading":"Choose the truthful universe","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-configure-ui.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Live and simulation modes; network and RPC selection.","text":"Choose Yes or No for the displayed Augur v2 fork question. To open that choice directly, add genesis=yes or genesis=no to the URL query, for example ?simulate=1&genesis=yes . The selected universe uses its own REP token and protocol deployment. To change the choice, open Universe under More , follow the selected root's Parent universe link to Augur v2 universe , and open the other child. Returning to the Augur v2 universe clears the previous deployment's market and fork selection.","title":"Configure the Trading UI","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"network","heading":"Select a network and RPC","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-configure-ui.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Live and simulation modes; network and RPC selection.","text":"Open Settings in the header. Choose the RPC network , and optionally enter a Fallback RPC URL and select Save RPC . A custom RPC must use HTTPS, or HTTP on a loopback address, and must serve the selected chain. It is used only until a wallet is connected on the selected chain; after that, the wallet's own RPC serves reads and transactions. The UI then checks the RPC chain and the core contracts, and derives the Trading factory and router addresses for that network. If either is not deployed, the deployment screen opens; Deploy the Trading contracts covers it. The configuration works when the Markets tab opens. The configuration reference describes what the client derives and checks.","title":"Configure the Trading UI","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","deploy","factory","router","Anvil"],"path":"how-to/trading-deploy-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Deploy the Trading factory and router from the browser, with the testnet deployer on local Anvil, or as script-only copies for tests.","text":"How-to guide Trading needs one factory and one router at addresses derived from the Statoblast deployment. Use this guide to deploy them from the browser on a network where Statoblast is installed, or with the testnet deployer on a local Anvil node.","title":"Deploy the Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"before-you-start","heading":"Before you start","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-deploy-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Deploy the Trading factory and router from the browser, with the testnet deployer on local Anvil, or as script-only copies for tests.","text":"Confirm reviewed Statoblast core code, including its SecurityPoolFactory , for your selected network and Yes or No universe. Use the matching manifest listed in the deployment status reference ; planned addresses do not prove the contracts are live. To install the core on a test network first, follow Deploy testnet contracts .","title":"Deploy the Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"deploy","heading":"Deploy from the browser","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-deploy-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Deploy the Trading factory and router from the browser, with the testnet deployer on local Anvil, or as script-only copies for tests.","text":"Serve the Trading UI as Set up Trading development describes, from source or in Docker. Open http://localhost:4163/#/deploy and choose the truthful universe if prompted. To use another network or RPC URL, set it under Settings before connecting. Select Connect wallet . The Trading contracts panel shows the SecurityPoolFactory address and the status of the Trading factory ( TwoWayConstantProductFactory ) and Trading router ( TwoWayConstantProductRouter ). Select Deploy trading factory and confirm, then select Deploy trading router and confirm. Deployment progress reaches 2 / 2 once both contracts and their links are verified. If you leave and return with the same settings, the flow resumes at the first missing contract. A missing prerequisite. The SECURITY POOL FACTORY IS NOT DEPLOYED badge and the disabled deploy buttons mean the selected Statoblast SecurityPoolFactory is not deployed. The factory's trading fee is fixed at 0.30% and cannot be changed after deployment. Creating a trading pool for a market is a separate step; see Your first Trading market .","title":"Deploy the Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"use-local-anvil","heading":"Use local Anvil","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-deploy-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Deploy the Trading factory and router from the browser, with the testnet deployer on local Anvil, or as script-only copies for tests.","text":"The UIs do not offer Ethereum mainnet, so start Anvil with Sepolia's chain ID, 11155111 , and use the Sepolia address profile: bun run anvil -- --chain-id 11155111 --hardfork osaka Deploy to the node with the testnet deployer, following Deploy locally with --rpc-url=http://127.0.0.1:8545 and an Anvil account's key. It installs the core and the Trading factory and router. Its final explorer-verification step targets public Sepolia, so a failure there does not mean the local deployment failed. In your wallet, point the chain 11155111 network at http://127.0.0.1:8545 and import an Anvil account. Once a wallet is connected, the UI reads and sends through the wallet's RPC, so a wallet still on public Sepolia would use the real network. Start the Trading UI, connect the wallet, and open #/deploy . Deployment progress shows 2 / 2 .","title":"Deploy the Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"deploy-script","heading":"Deploy for script and integration tests","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-deploy-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Deploy the Trading factory and router from the browser, with the testnet deployer on local Anvil, or as script-only copies for tests.","text":"The local deployment script deploys independent copies at ordinary CREATE addresses. The UI never finds them, because it looks only at the derived addresses; use them from scripts and integration tests only. The script needs a node whose chain ID equals the core manifest's, code at that manifest's SecurityPoolFactory , and an unlocked account on the node. For the Sepolia manifest below, complete step 1 of Use local Anvil first. ZOLTAR_DEPLOYMENT_MANIFEST=docs/sepolia-deployment-addresses.json \\ TRADING_RPC_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8545 \\ bun run trading:deploy:local The script deploys TwoWayConstantProductFactory(coreFactory, feeBps) and TwoWayConstantProductRouter(factory) , writes a manifest of its inputs, addresses, transaction hashes, and bytecode hashes to solidity/deployments/trading/local.json , and prints it. The configuration reference lists its environment variables and output.","title":"Deploy the Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","odds","initialize","basis points"],"path":"how-to/trading-seed-alternative-odds.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Seed a new trading pool at a chosen Conditional Yes price by calling its contract directly.","text":"How-to guide Use this guide when you call the trading pool contract (the pair) yourself and want a new trading pool to open at a specific Conditional Yes price rather than 50%.","title":"Seed alternative conditional odds","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"prerequisites","heading":"Before you call initialize","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-seed-alternative-odds.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Seed a new trading pool at a chosen Conditional Yes price by calling its contract directly.","text":"Creating the trading pool and initializing it directly are separate transactions, so anyone can initialize it first at another price. Prefer the router's createPairAndInitializeWithEth , which creates and seeds in one transaction and takes the price in basis points; the UI's Initialize form uses it, as Your first Trading market shows. Continue only if you must call initialize yourself. The question has not ended, its universe has not forked, and the SecurityPool is Operational and unresolved. The smaller deposit must exceed MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY (1,000 share base units). The trading pool mints that many LP base units to a lock address that no key controls, so you receive the smaller deposit minus 1,000. The pair reference lists the remaining preconditions of initialize .","title":"Seed alternative conditional odds","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"steps","heading":"Seed the trading pool","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-seed-alternative-odds.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Seed a new trading pool at a chosen Conditional Yes price by calling its contract directly.","text":"Create complete sets by sending ETH to the SecurityPool's createCompleteSet() . Let q be the Yes balance it minted to you, in share base units; the ETH-to-share rate is not fixed, so read the balance. Create the trading pool with the factory's createPair(securityPool) , unless it already exists. Choose the Conditional Yes price p in basis points with 0 < p < 10,000 and compute the deposits: If p ≥ 5,000 , deposit all q No and ⌊q × (10,000 − p) / p⌋ Yes. If p < 5,000 , deposit all q Yes and ⌊q × p / (10,000 − p)⌋ No. Approve the trading pool as an operator on the canonical ShareToken with setApprovalForAll ; initialize pulls both deposits from the caller. Call initialize(yesAmount, noAmount, minLiquidity, recipient) on the trading pool with those deposits. Set minLiquidity to the smaller deposit minus 1,000. Check the result: getReserves() returns your two deposits and tradingStatus() returns Open . If initialize reverted with Already initialized , someone else seeded the trading pool first. Conditional pricing works through the 70% example and explains why the larger reserve is the side you are betting against.","title":"Seed alternative conditional odds","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","exit","sell","maximumInsuredExit","Invalid"],"path":"how-to/trading-exit-a-position.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Sell shares for ETH in the UI or find the largest insured exit with the SDK.","text":"How-to guide Use this guide to turn Yes or No shares back into ETH while the market's trading pool is open. The exit spends one Invalid share for each complete set it redeems, so your Invalid balance limits it. Connect your wallet and open Portfolio to discover Yes, No, Invalid and LP holdings across registered security pools in the selected universe, including pools never opened in this browser. Discovery covers the configured deployment; switch universes to inspect other branches. A failed balance read remains visible as unavailable rather than being counted as zero.","title":"Exit an insured position","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"in-the-ui","heading":"Exit in the UI","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-exit-a-position.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Sell shares for ETH in the UI or find the largest insured exit with the SDK.","text":"Open the market, select Sell and the side you hold. Select Max to fill Shares to sell with the largest amount you can sell now, or enter a smaller amount. The hint under the field shows your holding and that limit. Check You receive ≈ , Minimum received , and Invalid used , then select Sell Yes (or Sell No ) and confirm. An insured Yes exit. The hint under the amount is the limit set by your Invalid, your Yes, and the trading pool's liquidity. If Invalid is the binding limit and you enter more than it covers, the ticket shows Not enough Invalid shares to insure this sale. and offers Sell … instead with the largest amount. The shares you do not sell stay in your wallet. Acquiring more Invalid helps only when Invalid is the binding limit; Why early ETH exits are limited works through the bounds. When the sale confirms, the Invalid balance under Your position has dropped by Invalid used and the ETH is in your wallet.","title":"Exit an insured position","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"through-the-sdk","heading":"Exit through the SDK","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-exit-a-position.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Sell shares for ETH in the UI or find the largest insured exit with the SDK.","text":"Call maximumInsuredExit from the SDK , a private workspace package in this repository, with your wallet balances and the trading pool's reserves and fee. It returns the largest number of complete sets q that your Invalid, your long shares , and the opposite reserve all allow. For a Yes position, q complete sets need q Invalid, q Yes to redeem, and enough extra Yes to buy exactly q No. Choose maxLongSharesIn , minEthOut , payoutRecipient , refundRecipient , and deadline explicitly, and encode the request with encodeReceiveRequest . Send a ShareToken safeBatchTransferFrom to the router carrying q Invalid and maxLongSharesIn long shares with the encoded request. Simulate it, simulate again immediately before submission, and submit only if the result still satisfies maxLongSharesIn and minEthOut . The router reference lists the request fields, their constraints, and what the router delivers. A successful exit emits PositionExitedByTransfer with the long shares used and refunded.","title":"Exit an insured position","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","liquidity","removeLiquidity","permit"],"path":"how-to/trading-remove-liquidity.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Withdraw an LP position as raw Yes and No in any lifecycle phase.","text":"How-to guide Use this guide to withdraw an LP position from a trading pool .","title":"Remove liquidity","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"in-the-ui","heading":"Remove in the UI","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-remove-liquidity.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Withdraw an LP position as raw Yes and No in any lifecycle phase.","text":"Open the market and select the Liquidity tab, then Remove . Enter the LP tokens to remove in Amount . You receive ≈ shows the Yes and No you get back. Select Remove liquidity and confirm. The transaction reverts if it would return less than the estimate allows after your slippage tolerance. Removing liquidity. The UI calls the trading pool directly, so it never asks for a router allowance.","title":"Remove liquidity","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"through-the-pair","heading":"Remove through the trading pool contract","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-remove-liquidity.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Withdraw an LP position as raw Yes and No in any lifecycle phase.","text":"Compute the outputs from getEffectiveReserves() : each reserve × liquidity ÷ LP total supply, rounded down. Removal first synchronizes the reserves, so donated shares count. Call the trading pool's removeLiquidity(liquidity, minYes, minNo, recipient, deadline) with minYes and minNo set from those outputs and your tolerance. The deadline is enforced on chain. After either path, your LP balance has fallen by liquidity , your Yes and No balances have risen by the outputs, and the trading pool has emitted LiquidityRemoved . Integrations that need router-based removal call the router's removeLiquidityWithPermit , whose bounds are named minYesOut and minNoOut . The router reference gives the values the permit must sign and the allowance fallback. Never grant a permanent or infinite allowance.","title":"Remove liquidity","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"what-removal-does-not-do","heading":"What removal does not do","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-remove-liquidity.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Withdraw an LP position as raw Yes and No in any lifecycle phase.","text":"Removal always returns raw Yes and No, before and after question end, resolution, and forks. It does not use your wallet Invalid, redeem complete sets, settle winning shares, choose a child branch, or migrate liquidity. For those, see Handle resolution and Handle a universe fork .","title":"Remove liquidity","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","resolution","settlement","redeemShares","Invalid"],"path":"how-to/trading-handle-resolution.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Unwind LP and redeem complete sets after question end, then claim winning shares once the market finalizes.","text":"How-to guide A trading pool closes to swaps and added liquidity when its question ends; the market finalizes later, when its outcome is final. Use this guide from question end onward to turn LP tokens, complete sets, and winning shares into their redeemable assets.","title":"Handle resolution","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"steps","heading":"Unwind after question end","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-handle-resolution.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Unwind LP and redeem complete sets after question end, then claim winning shares once the market finalizes.","text":"Remove your LP tokens from the trading pool into Yes and No shares ( Remove liquidity ). Redeem matching Invalid, Yes, and No as complete sets while the SecurityPool is Operational and its universe has not forked. If the universe forks while the question is unresolved, follow Handle a universe fork . After the market finalizes, redeem the winning share through SecurityPool.redeemShares , which requires the SecurityPool to be Operational . Keep redeeming through this pool if its universe forks later; resolved shares cannot migrate. On an Invalid result, Invalid is the only winning share. Yes and No balances, including trading fees earned as LP, are worth nothing once they lose. Each redemption burns the redeemed shares and pays ETH at the SecurityPool's current collateral rate. A shared portfolio child can also pay recovery REP from its fork claim basket . The receipt can contain REP even when its ETH payout is zero; a new price report or an auction buyer is not required for redemption.","title":"Handle resolution","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"in-the-ui","heading":"In the UI","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-handle-resolution.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Unwind LP and redeem complete sets after question end, then claim winning shares once the market finalizes.","text":"Open the market. A closed market opens on its Settlement tab. To redeem complete sets, select Redeem sets , enter an amount in ETH to redeem (or Complete sets to redeem when the claim includes REP), check the ETH estimate and any Recovery REP , and select Redeem complete sets . Once the market finalizes, a Redeem Yes , Redeem No , or Redeem Invalid option appears for the winning outcome. It redeems your entire balance of that share through this exact SecurityPool. Before finalization the option is absent. Settlement after the question ends. The market has not finalized yet, so only complete-set redemption and fork migration are offered. Check both asset balances in the selected universe after confirmation. REP recovery is shown separately from the portfolio’s ETH total. Every settlement transaction is simulated again at the latest block before your wallet opens, and stops if it would now fail.","title":"Handle resolution","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","fork","migrate","ShareToken"],"path":"how-to/trading-handle-a-fork.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Migrate parent shares into chosen child branches and open a child trading pool.","text":"How-to guide A fork closes the parent trading pool for swaps and additions and leaves every migration decision to you. This guide walks through moving a position into the child universe or universes you choose.","title":"Handle a universe fork","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"steps","heading":"Migrate a position","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-handle-a-fork.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Migrate parent shares into chosen child branches and open a child trading pool.","text":"Check the market's outcome. If it has already finalized, follow Handle resolution to redeem winning shares through its SecurityPool. Continue below only while the question is unresolved. Remove parent LP into parent Yes and No shares ( Remove liquidity ). Decide the destination child universe or universes for each of your Invalid, Yes, and No balances. Nothing in Trading chooses a child universe or migrates LP for you. For every nonzero parent balance you intend to migrate, submit one ShareToken.migrate(fromId, targetOutcomeIndexes) call with that token ID and the explicitly chosen, strictly increasing target outcome indexes. Each call mints the matching child-universe share balance. The parent balance stays visible, but that source token ID is locked for the wallet and can no longer be transferred. After each call, verify the expected child token ID and balance, then refresh or discover the canonical child SecurityPool. Wait until the child SecurityPool is Operational : shared portfolio branches use their fixed activation deadline and shared Collateral Repair Auction where required. Then create a trading pool only if you intend to trade. A child SecurityPool can take a trading pool only if it is back to Operational before the question's end date. Parent and child token IDs and reserves stay separate. To recover collateral from an active shared portfolio child, use Settlement . Complete-set and winning-share claims can pay ETH and REP together, including REP-only recovery. Redemption does not require a trading pool or an auction bid.","title":"Handle a universe fork","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"choosing-targets","heading":"Choose fork targets","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-handle-a-fork.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Migrate parent shares into chosen child branches and open a child trading pool.","text":"Source and destination are independent: a parent Invalid, Yes, or No share can migrate into any valid child universe of the fork question, and the child receives the same share outcome as the source. Migrating parent Yes into three scalar child universes, for example, produces child Yes shares in all three children. For a categorical fork, select Invalid or one or more labeled answers. For a scalar fork, select Invalid or add one or more exact scalar ticks. The UI displays the human value and derives the packed onchain outcome index. A single-target call may create a missing child SecurityPool. A multi-target call must reference children whose canonical pools already exist. To migrate one source share to several new scalar outcomes, submit each missing outcome by itself; locking prevents transfers but not later migration of the same source into a new child. Once those children exist, another source share can target all of them in one call. Every call must add at least one source-and-child entitlement the wallet has not migrated before.","title":"Handle a universe fork","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"in-the-ui","heading":"In the UI","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-handle-a-fork.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Migrate parent shares into chosen child branches and open a child trading pool.","text":"Open the market's Settlement tab and select Migrate . Choose the Share to migrate (Invalid, Yes, or No). Pick one or more child universes from the fork question's categorical answers or scalar ticks. Tick the acknowledgment that the migration moves your entire balance and permanently locks that share's transfers in the parent universe. Select Migrate to N child universes . The transaction copies and locks your entire balance of the selected source share. Repeat for each source share you want to move. Forks and resolution explains why the trading pool behaves this way.","title":"Handle a universe fork","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","constant product","complete set","Yes","No"],"path":"explanation/trading/two-way-market.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"One ETH entry as a complete set, a swap, and Invalid insurance.","text":"Explanation Trading is the two-reserve constant-product design from How To Build: Augur Constant Product with Invalid Insurance , adapted to Statoblast. This page explains what happens inside one entry.","title":"The two-way market","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"mental-model","heading":"Mental model","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/two-way-market.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"One ETH entry as a complete set, a swap, and Invalid insurance.","text":"A SecurityPool is the Statoblast contract that holds ETH collateral for one question , universe , and pool configuration. Its canonical ShareToken is ERC-1155: creating one complete set mints equal Invalid, Yes, and No shares for that pool's universe-specific token IDs; redeeming a complete set burns those three shares and returns collateral at the pool's current dynamic rate. The Trading factory maps the exact SecurityPool address to one immutable-fee pair . The pair is both the Yes/No constant-product reserve and the transferable LP token; it never accepts Invalid shares. The router , which holds no balances or positions between calls, coordinates complete-set creation or redemption, pair swaps, and bounded asset delivery. Users and liquidity providers keep Invalid shares separately in their wallets. The invariant to remember is: shareToken.balanceOf ( pair , invalidTokenId ) = 0 Swaps and added liquidity are open only while the market is live; Forks and resolution lists what closes a pair. Parent and child SecurityPools are distinct markets and therefore use distinct pairs.","title":"The two-way market","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"one-entry","heading":"One entry, step by step","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/two-way-market.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"One ETH entry as a complete set, a swap, and Invalid insurance.","text":"The pair maintains k = Yes reserve × No reserve . Entering Yes looks like this; entering No is the mirror image. User → Router: enterPosition(pair, YES) with ETH Router → SecurityPool: createCompleteSet{value: ETH}() SecurityPool → Router: q Invalid + q Yes + q No Router → Pair: swap q No for Yes Pair → Router: additional Yes Router → User: q Invalid + total Yes Entering a Yes position. The trader ends up holding the Invalid shares from the complete set; the pair never sees them. Suppose the reserves are 1,000 Yes and 1,000 No, the fee is zero, and 100 complete-set shares are created. Contract math uses attoShares, where one displayed share is 10 18 attoShares. Swapping 100 No therefore yields 90.909090909090909090 Yes after flooring to a whole number of attoShares. The wallet receives 190.909090909090909090 Yes and 100 Invalid; the reserves become 909.090909090909090910 Yes and 1,100 No. Flooring the output favors existing liquidity providers. A No purchase simply reverses the reserves. No position record is created anywhere: wallet Yes, No, Invalid, and LP balances are the complete accounting state.","title":"The two-way market","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","Invalid","insurance"],"path":"explanation/trading/invalid-insurance.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why Invalid is held rather than traded and what it pays.","text":"Explanation Invalid shares are held, not traded. This page explains what that buys a trader and what it cannot tell them.","title":"Invalid insurance","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"how-it-works","heading":"How it works","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/invalid-insurance.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why Invalid is held rather than traded and what it pays.","text":"Each ordinary ETH entry returns one Invalid share for each complete set created. If the question resolves Invalid, the holder redeems those shares with the SecurityPool 's redeemShares() at the current collateral value. Keeping Invalid shares out of the pair removes the on-chain three-reserve quadratic and makes an ordinary entry insured against an Invalid result. The cost is that nothing in the AMM can detect a suspicious market from an Invalid price, because no such price exists.","title":"Invalid insurance","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"worked-example","heading":"A worked Invalid result","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/invalid-insurance.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why Invalid is held rather than traded and what it pays.","text":"Alice entered with enough ETH to mint 100 complete-set shares, received 190 Yes after her swap, and kept 100 Invalid. The market resolves Invalid. The pair's Yes and No balances do not become valuable merely because they are nonzero. Alice redeems her 100 winning Invalid shares through the SecurityPool. Because of protocol holding fees , the refund is the current net collateral value, not necessarily her original gross ETH. Liquidity providers hold Invalid shares separately in exactly the same way. Selling or transferring LP tokens never moves that insurance.","title":"Invalid insurance","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","price","conditional","oracle"],"path":"explanation/trading/pricing.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"What Conditional Yes and No mean and why the spot price is not an oracle.","text":"Explanation A two-reserve pair produces exactly two prices, and both are conditional on the market resolving validly.","title":"Conditional pricing","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"formula","heading":"What the prices are","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/pricing.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"What Conditional Yes and No mean and why the spot price is not an oracle.","text":"The constant-product marginal relationship prices one outcome using the opposite reserve: conditionalYes = noReserve yesReserve + noReserve conditionalNo = yesReserve yesReserve + noReserve The two values sum to 100% because they partition the valid outcomes only. That does not imply Invalid has zero probability; there is no unconditional Yes or No probability in this AMM. At 428.571 Yes and 1,000 No, Conditional Yes is about 70% and Conditional No about 30%. The larger No reserve makes Yes the more expensive side, which is why initializing at 70% Conditional Yes deposits more No than Yes.","title":"Conditional pricing","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"not-an-oracle","heading":"Not an oracle","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/pricing.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"What Conditional Yes and No mean and why the spot price is not an oracle.","text":"The displayed before-and-after price is a trade-impact aid, nothing more. Reserve spot values can be moved within a block, donated to, sandwiched, or go stale before inclusion. Protocol logic must never consume this price as an oracle; Trading includes no TWAP.","title":"Conditional pricing","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","LP","liquidity","minimum liquidity"],"path":"explanation/trading/liquidity-provider-position.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How LP supply is scaled and what an LP token claims.","text":"Explanation An LP token is a proportional claim on a pair 's Yes and No reserves and nothing else. This page explains how that claim is minted and what it does not cover.","title":"The liquidity-provider position","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"supply","heading":"How LP supply is scaled","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/liquidity-provider-position.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How LP supply is scaled and what an LP token claims.","text":"Initial LP supply uses the smaller reserve as an accounting scale. The pair permanently mints 1,000 units to MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY_LOCK ( address(1) ), then mints min(Yes, No) − 1,000 to the initializer, so total initial supply is min(Yes, No) . At attoShare scales this avoids an overflowing geometric-mean multiplication. The absolute LP supply is arbitrary; wallet LP / total LP defines ownership. LP → Router: addLiquidityWithEth with ETH Router → SecurityPool: createCompleteSet() SecurityPool → Router: equal Invalid, Yes, No Router → Pair: add largest proportional Yes + No Pair → LP: LP tokens Router → LP: all Invalid + unused Yes/No Adding liquidity with ETH. The LP token covers only the Yes and No shares that entered the pair; the Invalid shares stay with the provider.","title":"The liquidity-provider position","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"what-it-claims","heading":"What the token claims","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/liquidity-provider-position.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How LP supply is scaled and what an LP token claims.","text":"A holder of wallet LP / total LP can withdraw that fraction of both reserves as raw Yes and No shares at any time, including after trading closes. Swap fees are taken from the input and stay in the reserves, and direct Yes or No donations are synchronized into them, so both accrue to existing LPs. The claim is on shares, not ETH. Its mix of Yes and No shifts as trades move the price, and neither share redeems for anything if the question resolves Invalid. The pair cannot hold Invalid shares, so the Invalid minted with a provider's complete sets stays in the provider's wallet, outside the token; Invalid insurance explains what it covers.","title":"The liquidity-provider position","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","exit","bounds","Invalid"],"path":"explanation/trading/early-exit-limit.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"The three bounds on redeeming complete sets from a directional wallet.","text":"Explanation Turning a directional position back into ETH means rebuilding complete sets. Three wallet and pair quantities cap how many you can rebuild.","title":"Why early ETH exits are limited","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"bounds","heading":"The three bounds","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/early-exit-limit.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"The three bounds on redeeming complete sets from a directional wallet.","text":"An insured exit of q complete sets from a wallet holding long Yes shares needs q Invalid shares, q Yes shares for the sets, and extra Yes shares to buy exactly q No shares from the pair : q ≤ wallet Invalid q + required Yes input(q) ≤ wallet Yes q < pair No reserve The SDK binary-searches these monotonic conditions; the chosen candidate is then simulated through the router . All three bounds also presume a live market: the swap reverts once the pair closes, and the SecurityPool redeems complete sets only while it is operational. Forks and resolution covers what closes a pair and how to settle afterwards. User → ShareToken: batch transfer of q Invalid + maximum Yes input to the router, with the encoded request ShareToken → Router: receive callback with the shares and request Router → Pair: exact-output swap for q No Pair → Router: q No Router → SecurityPool: redeemCompleteSet(q) SecurityPool → Router: current ETH value Router → User: measured ETH delta Router → User: unused Yes shares An insured exit. The exit starts with a share transfer to the router , not a router call. Pre-existing ETH in the router is never mixed into the delta the user receives.","title":"Why early ETH exits are limited","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"example","heading":"Example","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/early-exit-limit.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"The three bounds on redeeming complete sets from a directional wallet.","text":"A wallet holds 150 Invalid and 260 Yes shares. If buying 100 No requires 112 Yes, redeeming 100 sets needs 212 Yes and succeeds. Trying 150 might require 181 extra Yes, or 331 in total, and fails on Yes even though Invalid would cover it. If the wallet instead had only 60 Invalid, at most 60 sets are insured. Whatever Yes cannot be insured remains a valid directional asset; it has not disappeared.","title":"Why early ETH exits are limited","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","fork","resolution","lifecycle"],"path":"explanation/trading/forks-and-resolution.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"What closes a pair and what a holder does after a fork or resolution.","text":"Explanation A pair closes when its market can no longer be traded safely, but it never traps a position. This page explains which events close it and what a holder does next.","title":"Forks and resolution in Trading","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"what-closes","heading":"What closes a pair","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/forks-and-resolution.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"What closes a pair and what a holder does after a fork or resolution.","text":"Swaps, initialization, and added liquidity close permanently at question end, when the universe forks, or when the SecurityPoolForker exposes a final outcome . They are also blocked while the SecurityPool is not operational or is awaiting fork continuation . LP removal deliberately has none of those guards. tradingStatus() reports which condition applies.","title":"Forks and resolution in Trading","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"after-a-fork","heading":"After a fork","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/forks-and-resolution.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"What closes a pair and what a holder does after a fork or resolution.","text":"LP → Parent pair: removeLiquidity() Parent pair → LP: parent Yes + No LP → ShareToken: migrate(fromId, chosen outcomes) ShareToken → LP: selected child shares Wait until migration and any Collateral Repair Auction return the child pool to Operational LP → Factory: createPair(exact child pool) Factory → LP: isolated child pair Carrying liquidity across a fork. Every step is the LP's own decision; nothing here happens automatically. A fork does not select a branch or migrate LP. A child pair can be initialized only if the child SecurityPool is back to Operational before the question's end date, which requires a fork caused by another question: pairs refuse initialization and swaps once the question has ended, and a fork caused by disputing this question always comes after its end date. Parent and child pools may share one ShareToken contract, but they have different universe-specific token IDs and different pair addresses. Handle a universe fork gives the step-by-step procedure.","title":"Forks and resolution in Trading","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"after-resolution","heading":"After resolution","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/forks-and-resolution.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"What closes a pair and what a holder does after a fork or resolution.","text":"Pair → Forker: getQuestionOutcome(pool) Forker → Pair: current Yes / No / Invalid outcome Pair → Pair: swaps and additions closed Holder → Pair: removeLiquidity remains available Pair → Holder: raw Yes + No Holder → SecurityPool: redeemShares() for the winning token SecurityPool → Holder: current collateral value Settling after resolution. The pair only closes; value is redeemed at the SecurityPool, never at the pair. If the question resolves Invalid, the Invalid shares held in the wallet are the settlement insurance. Fee growth in the pair's Yes and No reserves creates no redemption value on an Invalid result. Handle resolution gives the procedure.","title":"Forks and resolution in Trading","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","security","permit","reentrancy","reporting"],"path":"reference/trading/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Authorization, custody, assumptions, known risks, and vulnerability reporting.","text":"Reference Trading has not been audited. Tests and internal reviews are not substitutes for an independent security assessment. This page states what the contracts defend against, what they assume, and what they leave to the user.","title":"Trading security model","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"authorization","heading":"Signature and callback authorization","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Authorization, custody, assumptions, known risks, and vulnerability reporting.","text":"LP permits use the standard ERC-2612 nonce, deadline, chain-bound EIP-712 domain, owner, spender, and exact value. The LP ledger is the repository's vendored ERC20.sol , sourced from the MIT-licensed OpenZeppelin Contracts v5.2.0 release . Its local changes pin Solidity 0.8.35, use repository formatting and descriptive revert strings, and substitute Augur unit terminology in comments; its state-transition and allowance algorithms are unchanged. TradingLiquidityToken.sol adapts the MIT-licensed v5.2.0 ERC20Permit by using the repository's shared OpenZeppelin-derived EIP-712/ECDSA primitive, a fixed version 1 , descriptive revert strings, and the stable Zoltar Two-Way LP domain name. It omits Nonces , IERC20Permit , and EIP712 inheritance only because their exact storage and hashing behavior is implemented directly by the local nonce mapping and shared authorization primitive. No signature or curve algorithm is reimplemented in the pair. OpenZeppelin ERC-20 prohibits minting to the zero address. The pair therefore locks MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY at MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY_LOCK , address(1) , which no key controls, instead of treating the zero address as an LP holder. Only pair operations can invoke the inherited internal mint and burn functions. A signature is authorization, not transaction privacy: integrations should use short deadlines and exact amounts. The router reference gives the allowance alternative for signers without ECDSA permits. Receive-based ERC-1155 operations use no off-chain signature. The ShareToken call must be submitted by the owner, while the versioned payload binds all contract identities, operation inputs, recipients, slippage, and deadline. Copying an observed payload cannot redirect assets and cannot separately consume an allowance because the router requires operator == from .","title":"Trading security model","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"custody","heading":"Custody and reentrancy","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Authorization, custody, assumptions, known risks, and vulnerability reporting.","text":"The pair rejects foreign ShareTokens, Invalid shares, and noncanonical universe IDs in both single and batch callbacks. A reentrancy lock covers every reserve-changing operation and every LP mint or burn; LP transfer , approve , and permit change no reserves and are not locked. Recipient callbacks can execute arbitrary code, so final reserves are read from authoritative balances after transfers. Recorded-balance deficits revert; valid Yes and No donations synchronize upward and accrue to current LPs. The router recognizes pairs only through its immutable factory, opens callback state only around one expected pool and share token, and restores its starting share balances. Its ETH receiver opens only around redemption from that pool. This prevents unsolicited callbacks and keeps forced ETH separate from exit proceeds. Factory checks prevent a malicious pool from borrowing another question's ShareToken or universe identity. CREATE2 plus one immutable fee gives one canonical pair per exact pool without privileged replacement. Because an ERC-1155 transfer to an address without code cannot invoke a receiver callback, an attacker can send shares to a predicted CREATE2 address before deployment. The factory's predeployment quarantine removes those canonical balances during pair construction, so initialization starts with zero canonical balances, including zero Invalid. Foreign token contracts cannot be enumerated and may still assign irrelevant balances to the address; the pair never recognizes or moves them.","title":"Trading security model","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumptions","heading":"Assumptions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Authorization, custody, assumptions, known risks, and vulnerability reporting.","text":"The configured Statoblast SecurityPoolFactory , pool, ShareToken , forker , question data, and universe state are canonical and behave as implemented in the matching core release. Users set realistic slippage and deadlines and obtain a fresh simulation in the current block immediately before submission. Wallets and RPC endpoints accurately report account, chain, balances, approvals, and receipts.","title":"Trading security model","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"known-risks","heading":"Known risks","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Authorization, custody, assumptions, known risks, and vulnerability reporting.","text":"Spot prices are not an oracle and expose users to front-running and sandwiches. A lifecycle transition, fork, resolution, reserve donation, retention update, or intervening trade can invalidate a quote; contract guards and slippage bounds make the transaction revert rather than promise execution. Initial liquidity determines the starting conditional price and should be initialized atomically. LP tokens cover only Yes/No reserves; separately held Invalid can be lost, transferred, or insufficient. A valid-resolution LP can suffer directional loss, and Yes/No reserves may have no redemption value after an Invalid result. Remaining risks also include first-price choice, stale simulation, lifecycle races, recipient denial of ETH or ERC-1155 reception, core-contract bugs, and approval misuse. Slippage and deadlines bound execution but do not remove MEV. The immutable factory fee is a deployment choice, not a claim of economic optimality.","title":"Trading security model","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"reporting","heading":"Reporting a vulnerability","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Authorization, custody, assumptions, known risks, and vulnerability reporting.","text":"Report a suspected vulnerability through GitHub private vulnerability reporting . Include the affected commit, contracts and functions, prerequisites, a minimal reproduction, expected impact, and whether funds or deployment keys may be at immediate risk. If private reporting is unavailable, open a public issue asking maintainers to establish a private channel, but do not include vulnerability details. Do not exploit a live deployment or publish details before maintainers have had a reasonable opportunity to respond.","title":"Trading security model","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","design","limitations","non-goals"],"path":"explanation/trading/architecture-decisions.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"The seven design decisions and the features the MVP leaves out.","text":"Explanation Trading is deliberately small. This page records why each decision was made and which features are out of scope, so that future work does not weaken the pair's one hard invariant.","title":"Trading architecture decisions and limitations","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"decisions","heading":"Decisions","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/architecture-decisions.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"The seven design decisions and the features the MVP leaves out.","text":"Two reserves only. A pair holding Yes × No shares avoids the three-way quadratic and does not price Invalid . Exact SecurityPool identity. A pair is keyed by the pool address, so parent and child branch pools always map to different pairs even when they share one ShareToken . The factory's canonical validation stops a pool from borrowing another market's identity. One immutable fee per factory. There is no owner, protocol fee switch, or mutable parameter. Balance-delta integration. The router measures complete-set creation and redemption by observed share and ETH balance deltas because holding fees change the ETH-to-share rate; it never assumes a fixed ratio. Its custody checks are listed in the router reference. Minimum-reserve LP scale. Initial LP supply follows the smaller reserve rather than a geometric mean, which would overflow at large share scales; only proportional ownership matters. The liquidity-provider position gives the rule. Donations accrue to LPs. Reserve-dependent operations first synchronize reserves up to actual balances, so unsolicited Yes or No shares benefit existing LPs instead of the next trader. The security model covers the related custody defenses. No automatic fork behavior. Parent liquidity is removed as parent shares. The user explicitly chooses migration targets and initializes distinct child pairs.","title":"Trading architecture decisions and limitations","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"limitations","heading":"Limitations and non-goals","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/architecture-decisions.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"The seven design decisions and the features the MVP leaves out.","text":"The MVP intentionally does not implement a three-way invariant, Invalid-share trading, an invalidity-probability oracle, weighted reserves, quadratic solvers, flash swaps, a protocol fee, governance controls, upgradeable proxies, automatic branch selection, automatic LP migration, an insured-position NFT, or per-user on-chain position accounting. It also has no TWAP, routing across markets, guaranteed deep liquidity, or mechanism to withdraw more early ETH than complete-set insurance and reserves permit. Trading is unaudited; the Trading security model states what it defends against and what it assumes. Future work may add separate Invalid markets, safer oracle observations, routing, or bounded convenience flows. Those are distinct designs and must not weaken the invariant that this pair never accepts or holds Invalid shares.","title":"Trading architecture decisions and limitations","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","factory","pair","tradingStatus","CREATE2"],"path":"reference/trading/contracts.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, math library, and predeployment sink constructors, entrypoints, and revert conditions.","text":"Reference All Trading contracts compile with Solidity 0.8.35 for Osaka. Revert strings are operational diagnostics, not stable compatibility commitments.","title":"Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"factory","heading":"TwoWayConstantProductFactory","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/contracts.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, math library, and predeployment sink constructors, entrypoints, and revert conditions.","text":"constructor(ISecurityPoolFactory, uint256 feeBps) fixes the core factory and fee; feeBps < 10,000 . createPair(ISecurityPool) validates the exact canonical pool, deploys with CREATE2, and returns the existing canonical pair on duplicate calls. getPair(ISecurityPool) → pair maps exact pool identity to a pair. isPair(address) → bool recognizes router-eligible pairs. predictPair(ISecurityPool) → address calculates the CREATE2 address. securityPoolFactory() , feeBps() , and predeploymentShareSink() expose immutable configuration. Canonical validation requires the pool's own securityPoolFactory , the factory's (originId, universeId) lookup, and shareToken.canonicalPoolByUniverse(universeId) all to agree. CREATE2 pair addresses exist counterfactually before code is deployed, when ERC-1155 receiver checks cannot run. Each factory therefore deploys an ownerless PredeploymentShareSink . A pair constructor transfers any canonical Invalid, Yes, or No already present at its future address into that sink before returning. Those quarantined shares are permanently inaccessible; do not send shares to a predicted pair address.","title":"Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"pair","heading":"TwoWayConstantProductPair","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/contracts.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, math library, and predeployment sink constructors, entrypoints, and revert conditions.","text":"Immutable accessors expose factory , securityPool , shareToken , universeId , questionId , invalidTokenId , yesTokenId , noTokenId , and feeBps . Constants are MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY (1,000) and MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY_LOCK ( address(1) ), which holds the locked minimum liquidity. LP ERC-20 accessors are name , symbol , decimals , totalSupply , balanceOf , allowance , approve , transfer , and transferFrom . ERC-2612 accessors are permit(owner, spender, value, deadline, v, r, s) , nonces(owner) , and DOMAIN_SEPARATOR() . constructor(factory, securityPool, feeBps, predeploymentShareSink) is called only by the factory. It requires nonzero addresses and feeBps < 10,000 , reads the ShareToken, universe, question, and token IDs from the SecurityPool, and quarantines any canonical shares already at its address, emitting PredeploymentSharesQuarantined . getReserves() returns the recorded Yes and No reserves as of the last pair mutation or sync() . getEffectiveReserves() returns the pair's current Yes and No balances, including unsynchronized donations, and reverts if either balance is below its recorded reserve or the pair holds Invalid shares. The router's enterPosition reads it for the before-trade Conditional Yes price. tradingStatus() returns Open , QuestionEnded , PoolInactive , AwaitingForkContinuation , UniverseForked , QuestionResolved , or Uninitialized . initialize(yesAmount, noAmount, minLiquidity, recipient) pulls the first reserves, locks MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY , and mints the remainder once. It requires an uninitialized pair ( Already initialized ), a nonzero recipient, zero Invalid, Yes, and No balances at the pair ( Nonzero initial balance ), min(yesAmount, noAmount) > MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY ( Initial liquidity too small ), and minted liquidity of at least minLiquidity ( Minimum liquidity ). addLiquidity(maxYes, maxNo, minLiquidity, recipient) synchronizes donations, selects the largest proportional amounts, pulls them, and mints LP. It reverts with Liquidity rounds to zero when either used amount is zero and with Minimum liquidity when the minted LP is zero or below minLiquidity . removeLiquidity(liquidity, minYes, minNo, recipient, deadline) burns caller LP and returns proportional raw shares when called at or before deadline . It has no lifecycle-close guard. It reverts with Liquidity slippage below minYes or minNo and with Liquidity output is zero when either output rounds to zero, so a dust LP amount cannot be removed on its own. swapExactInput(yesForNo, amountIn, minAmountOut, recipient) pulls the gross input and sends the floor-rounded output; it reverts with Swap slippage below minAmountOut . swapExactOutput(yesForNo, amountOut, maxAmountIn, recipient) pulls the ceil-rounded required input and sends the exact output; it reverts with Swap slippage above maxAmountIn . quoteExactInput(yesForNo, amountIn) → (amountOut, feeAmount) and quoteExactOutput(yesForNo, amountOut) → (amountIn, feeAmount) quote against effective reserves. sync() records current balances as reserves. It reverts when the pair is uninitialized, a balance fell below its reserve, or the pair holds Invalid shares. onERC1155Received , onERC1155BatchReceived , and supportsInterface implement receiver discovery. Only the canonical ShareToken 's exact Yes/No IDs are accepted. Pre-initialization transfers must be initiated by the pair's own pull. initialize , addLiquidity , and both swaps require an open lifecycle and otherwise revert with Question ended , Universe forked , Fork continuation pending , Pool inactive , or Question resolved ; all but initialize also revert with Uninitialized before the first deposit. tradingStatus() is a concise action blocker, not an authoritative settlement summary. The first matching condition wins in this order: Uninitialized , UniverseForked , AwaitingForkContinuation , PoolInactive , QuestionResolved , QuestionEnded , then Open . An uninitialized forked pair therefore reports Uninitialized , and a finalized question reports QuestionResolved even after its end time. Read SecurityPoolForker.getQuestionOutcome(pool) when the finalized outcome itself is required.","title":"Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"sink","heading":"PredeploymentShareSink","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/contracts.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, math library, and predeployment sink constructors, entrypoints, and revert conditions.","text":"Accepts ERC-1155 transfers and has no withdrawal or administrative method. It exists only to preserve clean pair initialization and the zero-Invalid invariant after counterfactual-address poisoning attempts.","title":"Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"math","heading":"TwoWayConstantProductMath","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/contracts.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, math library, and predeployment sink constructors, entrypoints, and revert conditions.","text":"The internal library provides exact-input and exact-output quotes, ceil rounding through the repository's full-precision Math.mulDiv , proportional deposits, initial odds, and conditional price basis points. Production paths never multiply reserve products. Rounding directions are listed in Units and rounding .","title":"Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","router","enterPosition","receive request","permit"],"path":"reference/trading/router.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Router entrypoints, custody guarantees, and approval-free share operations.","text":"Reference Trading deploys one canonical factory and one canonical router. The router handles entry and liquidity operations, share-transfer exits and complete-set redemption, and permit-assisted LP removal.","title":"Trading router","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"entrypoints","heading":"Entrypoints","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/router.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Router entrypoints, custody guarantees, and approval-free share operations.","text":"TwoWayConstantProductRouter(factory) fixes the only eligible factory. All user mutations enforce a deadline and a reentrancy guard. The router's receiver callbacks accept only the active pool's canonical share token and universe IDs. receive() accepts ETH only during redemption and only from the expected SecurityPool or its canonical portfolio settlement engine. enterPosition(pair, longOutcome, minLongSharesOut, recipient, deadline) is payable. It creates actual complete-set shares from msg.value , verifies equal Invalid/Yes/No deltas, swaps all opposite shares, and delivers long shares plus Invalid. It reverts with Minimum long shares below minLongSharesOut . It returns EnterResult : ethSpent , completeSetShares , oppositeSharesSwapped , additionalLongShares , totalLongShares , invalidInsurance , feeAmount , conditionalYesBpsBefore , and conditionalYesBpsAfter . createPairAndInitializeWithEth(pool, conditionalYesBpsValue, minLiquidity, recipient, deadline) creates or reuses the deterministic pair and requires it to be uninitialized ( Pair already initialized ) before atomically seeding it at a Conditional Yes price strictly between 0 and 10,000 basis points. initializeWithEth(pair, conditionalYesBpsValue, minLiquidity, recipient, deadline) initializes an already created canonical pair. addLiquidityWithEth(pair, maxYesUsed, maxNoUsed, minLiquidity, recipient, deadline) creates complete sets, deposits the authoritative proportional maximum, and returns Invalid and unused shares. It reverts with Liquidity price slippage when yesUsed > maxYesUsed or noUsed > maxNoUsed . minLiquidity alone does not bound the price: a swap toward even odds before the call raises both the deposit of the side with the smaller reserve and the minted LP. removeLiquidityWithPermit(pair, liquidity, minYesOut, minNoOut, recipient, deadline, v, r, s) calls the pair's permit for the caller as owner, the router as spender, exactly liquidity as value, and the same deadline ; the signature must cover those values. A failing permit is ignored when the router's allowance already covers liquidity ; otherwise the call reverts with LP permit or allowance . The router then pulls the LP and returns raw Yes/No directly to the recipient. factory() exposes the immutable factory. onERC1155Received , onERC1155BatchReceived , and supportsInterface exist only for the receive-based operations described below and for the pair callbacks inside an operation. The payable entrypoints require nonzero msg.value ( ETH input is zero ). Every entrypoint requires a recipient that is neither zero nor the router ( Invalid recipient ) and block.timestamp ≤ deadline ( Deadline expired ); those that take a pair require it to be the factory's canonical pair for its SecurityPool ( Unrecognized pair , Noncanonical pair ). The three liquidity entrypoints that take ETH return LiquidityResult : pair , completeSetShares , yesUsed , noUsed , yesReturned , noReturned , invalidInsurance , and liquidity . Every successful share-custody operation, including the receive-based operations below, asserts that the router's Invalid, Yes, and No balances equal their starting values, preserving pre-existing forced shares.","title":"Trading router","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"approval-free","heading":"Exits and complete-set redemption by share transfer","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/router.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Router entrypoints, custody guarantees, and approval-free share operations.","text":"The router has no exitPosition or redeemCompleteSet entrypoint. Users exit and redeem by calling the ShareToken 's safeBatchTransferFrom with themselves as both caller and owner, the router as recipient, and an encoded request in the data field. No setApprovalForAll is required. The request is the ABI-encoded ReceiveRequest struct with fields in this order: version (must equal CALLBACK_VERSION , currently 1), operation (0 ExitPosition , 1 RedeemCompleteSet ), shareToken , securityPool , pair , universeId , questionId , invalidTokenId , yesTokenId , noTokenId , longOutcome , completeSetShares , maxLongSharesIn , minEthOut , payoutRecipient , refundRecipient , and deadline . encodeReceiveRequest produces it. The router accepts only owner-initiated top-level transfers ( operator == from ); internal pair callbacks are distinguished from top-level user callbacks. Insured exit . The transfer carries exactly two IDs in the order [invalidTokenId, long token ID] with the values [q, maxLongSharesIn] , where q is completeSetShares , and longOutcome is Yes (1) or No (2). The router buys exactly q opposite shares with an exact-output swap, redeems q complete sets at the pool, and returns any unused long shares to the bound refund recipient. It emits PositionExitedByTransfer . Complete-set redemption. The transfer carries exactly three IDs in the order [invalidTokenId, yesTokenId, noTokenId] , each with the value completeSetShares ; longOutcome must be None (3) and maxLongSharesIn zero. The router redeems them at the exact canonical SecurityPool and emits CompleteSetRedeemedByTransfer . Both operations require completeSetShares > 0 , a payoutRecipient and a refundRecipient that are neither zero nor the router, and canonical pool, share-token, universe, question and outcome IDs. An insured exit requires the factory's canonical pair. Complete-set redemption permits a zero pair ; a supplied nonzero pair must still be canonical. A child with no trading pair can therefore redeem directly through this transfer route. Both operations measure the ETH and native REP produced by redemption and forward exactly those deltas to the payout recipient. ETH must meet minEthOut , and at least one asset must be positive. A REP-only basket therefore accepts a zero ETH minimum. Pre-existing ETH and REP in the router are excluded from the payout; minEthOut protects only the ETH component. For LP removal through the router, Safes and integrations without signature support may use ordinary exact allowances instead of a permit. The first-party UI calls the pair directly and needs no router allowance.","title":"Trading router","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","events","Swap","indexer"],"path":"reference/trading/events.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, and router events and indexer attribution rules.","text":"Reference The factory and pair emit every reserve and LP state change. The router emits an event only for the two share-transfer operations it executes itself. Parameters prefixed with indexed are log topics.","title":"Trading events","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"factory","heading":"Factory","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/events.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, and router events and indexer attribution rules.","text":"PairCreated(indexed securityPool, indexed shareToken, indexed universeId, pair, feeBps) identifies one exact branch market and its immutable fee.","title":"Trading events","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"pair","heading":"Pair","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/events.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, and router events and indexer attribution rules.","text":"LiquidityInitialized(indexed provider, indexed recipient, yesAmount, noAmount, liquidity) records the first reserve contribution and circulating LP minted; locked minimum liquidity is separate. LiquidityAdded(indexed provider, indexed recipient, yesAmount, noAmount, liquidity) records proportional reserve use. LiquidityRemoved(indexed provider, indexed recipient, yesAmount, noAmount, liquidity) records raw-share output. Swap(indexed sender, indexed recipient, yesForNo, exactOutput, amountIn, amountOut, feeAmount, resultingYesReserve, resultingNoReserve) provides authoritative direction, mode, amounts, fee, and resulting state. Sync(yesReserve, noReserve) records reserves after synchronization or mutation. PredeploymentSharesQuarantined(invalidAmount, yesAmount, noAmount) records canonical shares removed from a counterfactual pair address during construction. The destination is the factory's immutable predeploymentShareSink . Standard ERC-20 Transfer and Approval events describe LP ownership and allowances.","title":"Trading events","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"router","heading":"Router","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/events.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, and router events and indexer attribution rules.","text":"PositionExitedByTransfer(indexed owner, indexed pair, completeSetShares, longSharesUsed, longSharesRefunded, payoutRecipient) records an insured exit initiated by owner 's share transfer. CompleteSetRedeemedByTransfer(indexed securityPool, completeSetShares, payoutRecipient) records a complete-set redemption initiated by share transfer.","title":"Trading events","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"attribution","heading":"Attributing router operations","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/events.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, and router events and indexer attribution rules.","text":"For router-mediated operations, pair provider / sender fields and SecurityPool creator / redeemer fields identify the router, not the initiating wallet. Pair recipient identifies the explicit asset recipient only for direct pair calls and router liquidity operations; router entry and insured-exit swaps name the router, which then forwards the assets. Insured exits carry the owner in PositionExitedByTransfer . For complete-set redemptions, take the owner from the ShareToken TransferBatch log's from field, since CompleteSetRedeemedByTransfer records only the pool and payout recipient. Entry and liquidity operations emit no router event, so for those an indexer must combine the transaction sender, the called router method, pair and core logs, and the recipient from calldata or the ShareToken TransferBatch log; it must not infer wallet attribution from the immediate-caller fields alone. Router return tuples are available from authoritative call simulation only; they are not stored in a transaction receipt. Complete-set and ETH-flow evidence comes from the SecurityPool's CompleteSetCreated and CompleteSetRedeemed events plus transaction logs.","title":"Trading events","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","SDK","quoteExactOutput","maximumInsuredExit","encodeReceiveRequest"],"path":"reference/trading/sdk.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Shared bigint math, exit search, receive-request encoding, and simulation-pinning helpers.","text":"Reference The private workspace package @zoltar/trading-shared holds the pure Trading math that the UI and the Solidity parity tests share. All chain quantities are bigint .","title":"Trading SDK","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"pure-math","heading":"Pure math","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/sdk.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Shared bigint math, exit search, receive-request encoding, and simulation-pinning helpers.","text":"Module @zoltar/trading-shared/trading/math mirrors the quote and price functions of TwoWayConstantProductMath . It has no counterpart of the library's initial-odds and proportional-deposit functions; the UI estimates those in ui/trading/ts/features/live/liquidityEstimate.ts . quoteExactInput(reserveIn, reserveOut, amountIn, feeBps) and quoteExactOutput(reserveIn, reserveOut, amountOut, feeBps) return a SwapQuote , { amountIn, amountOut, netInput, feeAmount } , with the contract's floor (exact input) and ceil (exact output) rounding. They throw on empty reserves, an out-of-range output, or feeBps ≥ 10,000 . conditionalYesBps(yesReserve, noReserve) returns the Conditional Yes price in basis points, floor-rounded.","title":"Trading SDK","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"positions","heading":"Position quotes","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/sdk.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Shared bigint math, exit search, receive-request encoding, and simulation-pinning helpers.","text":"Module @zoltar/trading-shared/trading/positions takes { yesReserve, noReserve, feeBps } and a longOutcome of 'YES' or 'NO' : quoteEnterPosition(longOutcome, completeSetShares, reserves) mirrors enterPosition after minting and returns an EnterPositionQuote : longOutcome , completeSetShares , oppositeSharesSwapped , additionalLongShares , totalLongShares , invalidInsurance , feeAmount , conditionalYesBpsBefore , and conditionalYesBpsAfter . quoteExitPosition(longOutcome, completeSetShares, reserves) mirrors the insured exit and returns an ExitPositionQuote : longOutcome , completeSetShares , longSharesSwapped , totalLongShares , invalidRequired , feeAmount , conditionalYesBpsBefore , and conditionalYesBpsAfter . largestExitForLongShares({ ...reserves, longOutcome, longShares, completeSetCap? }) binary-searches the largest complete-set amount whose total long-share cost fits within longShares and stays below the opposite reserve. The optional completeSetCap bounds the result further. maximumInsuredExit({ ...reserves, longOutcome, longBalance, invalidBalance }) calls largestExitForLongShares with the long balance as longShares and the Invalid balance as completeSetCap .","title":"Trading SDK","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"receive-request","heading":"Receive request encoding","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/sdk.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Shared bigint math, exit search, receive-request encoding, and simulation-pinning helpers.","text":"Module @zoltar/trading-shared/trading/receiveRequest exports encodeReceiveRequest(request) , which ABI-encodes the data argument for a share transfer to the router. The request, typed ReceiveRequest , is a positional tuple in the field order of the router's ReceiveRequest struct, which the router reference lists.","title":"Trading SDK","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"transaction-helpers","heading":"Transaction helpers","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/sdk.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Shared bigint math, exit search, receive-request encoding, and simulation-pinning helpers.","text":"Transaction-facing helpers live in the Trading UI at ui/trading/ts/protocol/tradeQuote.ts , not in the shared package: minimumAfterSlippage(amount, slippageBps?) floors and maximumAfterSlippage(amount, slippageBps?) ceils a bound; slippage defaults to UI_SLIPPAGE_BPS (50). requireTransactionSlippageBps throws outside MINIMUM_SLIPPAGE_BPS (1) to MAXIMUM_SLIPPAGE_BPS (500). stableSimulation(client, simulate) reads the latest block with latestBlockIdentity(client) and passes its blockNumber , blockHash , and blockTimestamp to simulate , whose reads must all use that block number. A reorg during those reads is not detected. simulateWithDeadline(client, expiry, simulate, resolveDeadline?) does the same and also resolves expiry , a TransactionExpiry that is either an absolute deadline or { validityMinutes } between 1 and 1,440 ( requireTransactionValidityMinutes ), against the pinned block's timestamp. The optional resolveDeadline(block, requestedDeadline) can replace that deadline, and simulate receives (block, deadline) . retainApprovedMinimum(approved, refreshed, label) and retainApprovedMaximum(approved, refreshed, label) return the approved bound, or throw when the submission-time simulation no longer satisfies it. Pure math is a responsive preview only. A quote does not expire when a new block arrives: immediately before wallet submission the client simulates the actual call again at the latest block, and submits with the bounds and deadline approved in the quote only if that simulation succeeds within them.","title":"Trading SDK","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","configuration","manifest","environment"],"path":"reference/trading/configuration.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment script environment variables, the local manifest, and what the client derives and stores.","text":"Reference The local deployment script solidity/ts/trading/deploy/deploy-local.mts reads the environment variables below. No public trading deployment manifest is bundled; the client derives and checks its contracts at startup.","title":"Trading deployment configuration","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"environment","heading":"Environment variables","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/configuration.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment script environment variables, the local manifest, and what the client derives and stores.","text":"Name Meaning Default TRADING_RPC_URL JSON-RPC endpoint http://127.0.0.1:8545 TRADING_DEPLOYER Unlocked RPC account First eth_accounts entry ZOLTAR_DEPLOYMENT_MANIFEST Existing matching core manifest Required TRADING_FEE_BPS Immutable trading fee 30 TRADING_FEE_BPS must be a whole number from 0 to 9999. The script stops when the RPC chain ID differs from the core manifest's, when the manifest's SecurityPoolFactory has no code on that chain, or when the RPC exposes no account. Manifests must not contain secrets.","title":"Trading deployment configuration","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"manifest","heading":"Deployment manifest","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/configuration.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment script environment variables, the local manifest, and what the client derives and stores.","text":"bun run trading:deploy:local writes solidity/deployments/trading/local.json for scripts and integration environments. The file is git-ignored because local addresses are ephemeral. A generated manifest contains: network : decimal and hexadecimal chain IDs plus the deployment RPC; core : the SecurityPoolFactory address, checked to have code on the RPC chain, and the source manifest path; trading : factory, router, and immutable feeBps ; transactions : factory and router objects shaped as { hash, blockNumber: \"<base-10 integer string>\" } . blockNumber is a JSON string, not a JavaScript number, so large receipt block numbers remain exact; compilerProfiles : exact versions and settings from the main Solidity artifact; bytecodeHashes : creation-bytecode commitments; deployer and deployedAt . Pairs never appear as static deployment inputs. Discover them dynamically with getPair(pool) , verify with isPair , or derive the deterministic address with predictPair . Review a manifest before publishing it, and never copy local ephemeral addresses into a public-network file.","title":"Trading deployment configuration","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"client","heading":"What the client derives","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/configuration.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment script environment variables, the local manifest, and what the client derives and stores.","text":"The standalone client reads no environment variables. At startup it uses: Networks: the build writes core-deployments.json from the Yes and No deployment manifests for mainnet and Sepolia in docs/ , with each network's default public RPC URL. Mainnet is listed but disabled in the UI build, and the default network is Sepolia. RPC override: Settings beside the wallet selects another supported network or accepts an optional HTTPS or loopback HTTP RPC URL. URL parameters: genesis ( yes or no ) selects the genesis deployment; an absent or invalid choice opens the fork-question picker before startup. network ( sepolia ) pins the network instead of following the wallet, rpcUrl overrides the saved RPC URL, universe selects a universe within the selected genesis deployment, and simulate=1 with an optional simScenario selects the simulator . Trading contracts: the factory and router addresses are computed with the fixed 30 basis-point fee through the core deployment's canonical CREATE2 proxy, and each address is checked for code. If either contract is missing, the connected wallet can deploy it. Markets: the market lists page through the SecurityPools that SecurityPoolFactory registers in the selected universe and that have a trading pool, 25 at a time, with favorites saved in the browser first; the SecurityPool list shows only favorites. Opening an address reads that SecurityPool and checks it against the canonical SecurityPoolFactory , Zoltar, and ShareToken; no market address is hard-coded. Mint capacity follows capital health . Trade settings: slippage tolerance (1 to 500 basis points, default 50) and transaction validity (1 to 1,440 minutes, default 20) are kept in browser local storage under zoltar.trading.tradeSettings . Price impact: the trade ticket cautions above 2%, requires an explicit confirmation above 5%, and refuses the trade above 15%. Quote and simulation behavior is described in the Trading SDK .","title":"Trading deployment configuration","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","invariants","custody","k"],"path":"reference/trading/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Custody, swap, and lifecycle conditions at every state transition.","text":"Reference These conditions are required at every successful external state transition. Reverting calls leave the prior state unchanged. Protocol-wide invariants are catalogued separately in Protocol invariants .","title":"Trading invariants","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"pair-custody","heading":"Pair custody","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Custody, swap, and lifecycle conditions at every state transition.","text":"The pair's Yes balance is greater than or equal to its stored Yes reserve. The pair's No balance is greater than or equal to its stored No reserve. The pair's Invalid balance is exactly zero. An LP withdrawal cannot claim more Yes or No shares than the pair owns. Withdrawal amounts are proportional to synchronized reserves and the liquidity burned. Unsolicited Yes or No donations can make a balance greater than its stored reserve. Before reserve-dependent mutations, the pair synchronizes stored reserves upward to those effective balances. Invalid shares are never a trading asset and cannot be donated through an accepted ERC-1155 callback.","title":"Trading invariants","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"router-custody","heading":"Router custody","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Custody, swap, and lifecycle conditions at every state transition.","text":"Every successful router operation ends with the same Invalid, Yes, and No balances with which that operation began. This deliberately preserves any pre-existing balances while preventing the operation from accumulating new share residue.","title":"Trading invariants","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"swaps","heading":"Swaps","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Custody, swap, and lifecycle conditions at every state transition.","text":"For every successful exact-input or exact-output swap, k_after ≥ k_before , where k is the synchronized effective Yes reserve multiplied by the synchronized effective No reserve. An exact-output swap delivers exactly the requested output amount. An exact-output swap never charges more than the caller's maxAmountIn . The product comparison starts from effective reserves so a donation cannot be mistaken for swap-created growth.","title":"Trading invariants","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"lifecycle","heading":"Lifecycle","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Custody, swap, and lifecycle conditions at every state transition.","text":"Once the question has ended, the question has resolved, or the universe has forked, initialization, liquidity addition, and both swap forms revert permanently. While fork continuation is pending or the SecurityPool is not operational, the same operations also revert for as long as that state lasts. Proportional liquidity removal has no lifecycle guard, so LP tokens never become trapped.","title":"Trading invariants","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","attoShares","rounding","token ID"],"path":"reference/trading/units-and-rounding.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Atomic units, token ID layout, and rounding directions.","text":"Reference Every Trading quantity is an integer in an atomic unit, and swap rounding always favors existing liquidity providers.","title":"Trading units and rounding","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"units","heading":"Units","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/units-and-rounding.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Atomic units, token ID layout, and rounding directions.","text":"ETH and collateral outputs use attoETH (10 −18 ETH). Shares and LP tokens use attoShares, the 18-decimal atomic uint256 unit: one displayed share is 10 18 attoShares. At an empty SecurityPool, one attoETH creates one attoShare of every outcome: 1 ETH creates one Yes, one No, and one Invalid share. Protocol holding fees makes the later collateral-to-share ratio dynamic, so clients must use the current mutation result rather than assuming this initial rate. Fees, slippage, initial conditional price, and displayed conditional price use basis points over 10,000 where applicable. Deadlines and question end times are Unix seconds. Universe IDs are uint248 . A token ID is (universeId << 8) | outcome , with local outcomes Invalid = 0, Yes = 1, No = 2.","title":"Trading units and rounding","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"rounding","heading":"Rounding","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/units-and-rounding.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Atomic units, token ID layout, and rounding directions.","text":"Exact input computes net = ⌊gross × (10,000 − fee) / 10,000⌋ and out = ⌊reserveOut × net / (reserveIn + net)⌋ . Exact output computes net = ⌈reserveIn × out / (reserveOut − out)⌉ and gross = ⌈net × 10,000 / (10,000 − fee)⌉ . These directions favor existing LPs. Liquidity deposits round reserve use down, and the LP minted is the smaller of ⌊yesUsed × supply / yesReserve⌋ and ⌊noUsed × supply / noReserve⌋ . Removal rounds each reserve output down. Initial alternative odds round the smaller deposited reserve down. Conditional Yes is NO / (YES + NO) and Conditional No is YES / (YES + NO) ; in basis points, Conditional Yes is ⌊10,000 × NO / (YES + NO)⌋ . Client slippage bounds round a minimum down and a maximum up.","title":"Trading units and rounding","topic":"Trading","weight":0}]
